	<xs:element name="AccessAssessment" type="uci:AccessAssessmentMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the results of an access assessment.  An access assessment is a temporal analysis of possibilities for employment of capabilities of Objects against/to Subjects.  The results can be thought of as being organized in a table with Subjects as columns and Objects as rows.  For a given Subject-Object cell/pair in the table there are potentially many temporal and other assessment parameters given per Object level of effort per Object capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AccessAssessmentRequest" type="uci:AccessAssessmentRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to generate an access assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AccessAssessmentRequestStatus" type="uci:AccessAssessmentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to the request for an access assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Action" type="uci:ActionMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a C2 Requirement in the form/abstraction of an Action.  Other C2 Requirement forms include Effect, Task, [Capability]Command and  Response.  For example, a DEFEAT Effect could be planned/decomposed into a FIND Action, SAR Task, SAR_Command, Attack Action and Strike Task.  See the child EffectType element and its enumerates for the Effect taxonomy.  Actions can be planned (*Plan messages), directly commanded (ActionCommand* messages) and/or event-driven responses (Response* messages).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionActivity" type="uci:ActionActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Action Capability of a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionCancelCommand" type="uci:ActionCancelCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to cancel an Action which has been allocated and planned.  A planning input such as Effect, Action, Task and DMPI can't easily be canceled or removed from the System due to race conditions between their various users and publishers, inevitability of the associated activity, etc.  Additionally, the operator or service initiating the cancellation might be different than the one that manages RequirementSets and/or issues *PlanCommand, introducing additional race conditions.  In order to avoid race conditions, receive positive feedback on the success of the cancellation and allow canceled messages/objects to remain in the system for historical purposes, an explicit ActionCancelCommand is necessary.  An ActionCancelCommand shall result in the Action being canceled unless execution was already inevitable.  If the Action was inevitable and started executing but can be stopped (such as a long collection Action) it shall cancel as soon as possible.  ActionCancelCommand shall result in managers of RequirementSets to remove the associated Action, thereby removing the Action from the next planning cycle.  Services other than those that manage RequirementSets and execute activities aren't required to handle ActionCancelCommand.  ActionCancelCommand doesn't necessarily trigger a replan (there isn't a corresponding planning trigger), therefore a System could continue to navigate routes related to the canceled Action, even though the Action won't occur.  A subsequent replan could exclude the Action.  Status or feedback for this message is given through ActionCancelCommandStatus but the resulting status of the Action is given through ActionStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionCancelCommandStatus" type="uci:ActionCancelCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to an ActionCancelCommand and indicates if the Action was canceled and if not why it was not canceled.  The resulting status of the Action is reported in ActionStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionCapability" type="uci:ActionCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Action category of UCI Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionCapabilityStatus" type="uci:ActionCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Action capabilities available to the system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionCommand" type="uci:ActionCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Action form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionCommandStatus" type="uci:ActionCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an ActionCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionExecutionApprovalStatus" type="uci:ActionExecutionApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the state of approval for execution of a task. ApprovalPolicy allow an authority to define policies to approve or reject a Action as it enters execution. Approval may or may not be required. If approval is required, there can be multiple approvers. See Approval* messages for additional details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlan" type="uci:ActionPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Actions allocated to a System.  Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination.  This message decouples Action allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Action allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on Action allocation.
An ActionPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example the ActionPlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to an Action allocation service that the produces an ActionPlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Actions.
Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the Action allocation process and captured in the ActionPlan to supplement the information in the allocated Actions themselves.
An ActionPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  An ActionPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple ActionPlans and *Plans in general.  An ActionPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an ActionPlan are given in other ActionPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:ActionPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for an ActionPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanCommand" type="uci:ActionPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes an ActionPlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  This message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, an ActionPlan could be constrained by a previous RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:ActionPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an ActionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ActionPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of an ActionPlan  may occur autonomously, without an ActionPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ActionPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:ActionPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more ActionPlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via ActionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanningStatus" type="uci:ActionPlanningStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines planning status of a single Action.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanStatus" type="uci:ActionPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for an ActionPlan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanValidation" type="uci:ActionPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the ActionPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:ActionPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing ActionPlan.  An ActionPlan can become invalid due to changes in Action timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing ActionPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:ActionPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate an ActionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActionStatus" type="uci:ActionStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for an Action.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityMetrics" type="uci:ActivityMetricsMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides metrics on predicted performance details of an ActivityPlan. Metrics include time-related metrics of the ActivityPlan. This message is intended for use prior to execution of a plan; for actual results of the plan, systems should use those status and activity messages produced during execution to derive metrics.
The intent of the *Plan Metrics messages are to provide calculated metrics that are based on existing plan input data. This is to continue the current paradigm of how metrics were represented in existing/previous MissionPlan and other sub plans. There is a distinction between the *Plan Metrics and the Assessment message, in that Assessment gives much more specific data and can consider other supporting systems and their capabilities in the assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityMetricsRequest" type="uci:ActivityMetricsRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the generation of metrics for a specific ActivityPlan. For details on what metrics are provided, see ActivityMetrics.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityMetricsRequestStatus" type="uci:ActivityMetricsRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of an ActivityMetricsRequest received by the applicable C2 system. In the event that metric reporting was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the process was unsuccessful.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlan" type="uci:ActivityPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies planned *Capability-related activities for a System, independent of System kinematics.  Activities are the planned implementation details of Effects, Actions, Tasks, Capability Commands, etc.  Activity planning can be done manually by operators, by an activity planning service or a combination.  This message decouples activity planning from allocation, kinematic and other types of planning; the ActivityPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on activity planning.
An ActivityPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example an ActivityPlan from a previous round of planning could be an input to an activity planning service that adds additional planned activities to it.
A single System can have multiple independent ActivityPlans for different Task, Capability, sensor, etc. types; that are developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture; at different times then independently updated and approved.  Optionally, all types of activities for a System can be included in a single ActivityPlan.
An ActivityPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  An ActivityPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple ActivityPlans and *Plans in general.  An ActivityPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an ActivityPlan are given in other ActivityPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:ActivityPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in an approval state machine for an ActivityPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  This message provides current approval status regardless of how it was given.  This message is not to be used to indicate approval for individual activities within the ActivityPlan, only the whole plan.
ActivityPlan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are ActivityPlanStatus, ActivityPlanApprovalStatus, ActivityPlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanCommand" type="uci:ActivityPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands an ActivityPlan related planning function/action.  Example planning functions include updating an existing ActivityPlan, creating a new ActivityPlan or combining/stitching existing ActivityPlans together.  The command includes many inputs to the planning function while others may come from a variety of other messages.  The expected output of the planning function is an ActivityPlan message.
The command includes options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing plans of the same type or by other plan types.  For example, an ActivityPlan could be constrained by a previous ActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
The command includes environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be for added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of speculative or "what-if"  contingency planning.
The command includes settings for "weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:ActivityPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an ActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ActivityPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of an ActivityPlan may occur autonomously, without an ActivityPlanCommand, in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ActivityPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:ActivityPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in an execution state machine for one or more current and/or previous ActivityPlans both as a whole and for individual Activities.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via the MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanStatus" type="uci:ActivityPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in a planning and plan validation state machine for an ActivityPlan.  Planning and validation may occur autonomously or be commanded.  In the former case, there is no command status message indicating the resulting state.  This message provides current status regardless of how status changes were triggered.  This message is not to be used to status individual activities within the ActivityPlan, only the whole plan.
ActivityPlan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are ActivityPlanStatus, ActivityPlanApprovalStatus, ActivityPlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanValidation" type="uci:ActivityPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the ActivityPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:ActivityPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands validation or re-validation of an existing ActivityPlan.  This message enables plan validation to happen as a separate step, distinct from plan creation.  For example, a plan could be created manually by an operator then machine validated against performance models and other conditions.  Additionally, this message enables an existing valid plan to be revalidated against updated battlespace conditions.  Finally this message optionally allows the existing plan to be updated when invalid items/portions are found.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ActivityPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:ActivityPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate an ActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirfieldReport" type="uci:AirfieldReportMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of an airfield.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleActivity" type="uci:AirSampleActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the AirSample Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (AirSampleCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work AirSample, 4) AirSample scheduled for the future and 5) AirSample that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single AirSampleCommand or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the AirSampleActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleCapability" type="uci:AirSampleCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the AirSample category of UCI Capability.  AirSample includes direct sampling of the air and remote sensing with spectral analysis with the intent of detecting chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear or explosive (CBRNE) events.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready AirSample Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleCapabilityStatus" type="uci:AirSampleCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Air Sampling capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, vehicle flight profiles, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleCommand" type="uci:AirSampleCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the AirSample form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke an AirSample Capability to perform a new AirSample Activity or change an existing interactive AirSample Activity.  It differs from an AirSample Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleCommandStatus" type="uci:AirSampleCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an AirSampleCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the AirSampleActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleSettingsCommand" type="uci:AirSampleSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the AirSample Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AirSampleSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:AirSampleSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of an Air Sample settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_Activity" type="uci:AMTI_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the AMTI Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (AMTI_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work AMTI, 4) AMTI scheduled for the future and 5) AMTI that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single AMTI_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the AMTI_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_Capability" type="uci:AMTI_CapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the AMTI (Airborne Moving Target Indicator) category of UCI Capability.  AMTI detects aircraft through active radar search and track.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready AMTI Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:AMTI_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the AMTI capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.  Since AMTI capabilities refer specifically to an active capability, an EMCON restriction can result in the AMTI capabilities being temporarily unavailable.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_Command" type="uci:AMTI_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the AMTI (Airborne Moving Target Indicator) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  AMTI is a radar Capability that includes active transmission of RF signals.  It is used to manually invoke an AMTI Capability to perform a new AMTI Activity or to make a change to an existing interactive AMTI Activity.  It differs from an AMTI Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability and is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_CommandStatus" type="uci:AMTI_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an AMTI_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the AMTI_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_SettingsCommand" type="uci:AMTI_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the AMTI Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AMTI_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:AMTI_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of an AMTI settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AnalysisRoute" type="uci:AnalysisRouteMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a route to be used for analysis and optionally includes position data as would be flown for each path segment.  An AnalysisRoute could be used as an input to an AssessmentRequest or a RoutePlanValidationCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AnalysisRouteRequest" type="uci:AnalysisRouteRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a propagated route for an existing route plan.  An AnalysisRoute could be used as an input to an AssessmentRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AnalysisRouteRequestStatus" type="uci:AnalysisRouteRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a propagated route for an existing route plan.  An AnalysisRoute could be used as an input to an AssessmentRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_Activity" type="uci:AO_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the AO Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (AO_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work AO, 4) AO scheduled for the future and 5) AO that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single AO_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the AO_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_Capability" type="uci:AO_CapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the AO (Active Optical) category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready AO Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:AO_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the AO capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.  Since AO capabilities refer specifically to an active capability, an EMCON restriction can result in the AO capabilities being temporarily unavailable.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_Command" type="uci:AO_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the AO (Active Optical) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke an Active Optical Capability (such as a laser designation) to perform a new AO Activity or change an existing interactive AO Activity.  It differs from an AO Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_CommandStatus" type="uci:AO_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an AO_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the AO_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_ConsentRequest" type="uci:AO_ConsentRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request consent to perform and Active Optical Activity.  Status for this command message is provided via the AO_ConsentRequestStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_ConsentRequestStatus" type="uci:AO_ConsentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the consent request to perform an Active Optical Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_SettingsCommand" type="uci:AO_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the AO Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AO_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:AO_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of an AO settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalAuthority" type="uci:ApprovalAuthorityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message assigns ApprovalPolicy to a System or Systems and specifies which System or Systems is the approval authority for that policy.  An approval authority is responsible for approving or rejecting activities specified in the ApprovalPolicy for the specified System.  See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalAuthorityRequest" type="uci:ApprovalAuthorityRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests assignment of ApprovalPolicy to a System or Systems and a corresponding approval authority for that policy.  See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalAuthorityRequestStatus" type="uci:ApprovalAuthorityRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an ApprovalAuthorityRequest.  See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalManagementCommand" type="uci:ApprovalManagementCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a command for on demand approval management processing for a given approvable item.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalManagementCommandStatus" type="uci:ApprovalManagementCommandStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>An Approval Management Command status is used by a service to report status/state of an ApprovalManagementCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalPolicy" type="uci:ApprovalPolicyMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies which activities of a system require approval from an approval authority.  The Approval* series of messages are designed to request and establish an approval hierarchy that governs select, possibly automated, activities of the UCI family of services such as route planning and task execution.  They can be used collectively to support a variety of activity approval use cases by separating data content into independent messages aligned with typical use case actors.  As an example, one System could create/manage ApprovalPolicy while another could assign them to Systems and assign approval authorities via the ApprovalAuthority message.  Then, one or more other Systems could make ApprovalRequests based on the ApprovalPolicy.  Finally, the System or Systems assigned by ApprovalAuthority approve or reject the ApprovalRequest using ApprovalRequestStatus.  The messages allow multiple approval authorities per ApprovalPolicy and/or multiple ApprovalPolicy per System.  When multiple ApprovalPolicy apply to a single System, they should be evaluated independently; if any policy requires approval for a certain condition then approval is necessary; if multiple policies required approval for a certain condition then approval should be requested for each.  The messages PlanningFunction*, Approval*, MissionContingencyAlert and ControlStatus are closely related.  They use a common taxonomy of autonomous functions (task allocation, route planning, mission activation and mission execution), triggers for those functions (new threats, new zones, failures, etc.) and results of those functions (dropped Tasks, changes in vulnerability, etc.).  ControlStatus indicates the full set of available autonomous functions/settings of a System.  PlanningFunction* indicates how the available settings are currently configured.  MissionContingencyAlert indicates an autonomous function/setting that has been triggered.  Approval* messages allow Operators to approve the results of autonomous responses as needed.  Updates supersede previous  or instances of the message; each instance of the message shall have a complete specification of the approval policy.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalRequest" type="uci:ApprovalRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request for approval of an activity specified in an associated ApprovalPolicy.  See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ApprovalRequestStatus" type="uci:ApprovalRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an ApprovalRequest.  See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Assessment" type="uci:AssessmentMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides an assessment of other UCI data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AssessmentRequest" type="uci:AssessmentRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to generate different types of assessments.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="AssessmentRequestStatus" type="uci:AssessmentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to the request for an assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Authorization" type="uci:AuthorizationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message establishes authorization for a User to task a System.  Authorization to task is independent of the C2 hierarchy established via the Control* series of messages.  It is also independent of the approval hierarchy established via the Approval* series of messages.  For example, System1 could have mission control of System2 and have responsibility for approving System2's Tasks for execution but not be authorized to Task System2.  Further, authorization to task a System has different meanings based on the type of System and its position in the C2 hierarchy.  For example, authorization for System3 to task System4, where System4 is a vehicle, means that Tasks created by System3 can only be allocated to System4 even if other capable siblings of System4 are available.  In another example, authorization for System5 to task System6, where System6 controls many Systems, each of which controls multiple vehicle Systems, means that Tasks created by System5 can be allocated to any System subordinate to System6.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageArea" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a dynamic estimate of the Capability Coverage Area.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequest" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a dynamic estimate of the Capability Coverage Area.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatus" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests a dynamic estimate of the Capability Coverage Area.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CargoDeliveryCapability" type="uci:CargoDeliveryCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the cargo delivery form of the general UCI Capability concept.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready cargo delivery Capability so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CargoDeliveryCapabilityStatus" type="uci:CargoDeliveryCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Cargo Delivery capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, vehicle flight profiles, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CargoStatus" type="uci:CargoStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the location and physical characteristics of a cargo item.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_Activity" type="uci:COMINT_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the COMINT Capability of a Subsystem of a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_Capability" type="uci:COMINT_CapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the COMINT category of UCI Capability, and includes both COMINT and ELINT functions.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:COMINT_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the COMINT capabilities available to the system as advertised in the COMINT_Capability message.  This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_Command" type="uci:COMINT_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the COMINT form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a COMINT Capability to perform a new COMINT Activity or change an existing interactive Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_CommandStatus" type="uci:COMINT_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_ConsentRequest" type="uci:COMINT_ConsentRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request consent to perform a function.  Status for this command message is provided via the ConsentRequestStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_ConsentRequestStatus" type="uci:COMINT_ConsentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report consent request status for its corresponding ConsentRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_SettingsCommand" type="uci:COMINT_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="COMINT_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:COMINT_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report settings status for its corresponding SettingsCommand message used to change the settings associated with known capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommAsset" type="uci:CommAssetMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a specific communication asset within a particular communication system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommAssetEstimationRequest" type="uci:CommAssetEstimationRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to invoke generation of estimated system state in terms of position and status.  The request includes which messages the estimator service is expected to produce and at what frequency (periodic or single).  The message also optionally allows the user to specify a mission plan segment as the starting point for the estimation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommAssetEstimationRequestStatus" type="uci:CommAssetEstimationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a response to a request to generate an estimated representation of a system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommAssetOrbit" type="uci:CommAssetOrbitMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the report containing the current orbit elements (TLE) that will be used to calculate the position of an orbiting communication asset.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommAssetPositionReport" type="uci:CommAssetPositionReportMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the current position of a communication asset. If the asset is an air breather this will probably be the actual position it is reporting. If the asset is not an air breather then this will probably be the interpretation of the TLE or similar data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommPointing" type="uci:CommPointingMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a pointing vector solution used in pointing communication antenna/beam; it indicates proposed pointing/coverage footprint of a communication system in order to satisfy an upcoming CommRequirement constrained by a previous CommScheduleAllocation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommPointingCommand" type="uci:CommPointingCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a command to an actual communication system to perform the pointing/coverage footprint described in a CommPointing message/object.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommPointingCommandStatus" type="uci:CommPointingCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the status of a command to an actual communication system to perform the pointing/coverage footprint described in a CommPointing message/object.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommPointingRequest" type="uci:CommPointingRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a request to update a proposed pointing/coverage of a previous CommPointing message/object via an HMI.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommPointingRequestStatus" type="uci:CommPointingRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the status of a request to update a pointing vector.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelayActivity" type="uci:CommRelayActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the CommRelay Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (CommRelayCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work CommRelay, 4) CommRelay scheduled for the future and 5) CommRelay that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single CommRelayCommand or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the CommRelayActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelayCapability" type="uci:CommRelayCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Communications Relay category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready CommRelay Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelayCapabilityStatus" type="uci:CommRelayCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the CommRelay capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelayCommand" type="uci:CommRelayCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the CommRelay form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a CommRelay Capability to perform a new CommRelay Activity or change an existing interactive CommRelay Activity.  It differs from a CommRelay Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelayCommandStatus" type="uci:CommRelayCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a CommRelayCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the CommRelayActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelaySettingsCommand" type="uci:CommRelaySettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the settings associated with CommRelay Capabilities of a Subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommRelaySettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:CommRelaySettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of a CommRelaySettingsCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommScheduleAllocation" type="uci:CommScheduleAllocationMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents an internal allocation of communication support for a scheduled external communication resource.  The purpose of this message is two-fold.  First, specifying a different controlling System than the original request indicates a hand-off of control; the indicated System is now responsible for managing the allocated communication resource.  Second, this message allows the allocation of communication support based on a MissionPlan.  This linkage is necessary for a communication manager to know what plans, and subsequently what Systems, it is managing communications for.  An allocation may be released by deleting the allocation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommScheduleAllocationUpdateRequest" type="uci:CommScheduleAllocationUpdateRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a request to update an external communication allocation.  This message provides the operator the ability to modify allocations via an HMI.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommScheduleAllocationUpdateRequestStatus" type="uci:CommScheduleAllocationUpdateRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the status of a request to update an external communication allocation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommScheduleAvailability" type="uci:CommScheduleAvailabilityMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the different external communication resources that are available for scheduling.  This message would typically be generated by an external interface adapter which is converting an external communication asset ICD to UCI.  This message is used to expose the asset's scheduling availability to a UCI compliant communication scheduling system/service.  In other words, available communication that hasn't been scheduled or allocated on the server side.  Intended for publication by the service/function that interfaces with the actual communication system (which is generally not UCI compliant).  Updates supersede previous instance or instances of the message; each instance of the message shall have a complete specification of the communication schedule.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommSchedulingRequest" type="uci:CommSchedulingRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request for a communications resource that is external to the network of nodes which communicate with this schema.  The external communication resource can be released by deleting the request (RequestState = CANCEL).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommSchedulingRequestStatus" type="uci:CommSchedulingRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message returns the status from the processing of a CommSchedulingRequest message. When an approval is returned, the AllocationData list contains external communication resources that can be used by the requester.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommSchedulingRequirement" type="uci:CommSchedulingRequirementMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates required communication for a future system/mission plan; client side.   Might be constrained by CommScheduleAvailability.  Intended for publication by service/functions that analyze the routes, tasks and Capability commands of missions and declare when, where and how much communication is necessary.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommSystem" type="uci:CommSystemMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a specific communication system that may be utilized by a system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalActivity" type="uci:CommTerminalActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A Waveform Activity message describes what a Waveform Capability is doing.   This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Waveform Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalCapability" type="uci:CommTerminalCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A CommTerminalCapability message describes what a communications subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  The capabilities include waveform instantiation/tear-down and waveform, user I/O, crypto, etc. settings provided by the communications subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalCapabilityStatus" type="uci:CommTerminalCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Waveform capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated Waveform profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalCommand" type="uci:CommTerminalCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a derivation of the UCI Capability Command.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and Waveform Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, Waveform Command rank, Waveform Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalCommandStatus" type="uci:CommTerminalCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a CommCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the CommActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalPlan" type="uci:CommTerminalPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the CommTerminalPlan that will be performed by a communications subsystem or hierarchical family of communications subsystems during its mission.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalPlanActivationCommand" type="uci:CommTerminalPlanActivationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command upload and activation of a CommTerminalPlan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalPlanActivationCommandStatus" type="uci:CommTerminalPlanActivationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a CommTerminalPlanActivationCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalPlanActivationStatus" type="uci:CommTerminalPlanActivationStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of a communications plan along the planning, conversion, upload, and activation chain.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalPlanOverrideRequest" type="uci:CommTerminalPlanOverrideRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides an operator the ability to request the activation of pre-mission planned comm terminal settings, in coordination with, operator inputs.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalPlanOverrideRequestStatus" type="uci:CommTerminalPlanOverrideRequestStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a CommTerminalPlanOverrideRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalSettingsCommand" type="uci:CommTerminalSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with Waveform Capabilities of a Subsystem.  Waveform SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of applicable Waveform Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of Waveform Command.  Unlike most Waveform Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommTerminalSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:CommTerminalSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of a Waveform Settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommUtilizationRequest" type="uci:CommUtilizationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a dynamic request for use of a communication resource in support of a currently active mission.  Dynamic in the sense that the time to issue CommSchedulingRequirements has passed and it is understood that this request is immediate and will be subject to previously coordinated CommScheduleAllocations.  For some communication systems, this could be used completely independent of the communication scheduling messages.  Intended for interaction between a system/service that uses communications subject to approval by the service/function which manages allocations.  All client side.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CommUtilizationRequestStatus" type="uci:CommUtilizationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a CommUtilizationRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentConfiguration" type="uci:ComponentConfigurationMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains configuration information for a component.  The status of components is reported in the ComponentStatus and SubsystemStatus messages as applicable.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentConfigurationDataRequest" type="uci:ComponentConfigurationDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request configuration information for a component.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentConfigurationDataRequestStatus" type="uci:ComponentConfigurationDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This contains the response to a ComponentConfigurationRequest message.  It contains configuration information for a component.  It can also represent a tree structure of subcomponents (and their configurations) that comprise the component.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentSettingsCommand" type="uci:ComponentSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This is a message to send to a Component or on behalf of a Component.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:ComponentSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This is a message sent back to a ComponentSettingsCommand for or on behalf of a Component.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentStatus" type="uci:ComponentStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current operating state of a component or list of components.  A component can be physical or functional and could be outside of a Subsystem.  Any component that is relevant to the state, health and/or C2 of a system can be reported. This message can also report status for items in a component such as electrical equipment or hydraulic equipment that are documented outside of UCI in an ICD or to report engine specific status information such as throttle.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentStatusDataRequest" type="uci:ComponentStatusDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message request a Component's current ComponentStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ComponentStatusDataRequestStatus" type="uci:ComponentStatusDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains the results of a ComponentStatusRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlInterfacesCommand" type="uci:ControlInterfacesCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command from a controller to a controllee to establish or change its active/accepted control interfaces.  This message is only accepted/obeyed when it comes from the current controlling System (mission control) or the current primary controller (capability control).  A controllee reports its current active/accepted control interfaces via the ControlStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlInterfacesCommandStatus" type="uci:ControlInterfacesCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status for a ControlInterfacesCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlRequest" type="uci:ControlRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests control of a UCI System or its Capabilities.  UCI Systems are collections of capabilities that can be pseudo-independently shared/controlled by multiple users.  Control of a UCI System or its Capabilities can be requested using this message.  The System/Service that currently has control (mission control) or is the primary controller (capability control) is responsible for responding to this message.  The controlling System/Service informs the controlled System/Service of control changes via the ControlSourcesCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlRequestStatus" type="uci:ControlRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a ControlRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlSourcesCommand" type="uci:ControlSourcesCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a command from a controller to a controllee to establish or change its accepted control sources.  This message is only accepted/obeyed when it comes from the current controlling System (mission control) or the current primary controller (capability control); the current controller receives and adjudicates ControlRequest messages and then uses this message to inform the controllee of the approved control sources.  A controllee reports its current control sources via the ControlStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlSourcesCommandStatus" type="uci:ControlSourcesCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the status for a ControlSourcesCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ControlStatus" type="uci:ControlStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates who has various levels of control of a System, how the System is configured to be controlled, its autonomous/self-control capability, and its planning capability.  UCI Systems are collections of controllable capabilities that can be pseudo-independently shared/controlled by multiple users; this message indicates the current controllers.  UCI Systems support one or more forms of control via UCI messages/interfaces; this message indicates which categories of UCI control messages are accepted.  UCI Systems have various levels of control automation; this message indicates autonomous/self-control capabilities for each control category.  UCI Systems have a variety of planning processes; this message advertises supported processes.  The messages PlanningFunction*, Approval*, MissionContingencyAlert and ControlStatus are closely related.  They use a common taxonomy of autonomous functions (task allocation, route planning, mission activation and mission execution), triggers for those functions (new threats, new zones, failures, etc.) and results of those functions (dropped Tasks, changes in vulnerability, etc.).  ControlStatus indicates the full set of available autonomous functions/settings of a System.  PlanningFunction* indicates how the available settings are currently configured.  MissionContingencyAlert indicates an autonomous function/setting that has been triggered.  Approval* messages allow Operators to approve the results of autonomous responses as needed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CoordinatedRequirementSet" type="uci:CoordinatedRequirementSetMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Collects a set of linked Requirements which are intended to be executed in a coordinated way to achieve a common objective.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CoordinatedRequirementSetStatus" type="uci:CoordinatedRequirementSetStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Status message for a CoordinatedRequirementSet.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CounterSpaceActivity" type="uci:CounterSpaceActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the activity from a CounterSpace capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CounterSpaceCapability" type="uci:CounterSpaceCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the CS (CounterSpace) category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready CS Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CounterSpaceCapabilityStatus" type="uci:CounterSpaceCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the EA (Electronic Attack) category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready EA Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CounterSpaceDeconfliction" type="uci:CounterSpaceDeconflictionMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides the constraints and restrictions that must be followed within the CounterSpace mission area.  This is an Order.  The constraints and restrictions are provided by mission partners with the results compiled into a CS deconfliction plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CounterSpaceReport" type="uci:CounterSpaceReportMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The CounterSpaceReport is generated by the force.  The CounterSpaceReport summarizes the RF environment and system activity over a period of time (typically four hours).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CryptoCommand" type="uci:CryptoCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command actions for cryptographic slots.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CryptoCommandStatus" type="uci:CryptoCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to CryptoCommand and gives the processing status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="CryptoStatus" type="uci:CryptoStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of each cryptographic slot for a supporting crypto capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageAssessment" type="uci:DamageAssessmentMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates an assessment of the damage to an item or items in the battlespace.  Damage assessments are either for battle or collateral damage.  An assessment can be requested via the DamageAssessmentRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageAssessmentRequest" type="uci:DamageAssessmentRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request an assessment of battle or collateral damage.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageAssessmentRequestStatus" type="uci:DamageAssessmentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a DamageAssessmentRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageEstimate" type="uci:DamageEstimateMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates an estimate of the damage that will result from a Task.  Damage estimates are either for battle or collateral damage.  An estimate can be requested via the DamageEstimateRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageEstimateRequest" type="uci:DamageEstimateRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request an estimate of battle or collateral damage.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageEstimateRequestStatus" type="uci:DamageEstimateRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a DamageEstimateRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageReport" type="uci:DamageReportMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report battle or collateral damage that has been observed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DamageSummary" type="uci:DamageSummaryMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a summary of all the available damage reports for a given subject.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataDeleteRequest" type="uci:DataDeleteRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests deletion of non-message data.  It is a request to a data owner to delete products, files or other non-message data.  Message data can be deleted by the publisher/owner using the ObjectState element, independent of this message.  However, when message data is deleted the products, files and other data it references is generally not deleted and therefore this message is needed.  Further "data delete" differs from "erase" and specifically SubsystemEraseCommand in that it requests a "simple" specific delete with no expectation of the subsequent classification level.  This message can also be thought of as a more selective form of DataManagementRequest...Delete.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataDeleteRequestStatus" type="uci:DataDeleteRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status to a DataDeleteRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataManagementRequest" type="uci:DataManagementRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies data management activities to be performed on the system as a whole or on a specific service. System-Level data management requests are relative to a mission, and are accomplished by initiating a mission session and requesting that services perform specific actions (i.e., save, import, export, etc.).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataManagementRequestStatus" type="uci:DataManagementRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message statuses the result of data management activities. These responses can be the result of a request upon an entire "mission session" or a specific service. Services participate in a mission session, and the overall request upon the mission will be decomposed to specific requests to individual services.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataPlan" type="uci:DataPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Data Plan that will be applied to Data-Link Gateways during the mission in order to perform ingress and egress of Mission System and Product data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataPlanActivationCommand" type="uci:DataPlanActivationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command upload and activation of a DataPlan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataPlanActivationCommandStatus" type="uci:DataPlanActivationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a DataPlanActivationCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataPlanActivationStatus" type="uci:DataPlanActivationStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of a Data plan along the planning, conversion, upload, and activation chain.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataPlanOverrideRequest" type="uci:DataPlanOverrideRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides an operator the ability to command the activation of pre-mission planned Data-Link gateway settings, in coordination with, operator inputs.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataPlanOverrideRequestStatus" type="uci:DataPlanOverrideRequestStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a DataPlanOverrideRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataRecordListManagementRequest" type="uci:DataRecordListManagementRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to a data owner to add and/or delete Data Record List Entries to/from a Data Record List in an atomic (all or nothing) manner.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataRecordListManagementRequestStatus" type="uci:DataRecordListManagementRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a DataRecordListManagementRequest received by the applicable Data Record List managing interface. In the event that the update was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the update was unsuccessful. If successful, an updated message containing the modified Data Record Lists should also be published with an appropriate ObjectState value.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataRecordManagementRequest" type="uci:DataRecordManagementRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to a data owner to create, delete, and/or update Data Records in an atomic (all or nothing) manner.  Data Records that are the subject of a request to delete are indicated by one or more pairs of DataRecordID and MessageType for the purpose of conciseness whereas those that are subject to an update request are provided in totality.  When Data Records are deleted, the products, files, and other data that may be referenced are generally not deleted (see DataDeleteRequest for more details).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataRecordManagementRequestStatus" type="uci:DataRecordManagementRequestStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a DataRecordManagementRequest received by the applicable Data Record Managing Interface. In the event that the update was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the update was unsuccessful. If successful, a new DataRecord message or messages should also be sent with their ObjectState equal to the appropriate value depending on the operation performed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataStorageUtilizationRequest" type="uci:DataStorageUtilizationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a request the current data retention and storage availability on the network.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataStorageUtilizationRequestStatus" type="uci:DataStorageUtilizationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a view into the current data retention and storage availability on the network.  For instance, this message would characterize the remaining storage capacity on the network.  A display could request this object to determine if action is required at startup.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataUpdateRequest" type="uci:DataUpdateRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests updates for specific units or data types from a specific producing service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DataUpdateRequestStatus" type="uci:DataUpdateRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a DataUpdateRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Designation" type="uci:DesignationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to explicitly designate one or more specific objects as a target or non-target.  Optionally, the objective of the designation can be given.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DesignationRequest" type="uci:DesignationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request designation of one or more objects as either a target or a non-target.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DesignationRequestStatus" type="uci:DesignationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a DesignationRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DiscreteStatus" type="uci:DiscreteStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This Message contains the current status of a discrete within the system.  This message is for reporting unique data that cannot be reported with other messages.  This is not intended to replace the functionality for Special Signals or other system level discretes.  For example, this message could be used to report the WeightOnWheels status for informational use on a display, but the hardwired WeightOnWheels signal would still be required when needed as a safety interlock.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DLZ" type="uci:DLZ_MT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a single Dynamic Launch Zone (DLZ).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DLZ_Request" type="uci:DLZ_RequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request the generation of a Dynamic Launch Zone (DLZ) for a weapon.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DLZ_RequestStatus" type="uci:DLZ_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted DLZ_Request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI" type="uci:DMPI_MT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a single Designated/Desired Mean Point of Impact (DMPI) for a weapon/store.  The DMPI series of messages are designed to be used collectively to support a variety of use cases by separating data content into independent messages aligned with typical use case actors.  As an example, a dynamic mission planning use case would begin with a strike Task allocated to a System who would refer to it when sending a DMPI_DesignationRequest message.  A targeting cell or other system would then respond with a DMPI_Designation message plus one or more DMPI and DMPI_Status messages and, if applicable, a DMPI_Pattern message.  The System which the strike Task was allocated to (or its representative) would then report DMPI_Status as the strike Task proceeds.  A variety of pre-mission and other use cases are also supported.  For example, a targeting cell or other System could provide DMPI_Designation and/or DMPI messages independent of strike Tasks by omitting the DMPI_DesignationRequest message from the use case described above.
A single DMPI can have multiple desired weapons/stores.  See annotations for the DMPI_Status message for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_CancelCommand" type="uci:DMPI_CancelCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to cancel a previously planned and allocated DMPI.  A planning input such as MetaTask, Task and DMPI can't easily be canceled or removed from the System due to race conditions between their various users and publishers, inevitability of the associated action, etc.  Additionally, the operator or service initiating the cancellation might be different than the one that manages the DMPI.  In order to avoid race conditions, receive positive feedback on the success of the cancellation and allow canceled messages/objects to remain in the system for historical purposes, an explicit DMPI_CancelCommand is necessary.  A DMPI_CancelCommand shall result in the DMPI being canceled unless execution was already inevitable.  Status or feedback for this message is given through DMPI_Status.  Therefore, it does not form a typical request/response pair.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_CancelCommandStatus" type="uci:DMPI_CancelCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a DMPI_CancelCommand and indicates if the DMPI was canceled and if not why it was not canceled.  The resulting status of the DMPI is reported in DMPI_Status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_Designation" type="uci:DMPI_DesignationMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a set of DMPIs or a DMPI pattern for a given target.  This message can be a response to DMPI_DesignationRequest.  It can also be sent independently as a means of establishing a DMPI designation or pattern during pre-mission planning or at any other time prior to being requested.  See the annotations for the DMPI message for an overview of the entire DMPI series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_DesignationRequest" type="uci:DMPI_DesignationRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request DMPIs (to target an Entity) from a targeting cell or other node.  See the annotations for the DMPI message for an overview of the entire DMPI series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_DesignationRequestStatus" type="uci:DMPI_DesignationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a DMPI_DesignationRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_Pattern" type="uci:DMPI_PatternMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a pattern or group of DMPIs.  DMPI patterns are used to express spatial relationships between DMPIs of a single target.  See the annotations for the DMPI message for an overview of the entire DMPI series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DMPI_Status" type="uci:DMPI_StatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of a DMPI as it progresses through its life cycle.
For a single store DMPI (a DMPI that will be attacked with a single weapon/store), there is at most one associated Task and this message gives the complete status relative to it.  Similarly for a multiple store DMPI associated with a single Task.
For a multiple store DMPI associated with multiple Tasks this element provides partial status for one Task.  Multiple DMPI_Status messages from multiple sources are expected in this case, each referencing the same DMPI_ID but different Tasks, Systems and/or mission plans.
See the annotations for the DMPI message for an overview of the entire DMPI series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DoorCommand" type="uci:DoorCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands the weapons door to be opened or closed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DoorCommandStatus" type="uci:DoorCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The message indicates the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of the corresponding DoorCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="DoorStatus" type="uci:DoorStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the state of the weapons door.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_Activity" type="uci:EA_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the EA Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (EA_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work EA, 4) EA scheduled for the future and 5) EA that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single EA_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the EA_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_Capability" type="uci:EA_CapabilityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the EA (Electronic Attack) category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready EA Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:EA_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report the current status of the EA (Electronic Attack) capabilities available to the system as advertised in the EA_Capability message.  This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_Command" type="uci:EA_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the EA (Electronic Attack) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke an EA Capability to perform a new EA Activity or change an existing interactive EA Activity.  It differs from an EA Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_CommandStatus" type="uci:EA_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an EA_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the EA_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_ConsentRequest" type="uci:EA_ConsentRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request consent to perform EA.  Status for this command message is provided via the EA_ConsentRequestStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_ConsentRequestStatus" type="uci:EA_ConsentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report consent request status for its corresponding EA_ConsentRequest message used to perform Electronic Attack (EA) capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_SettingsCommand" type="uci:EA_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the EA Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EA_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:EA_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report settings status for its corresponding EA_SettingsCommand message used to change the settings associated with its advertised Electronic Attack (EA) capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EarthOrientationParameters" type="uci:EarthOrientationParametersMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report characteristics of and corrections for perturbations of earth's rotation and orbit around the sun.  Perturbation forces include those from the gravitational force of the moon, the oblate shape of the earth and the tilt and "wobble" of the earth's rotational axis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EEI" type="uci:EEI_MT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains, Essential Elements of Information (EEI); ad-hoc free text that describes information which is referred to during tasking and mission planning.  EEI are used to describe essential information that may not be available in other UCI messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Effect" type="uci:EffectMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a C2 Requirement in the form/abstraction of an Effect.  Other C2 Requirement forms include Action, Task, [Capability]Command and  Response.  For example, a DEFEAT Effect could be planned/decomposed into a FIND Action, SAR Task, SAR_Command, Attack Action and Strike Task.  See the child EffectType element and its enumerates for the Effect taxonomy.  Effects can be planned (*Plan messages), directly commanded (EffectCommand* messages) and/or event-driven responses (Response* messages).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectActivity" type="uci:EffectActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Effect Capability of a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectCancelCommand" type="uci:EffectCancelCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to cancel an Effect which has been allocated and planned.  A planning input such as Effect, Action, Task and DMPI can't easily be canceled or removed from the System due to race conditions between their various users and publishers, inevitability of the associated activity, etc.  Additionally, the operator or service initiating the cancellation might be different than the one that manages RequirementSets and/or issues *PlanCommand, introducing additional race conditions.  In order to avoid race conditions, receive positive feedback on the success of the cancellation and allow canceled messages/objects to remain in the system for historical purposes, an explicit EffectCancelCommand is necessary.  An EffectCancelCommand shall result in the Effect being canceled unless execution was already inevitable.  If the Effect was inevitable and started executing but can be stopped (such as a long collection Effect) it shall cancel as soon as possible.  EffectCancelCommand shall result in managers of RequirementSets to remove the associated Effect, thereby removing the Effect from the next planning cycle.  Services other than those that manage RequirementSets and execute activities aren't required to handle EffectCancelCommand.  EffectCancelCommand doesn't necessarily trigger a replan (there isn't a corresponding planning trigger), therefore a System could continue to navigate routes related to the canceled Effect, even though the Effect won't occur.  A subsequent replan could exclude the Effect.  Status or feedback for this message is given through EffectCancelCommandStatus but the resulting status of the Effect is given through EffectStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectCancelCommandStatus" type="uci:EffectCancelCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to an EffectCancelCommand and indicates if the Effect was canceled and if not why it was not canceled.  The resulting status of the Effect is reported in EffectStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectCapability" type="uci:EffectCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Effect category of UCI Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectCapabilityStatus" type="uci:EffectCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Effect capabilities available to the system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectCommand" type="uci:EffectCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Effect form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectCommandStatus" type="uci:EffectCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an EffectCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectExecutionApprovalStatus" type="uci:EffectExecutionApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the state of approval for execution of a task. ApprovalPolicy allow an authority to define policies to approve or reject a Effect as it enters execution. Approval may or may not be required. If approval is required, there can be multiple approvers. See Approval* messages for additional details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlan" type="uci:EffectPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Effects allocated to a System.  Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination.  This message decouples Effect allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Effect allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on Effect allocation.    
An EffectPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example the EffectPlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to an Effect allocation service that the produces an EffectPlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Effects.
Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the Effect allocation process and captured in the EffectPlan to supplement the information in the allocated Effects themselves.
An EffectPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  An EffectPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple EffectPlans and *Plans in general.  An EffectPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an EffectPlan are given in other EffectPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:EffectPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for an EffectPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanCommand" type="uci:EffectPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes an EffectPlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  This message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, an EffectPlan could be constrained by a previous RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:EffectPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an EffectPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the EffectPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of an EffectPlan  may occur autonomously, without an EffectPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the EffectPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:EffectPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more EffectPlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via EffectPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanningStatus" type="uci:EffectPlanningStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines planning status of a single Effect.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanStatus" type="uci:EffectPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for an EffectPlan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanValidation" type="uci:EffectPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the EffectPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:EffectPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing EffectPlan.  An EffectPlan can become invalid due to changes in Effect timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing EffectPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:EffectPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate an EffectPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EffectStatus" type="uci:EffectStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for an Effect.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ElevationRequest" type="uci:ElevationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is to request the elevation of a list of points.  Elevation data can come from different sources such as Digital Terrain Elevation Data (DTED), Global Digital Elevation Map (GDEM), mensuration tools, or operator input.  Although DTED is the most widely used there could be other elevation sources hence the use of term elevation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ElevationRequestStatus" type="uci:ElevationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains the response to an ElevationRequest with list of points in same order with elevation field filled in when possible.  Elevation data can come from different sources such as Digital Terrain Elevation Data (DTED), Global Digital Elevation Map (GDEM), mensuration tools, or operator input.  Although DTED is the most widely used there could be other elevation sources hence the use of term elevation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ElevationVisibilityRequest" type="uci:ElevationVisibilityRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is to request the visibility from a given point to a list of points; i.e. which points in the list are visible to the FromPoint.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ElevationVisibilityRequestStatus" type="uci:ElevationVisibilityRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains the response to an ElevationVisibilityRequest message.  The response list indicates request point visibility with respect to the request FromPoint.  The response visibility data list is in the same order as the requesting point list.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Entity" type="uci:EntityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents an object (vehicle, weapon, person, building, etc.) in the battlespace.  This message can represent blue players that are non-taskable UCI participants.  Its structure is applicable to both blue force and red force objects.  An Entity can be anything from a pre-briefed, EOB object derived from HUMINT to an actively tracked object fused from one or more dynamically tasked sensors.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityConfirmation" type="uci:EntityConfirmationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to confirm the Executing/Tactical System is observing the correct Entity by providing a Confirmation Probability or Confirmation Confidence Interval.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityConfirmationRequest" type="uci:EntityConfirmationRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This information will be used to confirm the Executing/Tactical System is observing the correct Entity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityConfirmationRequestStatus" type="uci:EntityConfirmationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an EntityConfirmationRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityLost" type="uci:EntityLostMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents details regarding the loss of a previously known Entity.  Other Entity-related messages characterize an Entity at a point in time based on an observation or propagation/prediction.  In contrast, this message provides an indication that a previously established Entity wasn't observed as expected/planned along with metrics characterizing the loss. For example, if several sensors are tracking an Entity and one sensor "loses" the Entity, this message would provide those details. This message could be provided directly by an observing System that produces other Entity* messages, by a Service that analyzes Entity* messages from other Systems over time, etc. This message applies to any object that is being observed by a sensor suite (including blue, red, and gray objects). This message also provides the details of an event occurring after enough data has been gathered to create this message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityManagementRequest" type="uci:EntityManagementRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a request for an Entity management function such as fusion control.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityManagementRequestStatus" type="uci:EntityManagementRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>An Entity Management Request status is used by a service to report status/state of an EntityManagementRequest.  Since an EntityManagementRequest can be applied across systems and services, an EntityManagementRequest may result in generation of many status messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityMetadata" type="uci:EntityMetadataMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies supplemental data for Entities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityNotification" type="uci:EntityNotificationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a notification related to an Entity.  There are endless possible events, states, state changes and behaviors of Entities to monitor/observe.  In lieu of enumerating them all, this message categorizes them into "perspectives" such as kinematics, weather and identity.  For example, a weather monitoring Service could produce an EntityNotification message advising that a particular Entity is entering an area with potential weather hazards.  This message optionally references other messages that triggered or are related to the notification.  For example, an EntityNotification with a weather perspective could reference a WeatherDataset message with detailed weather data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalCSO" type="uci:EntityOrbitalCSO_MT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This messages represents closely-space operations (CSO) of orbiting entities, observed and characterized over a period of time including behavioral details beyond basic kinematics. This message applies to any object that is being observed by a sensor suite (including blue, red, and gray objects).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalElementSet" type="uci:EntityOrbitalElementSetMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents what is commonly referred to as the two line element (TLE) for an Entity in orbit around Earth.  It describes a satellite's orbit kinematics using general perturbations theory (SGP, SGP4, PPT3) and Keplerian elements.  An orbital element set (OES), or simply "el set", contains six classical orbital elements and a time element (epoch) which can be converted into position and velocity vectors to generate the geometry and motion of where the satellite is located and where it is going.  An OES is a refined collection of data, continually updated over the life of the satellite.  Single position kinematics, ephemeris and VCM for an Entity are given in other Entity* messages.  Placing Entity-related data into separate messages enables separate sources/services to report data for the same Entity instance.   For example, an identity fusion service could publish the base Entity message to establish the existence of a battlespace object and other kinematic fusion and/or kinematic propagation services could publish Entity kinematics at higher rates and in different reference frames without resending the base Entity data that did not change.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalElementSetRequest" type="uci:EntityOrbitalElementSetRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests an orbital element set for one or more Entities, applicable for a particular time window.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalElementSetRequestStatus" type="uci:EntityOrbitalElementSetRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an EntityOrbitalElementSetRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalEphemeris" type="uci:EntityOrbitalEphemerisMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents ephemeris for an Entity in the form of a series of predicted or actual kinematic positions and velocities of a satellite in an orderly sequence of time points (may be created at fixed or variable time spacing). Optionally includes covariance matrices that contains information about the uncertainties associated with the solution of the element corrections.  Single position kinematics, TLE and VCM for an Entity are given in other Entity* messages.  Placing Entity related data into separate messages enables multiple sources/Services to report data for the same Entity instance.  For example, an identity fusion service could publish the base Entity message to establish the existence of a battlespace object and other kinematic fusion and/or kinematic propagation services could publish Entity kinematics at higher rates and in different reference frames without resending the base Entity data that did not change.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalEphemerisRequest" type="uci:EntityOrbitalEphemerisRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests orbital ephemeris for one or more Entities, applicable for a particular time window.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalEphemerisRequestStatus" type="uci:EntityOrbitalEphemerisRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an EntityOrbitalEphemerisRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalManeuver" type="uci:EntityOrbitalManeuverMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents maneuvers of orbiting entities, characterized over a period of time including behavioral details beyond basic kinematics. This includes indicating the details of the maneuver, even if the maneuver event is captured after it occurs. This message applies to any object that is being observed by a sensor suite (including blue, red, and gray objects).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalVCM" type="uci:EntityOrbitalVCM_MT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a "vector covariance matrix" or VCM which is a specialized state vector, along with extensive propagation parameters, tailored specifically for customers who are using an ASW system to propagate the vector.  A VCM should not be confused with the simpler and more common kinematic variance for position-position or position-velocity.  The VCM has all the additional information needed to correctly propagate the vector using the Special Perturbations Ephemeris Generator in ASW. The VCM includes a covariance matrix that provides the epoch covariance in equinoctial elements and only provides UVW variances.  Single position kinematics, TLE and ephemeris for an Entity are given in other Entity* messages.  Placing Entity related data into separate messages enables multiple sources/Services to report data for the same Entity instance.   For example, an identity fusion service could publish the base Entity message to establish the existence of a battlespace object and other kinematic fusion and/or kinematic propagation services could publish Entity kinematics at higher rates and in different reference frames without resending the base Entity data that did not change.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalVCM_Request" type="uci:EntityOrbitalVCM_RequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests an orbital Vector Covariance Matrix (VCM) for an Entity that is in orbit.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityOrbitalVCM_RequestStatus" type="uci:EntityOrbitalVCM_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains the response to an EntityOrbitalVCM_Request message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EntityPropagation" type="uci:EntityPropagationMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the extrapolated position of an Entity (vehicle, weapon, person, building, etc.) in the battlespace.  This message only contains extrapolated kinematic information.  Additional non-kinematic characteristics of a given Entity can be found in other messages.  This message is applicable to Services (such as kinematic fusion) which extrapolate kinematics between updates from sensors or other sources contributing fusion inputs.  Entity kinematics updates that are a result of updates from contributing sources shouldn't be reported using this message.  This message doesn't have a ObjectState; the extrapolated Entities follow the ObjectState of their associated Entity message/object.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EOB_CorrelationRecord" type="uci:EOB_CorrelationRecordMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents an "EOB correlation record". This is the correlated data, from either a Signal Report or Entity, that was potentially used to update an EOB Record.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_Activity" type="uci:ESM_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the ESM Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (ESM_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work ESM, 4) ESM scheduled for the future and 5) ESM that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single ESM_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the ESM_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_Capability" type="uci:ESM_CapabilityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Electronic Support Measure (ESM) category of UCI Capability.  ESM is a form of signals intelligence.  ESM is distinct from COMINT and ELINT.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready ESM Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:ESM_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the ESM (Electronic Support Measures) capabilities available to the system as advertised in the ESM_Capability message.  This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_Command" type="uci:ESM_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the ESM (Electronic Support Measures) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke an ESM Capability to perform a new ESM Activity or change an existing interactive ESM Activity.  It differs from an ESM Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_CommandStatus" type="uci:ESM_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an ESM_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the ESM_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_ConsentRequest" type="uci:ESM_ConsentRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request consent to perform ESM.  Status for this command message is provided via the ESM_ConsentRequestStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_ConsentRequestStatus" type="uci:ESM_ConsentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report consent request status for its corresponding ESM_ConsentRequest message used to perform Electronic Support Measures (ESM) capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_SettingsCommand" type="uci:ESM_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the ESM Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ESM_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:ESM_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report settings status for its corresponding ESM_SettingsCommand message used to change the settings associated with known Electronic Support Measures (ESM) capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="EW_Configuration" type="uci:EW_ConfigurationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to document the configuration settings of RF based subsystems such as ESM, EA, ELINT and COMINT.  These configuration settings can be used by a resource manager service to configure and match the subsystem capabilities  to the available RF Distribution and internal channel definitions.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ExternalDataRequest" type="uci:ExternalDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to initiate the retrieval of data that has been updated/provided by an external/national database/source.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ExternalDataRequestStatus" type="uci:ExternalDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is to report status/state of an ExternalDataRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Fault" type="uci:FaultMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents a fault for the system.  Intended to be used by services such as a health monitoring service for centralized fault management and fault isolation of a system.  Allows announcing a fault state change (raised or cleared) from a service as opposed to a capability or component of a subsystem.  Also conveys a service relationship in addition to a subsystem relationship.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FileLocation" type="uci:FileLocationMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the network location of a file.  See the annotations for FileMetadata for an explanation of how it works together with this message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FileMetadata" type="uci:FileMetadataMT" uci:version="001.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies UCI relevant metadata for a File.  A File is an item that isn't a Product, whose content and format are specified outside of the UCI standard.  It does not contain actual file contents nor does it specify their network location.  Network location is published in the FileLocation message.  "Metadata" includes a summary of the content type, classification, file name, file format and other characteristics of the file.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FlightActivity" type="uci:FlightActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is actively doing  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities for a Capability category (ESM, EA, etc.). In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FlightCapability" type="uci:FlightCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Flight category of UCI Capability.  It "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Flight Capability so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations and for the Flight Capability, it could be a required route sequence, orbit/loiter point, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FlightCapabilityStatus" type="uci:FlightCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Flight capabilities available to the system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ForeignKeyMap" type="uci:ForeignKeyMapMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains a list of ForeignKeyPair IDs that themselves specify IDs, within the domain of this schema, to be associated with a key, ID, etc. that exists in another system, protocol, network, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ForeignKeyPair" type="uci:ForeignKeyPairMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a paired mapping of a Foreign Key to a Native ID.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FusionSettings" type="uci:FusionSettingsMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is utilized to capture the settings for Fusion services.  This message is a reflection of all settings resulting from all requests that are currently in effect for the specified service.  It is expected that fusion services manage their own settings and publish them on updates and/or in response to QueryDataRequest.  Fusion settings can be changed via FusionSettingsRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FusionSettingsRequest" type="uci:FusionSettingsRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a request to change settings for a Fusion Service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="FusionSettingsRequestStatus" type="uci:FusionSettingsRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used by a Fusion service to report status/state of a FusionSettingsRequest.  Since a FusionSettingsRequest can be applied across systems and services, it may result in status messages from each.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewayActivity" type="uci:GatewayActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A Gateway Activity message describes what Data Processor Capability is doing.   This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Data Processor Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewayCapability" type="uci:GatewayCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A Gateway Capability message describes what a Gateway Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  The capabilities include Gateway data processor instantiation/tear-down, etc. settings provided by the Gateway.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewayCapabilityStatus" type="uci:GatewayCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Data Processor capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available, commanded operational states, associated Data Processor profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewayCommand" type="uci:GatewayCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a derivation of the UCI Capability command.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and Data Processor commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, Data Processor command rank, Data Processor Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewayCommandStatus" type="uci:GatewayCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a GatewayCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the GatewayActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewaySettingsCommand" type="uci:GatewaySettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with Data Processor Capabilities of a Gateway.  Data Processor SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of applicable Data Processor Capabilities of the Gateway; they are considered presettings, independent of Data Processor command.  Unlike most Data Processor commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="GatewaySettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:GatewaySettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of a Data Processor Settings Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_Activity" type="uci:IFF_ActivityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the IFF Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (IFF_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work IFF, 4) IFF scheduled for the future and 5) IFF that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single IFF_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the IFF_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_Capability" type="uci:IFF_CapabilityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Identify Friend or Foe (IFF) category of UCI Capability. It covers both the Transponder and Interrogator SubCapablities. This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready IFF Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:IFF_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the IFF (Identify Friend or Foe) capabilities available to the system as advertised in the IFF_Capability message.  This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_Command" type="uci:IFF_CommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the IFF (Identify Friend or Foe) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  IFF is a communication Capability that has both an Interrogation and Transponder SubCapability.  It is used to manually invoke an IFF Capability to perform a new IFF Activity or to make a change to an existing interactive IFF Activity.  Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_CommandStatus" type="uci:IFF_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an IFF_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the IFF_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_ConsentRequest" type="uci:IFF_ConsentRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request consent to perform IFF when applicable.  Status for this command message is provided via the IFF_ConsentRequestStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_ConsentRequestStatus" type="uci:IFF_ConsentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report consent request status for its corresponding IFF_ConsentRequest message used to perform IFF capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_SettingsCommand" type="uci:IFF_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with the Interrogator SubCapability of the IFF Capabilities of a Subsystem.  There are no relevant commandable settings for the Transponder SubCapability.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IFF_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:IFF_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report settings status for its corresponding ESM_SettingsCommand message used to change the settings associated with known Identify Friend or Foe (IFF) capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IO_PortCommand" type="uci:IO_PortCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command actions for a User IO Port.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IO_PortCommandStatus" type="uci:IO_PortCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to IO_PortCommand and gives the processing status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="IO_PortStatus" type="uci:IO_PortStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of each User IO Port.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="LAR" type="uci:LAR_MT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a single Launch Acceptability Region (LAR).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="LAR_Report" type="uci:LAR_ReportMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a System's status in relation to a Launch Acceptability Region (LAR).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="LAR_Request" type="uci:LAR_RequestMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request the generation of a Launch Acceptability Region (LAR) for a weapon.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="LAR_RequestStatus" type="uci:LAR_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted LAR_Request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="LaunchObservation" type="uci:LaunchObservationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability for a system to report an observation for an identified launch event or changes during a launch event. This message includes the reporting of stage separation through the lifecycle of a multi-stage launch event. Providing the stage of the launch at the time of observation allows the consumer to identify what Entity is continuing in the launch and what Entity is no longer continuing in the launch.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Log" type="uci:LogMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is intended to support centralized, OS agnostic logging by a service/subsystem/component/capability related to the operation or information of a service/subsystem/component/capability.  For example, the Message data could be captured and stored by a log aggregator and used for display and post mission analysis.  This differs from messages such as SubsystemStream which is intended for debugging in real time and Subsystem unique data that cannot be standardized; or ServiceErrorReport which is intended for error awareness.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskCommand" type="uci:MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The Acceptable Level of Risk (ALR) command message allows an operator to set a specific level of risk the MA is allowed to incur during the execution of a Mission Plan. The setting options (AcceptableRiskSetting) include HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW, or NONE at the package or vehicle level. This ALR setting applies to all tasks and actions for the vehicle or package. The ALR is a separate message from the SurvivabilityRiskLevelMT message in that the ALR defines the maximum risk that MA is allowed to plan for and the SurvivabilityRiskLevelMT describes what HIGH, MEDIUM, and LOW mean with respect to the Maximum Continuous Exposure for a given Vulnerability Type. The SurvivabilityRiskLevelID specifies the ID of the SurvivabilityRiskLevelMT message that should be active or used. The C2 operator will send the ALR command directly to a Package lead or an individual ACP. The Package Lead will distribute this command message to all followers, or the applicable ACPs. A new command will override any previously sent message, even if the CommandID has changed. The current ALR setting can be deactivated by sending a Command with the CommandState field set to CANCEL. If the MA does not have a command, its ALR will be NONE by default.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status for the MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskStatus" type="uci:MA_AcceptableLevelOfRiskStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of MA with respect to the active or current Acceptable Level of Risk setting, the activated SurvivabilityRiskLevel ID, and the Package or System ID with which it is associated.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Action" type="uci:MA_ActionMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a C2 Requirement in the form/abstraction of an Action. Other C2 Requirement forms include Effect, Task, [Capability]Command and  Response. For example, a DEFEAT Effect could be planned/decomposed into a FIND Action, SAR Task, SAR_Command, Attack Action and Strike Task. See the child EffectType element and its enumerates for the Effect taxonomy. Actions can be planned (*Plan messages), directly commanded (ActionCommand* messages) and/or event-driven responses (Response* messages).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionCapability" type="uci:MA_ActionCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Action category of UCI Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionCommand" type="uci:MA_ActionCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Action form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ActionCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an ActionCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionPlan" type="uci:MA_ActionPlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Actions allocated to a System. Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination. This message decouples Action allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Action allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on Action allocation.
An ActionPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. For example the ActionPlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to an Action allocation service that the produces an ActionPlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Actions.
Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the Action allocation process and captured in the ActionPlan to supplement the information in the allocated Actions themselves.
An ActionPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. An ActionPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple ActionPlans and *Plans in general. An ActionPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an ActionPlan are given in other ActionPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_ActionPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an ActionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ActionPlanCommand message. Creation/update of an ActionPlan may occur autonomously, without an ActionPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ActionPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ActionPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an ActionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ActionPlanCommand message. Creation/update of an ActionPlan may occur autonomously, without an ActionPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ActionPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_ActionPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the ActionPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActionStatus" type="uci:MA_ActionStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for an Action.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActivityPlan" type="uci:MA_ActivityPlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies planned *Capability-related activities for a System, independent of System kinematics. Activities are the planned implementation details of Effects, Actions, Tasks, Capability Commands, etc. Activity planning can be done manually by operators, by an activity planning service or a combination. This message decouples activity planning from allocation, kinematic and other types of planning; the ActivityPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on activity planning.
An ActivityPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. For example an ActivityPlan from a previous round of planning could be an input to an activity planning service that adds additional planned activities to it.
A single System can have multiple independent ActivityPlans for different Task, Capability, sensor, etc. types; that are developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture; at different times then independently updated and approved. Optionally, all types of activities for a System can be included in a single ActivityPlan.
An ActivityPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. An ActivityPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple ActivityPlans and *Plans in general. An ActivityPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an ActivityPlan are given in other ActivityPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActivityPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_ActivityPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands an ActivityPlan related planning function/action. Example planning functions include updating an existing ActivityPlan, creating a new ActivityPlan or combining/stitching existing ActivityPlans together. The command includes many inputs to the planning function while others may come from a variety of other messages. The expected output of the planning function is an ActivityPlan message.
The command includes options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing plans of the same type or by other plan types. For example, an ActivityPlan could be constrained by a previous ActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
The command includes environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be for added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of speculative or "what-if" contingency planning.
The command includes settings for "weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActivityPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ActivityPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an ActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ActivityPlanCommand message. Creation/update of an ActivityPlan may occur autonomously, without an ActivityPlanCommand, in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ActivityPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ActivityPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_ActivityPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the ActivityPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_AMTI_Capability" type="uci:MA_AMTI_CapabilityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the AMTI (Airborne Moving Target Indicator) category of UCI Capability. AMTI detects aircraft through active radar search and track. This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready AMTI Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others. Functions which support these end effects turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages. Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform. Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ApprovalAuthority" type="uci:MA_ApprovalAuthorityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation> This message assigns ApprovalPolicy to a System or Systems and specifies which System or Systems is the approval authority for that policy. An approval authority is responsible for approving or rejecting activities specified in the ApprovalPolicy for the specified System. See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ApprovalManagementCommand" type="uci:MA_ApprovalManagementCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a command for on demand approval management processing for a given approvable item.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ApprovalManagementCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ApprovalManagementCommandStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>An Approval Management Command status is used by a service to report status/state of an ApprovalManagementCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ApprovalPolicy" type="uci:MA_ApprovalPolicyMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies which activities of a system require approval from an approval authority. The Approval* series of messages are designed to request and establish an approval hierarchy that governs select, possibly automated, activities of the UCI family of services such as route planning and task execution. They can be used collectively to support a variety of activity approval use cases by separating data content into independent messages aligned with typical use case actors. As an example, one System could create/manage ApprovalPolicy while another could assign them to Systems and assign approval authorities via the ApprovalAuthority message. Then, one or more other Systems could make ApprovalRequests based on the ApprovalPolicy. Finally, the System or Systems assigned by ApprovalAuthority approve or reject the ApprovalRequest using ApprovalRequestStatus. The messages allow multiple approval authorities per ApprovalPolicy and/or multiple ApprovalPolicy per System. When multiple ApprovalPolicy apply to a single System, they should be evaluated independently; if any policy requires approval for a certain condition then approval is necessary; if multiple policies required approval for a certain condition then approval should be requested for each. The messages PlanningFunction*, Approval*, MissionContingencyAlert and ControlStatus are closely related. They use a common taxonomy of autonomous functions (task allocation, route planning, mission activation and mission execution), triggers for those functions (new threats, new zones, failures, etc.) and results of those functions (dropped Tasks, changes in vulnerability, etc.). ControlStatus indicates the full set of available autonomous functions/settings of a System. PlanningFunction* indicates how the available settings are currently configured. MissionContingencyAlert indicates an autonomous function/setting that has been triggered. Approval* messages allow Operators to approve the results of autonomous responses as needed. Updates supersede previous or instances of the message; each instance of the message shall have a complete specification of the approval policy.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ApprovalRequest" type="uci:MA_ApprovalRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request for approval of an activity specified in an associated ApprovalPolicy. See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ApprovalRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_ApprovalRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an ApprovalRequest. See the annotations for the ApprovalPolicy message for an overview of the entire Approval* series of messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Assessment" type="uci:MA_AssessmentMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides an assessment of other UCI data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_AssessmentRequest" type="uci:MA_AssessmentRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to generate different types of assessments.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_AssessmentRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_AssessmentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to the request for an assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Authorization" type="uci:MA_AuthorizationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message establishes authorization for a User to task a System. Authorization to task is independent of the C2 hierarchy established via the Control* series of messages. It is also independent of the approval hierarchy established via the Approval* series of messages. For example, System1 could have mission control of System2 and have responsibility for approving System2's Tasks for execution but not be authorized to Task System2. Further, authorization to task a System has different meanings based on the type of System and its position in the C2 hierarchy. For example, authorization for System3 to task System4, where System4 is a vehicle, means that Tasks created by System3 can only be allocated to System4 even if other capable siblings of System4 are available. In another example, authorization for System5 to task System6, where System6 controls many Systems, each of which controls multiple vehicle Systems, means that Tasks created by System5 can be allocated to any System subordinate to System6.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CAP_ExecutionStatusReport" type="uci:MA_CAP_ExecutionStatusReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains the CAP status of vehicles executing a CAP.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CapabilitySet" type="uci:MA_CapabilitySetMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The MA_CapabilitySet message provides a set of Capabilities that can optionally be linked to a System, Mission, or MissionPlan that the set is relevant to.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CarrierDeckHandlingCommand" type="uci:MA_CarrierDeckHandlingCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a command from the Carrier Deck Handler for an ACP to perform an activity critial for launch readiness or clearing the deck upon recovery.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CarrierDeckHandlingCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_CarrierDeckHandlingCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a response to the Carrier Deck Handler for an ACP performing an activity critial for launch readiness or clearing the deck upon recovery.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CarrierLaunchReadyStatus" type="uci:MA_CarrierLaunchReadyStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies whether the Mission Autonomy on an ACP has passed all internal checks and is ready for launch from an Aircraft Carrier.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CarrierReport" type="uci:MA_CarrierReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the state of an carrier.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CarrierState" type="uci:MA_CarrierStateMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the state of an carrier.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CommAvailableEndpoints" type="uci:MA_CommAvailableEndpointsMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Publishes available network endpoints as provided by network overhead messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CommErrorReport" type="uci:MA_CommErrorReportMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a general report of an communication error that is of significance to other Systems, Services, etc. in the network. This message is not intended for remote, centralized error logging; it is meant for "error awareness".</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_CommTeamReport" type="uci:MA_CommTeamReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status for the available endpoints within the communication network.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ConstraintPriorityList" type="uci:MA_ConstraintPriorityListMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The Constraint Priority List (CPL) allows an operator to set default values for the Priority field in OpZoneMT, OpVolumnMT, and OpLineMT (further referred to as geozones) messages based on type. If the field in those pre-planned messages are empty and the type is in the CPL, the Priority value from the CPL shall be used to set the Priority value for that message. A Priority value of zero (0) is the highest priority value. If the geozone message Priority field is empty and the geozone type is not in the CPL, the Priority field shall be set to the DefaultPriority from the CPL. Only a single CPL can be active at any given time, but CPLs can be associated with OpZones or OpVolumes to indicate when they shall be activated to override a default CPL.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ConstraintPriorityListActivationData" type="uci:MA_ConstraintPriorityListActivationDataMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message, when activated by the MA_ConstraintPriorityListCommand, defines what MA_ConstraintPriorityListMT message shall be active as default, or when the ACP is within specific geozones.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ConstraintPriorityListActivationStatus" type="uci:MA_ConstraintPriorityListActivationStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of MA with respect to the activated MA_ConstraintPriorityListMT ID, the activated MA_ConstraintPriorityListActivationData ID, and the Package or System ID its associated with.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ConstraintPriorityListCommand" type="uci:MA_ConstraintPriorityListCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message allows a C2 to activate a specific MA_ConstraintPriorityListActivationData message. The C2 will send this command message to a Package lead or an individual ACP directly. The Package Lead will distribute this command message to the rest of the ACPs. Any new command will override any previously sent message, even if the CommandID has changed. The command can be deactivated by sending a CommandState with CANCEL message. If the MA does not have a command, this is the same as having no Constraint Priority List.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ConstraintPriorityListCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ConstraintPriorityListCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status for the MA_ConstraintPriorityListCommandMT message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_COP_ConfigurationSettings" type="uci:MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Contains the current COP configuration settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsCommand" type="uci:MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Command message to adjust the configuration settings for the COP among ACPs.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Command status message in response to MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsRequest" type="uci:MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Data request message to request the current COP configuration settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_COP_ConfigurationSettingsRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Data request status message which provides current COP configuration settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DataDisseminationPlan" type="uci:MA_DataDisseminationPlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Data Dissemination Plan that will be applied to Dissemination Services during the mission in order to perform ingress and egress of Mission Systems, Product, and Protocol data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DataDisseminationPlanActivationCommand" type="uci:MA_DataDisseminationPlanActivationCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command upload and activation of a DataDisseminationPlan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DataDisseminationPlanActivationCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_DataDisseminationPlanActivationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a DataDisseminationPlanActivationCommand. The current state of activation of a DataDisseminationPlan and its parts is given in the DataDisseminationPlanActivationStatus and associated GatewayActivity, CommProtocolActivity and CommTerminalActivity messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DataDisseminationPlanActivationStatus" type="uci:MA_DataDisseminationPlanActivationStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of a DataDisseminationPlan along the planning, conversion, upload, and activation chain.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DataDisseminationPlanOverrideRequest" type="uci:MA_DataDisseminationPlanOverrideRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides an operator the ability to command the activation of pre-mission planned Data-Link gateway settings, in coordination with, operator inputs.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DataDisseminationPlanOverrideRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_DataDisseminationPlanOverrideRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a DataPlanOverrideRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DeconflictionSolutionRequest" type="uci:MA_DeconflictionSolutionRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation> This message sends a route plan, via Action Request, as a possible resolution for an identified path conflict between two or more platforms. Conflict status is included to indicate whether this proposed route plan resolves the conflict or not. </xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DeconflictionSolutionRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_DeconflictionSolutionRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation> This message defines a response to a proposed solution to a route path conflict, as either ACCEPTED or REJECTED </xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Designation" type="uci:MA_DesignationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to explicitly designate one or more specific objects as a target or non-target. Optionally, the objective of the designation can be given.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DesignationRequest" type="uci:MA_DesignationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request designation of one or more objects as either a target or a non-target.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_DesignationRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_DesignationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a MA_DesignationRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EffectCapability" type="uci:MA_EffectCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Effect category of UCI Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EffectPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_EffectPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes an EffectPlan planning process. Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process. This message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages. A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types. For example, an EffectPlan could be constrained by a previous RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EffectPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_EffectPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an EffectPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the EffectPlanCommand message. Creation/update of an EffectPlan may occur autonomously, without an EffectPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the EffectPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EffectPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_EffectPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the EffectPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EffectStatus" type="uci:MA_EffectStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for an Effect.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EngagementCriteria" type="uci:MA_EngagementCriteriaMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The Engagement Criteria allows an operator to set specific criteria for the target designation and weapons employment process. For the EngagementCriteria message, all fields but 1 Boolean (MA_EngagementCriteriaMT.StrikeConsentRequired) are optional, and if they are left blank then the Engagement Criteria does not allow for that activity to be authorized. The IdMatrixCriteria provides criteria that can be used to assign or confirm the setting of the Identity of an EntityMT (EntityMDT.Identity.Standard.StandardIdentity) to be FRIEND, NEUTRAL, SUSPECT or HOSTILE. The TargetDesignationCriteria field provides a mechanism to specify what criteria can be used to set or confirm that an EntityMT is a Target (meaning that a MA_DesignationRequestMT can be created, and sent to be approved by the related Target Authority who would then send a MA_DesignationMT). The TargetAuthorityCriteria field specifies who is authorized to approve or set something as a Target (by sending a MA_DesignationMT or a specific commanded/approved task defined in the associated ApprovalPolicyMT) and specifies who is authorized to respond to a StrikeConsentRequestMT. The TargetCustodyCriteria field can be used to define what must be true while the ACP is performing a Weapons Engagement on an Entity. The StrikeConsentRequired field specifies whether (TRUE) or not (FALSE) the Strike Activities requires consent to transition from the ENABLED state to an ACTIVE state. If TRUE, each Strike Activity would be required to send a StrikeConsentRequestMT to an Authorized Operator (defined in the TargetAuthorityCriteria field) and receive a StrikeConsentRequestStatusMT before each Strike Activity can execute the Strike.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EngagementCriteriaCommand" type="uci:MA_EngagementCriteriaCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message allows a C2 to activate a specific MA_EngagementCriteriaMT message and set optional parameters of the associated geozone (ApplicableRegion) and valid time (TimeValid) window for where and when the Engagement Criteria message is valid. If the ApplicableRegion field is configured, the ACP must be in this region when it fires, and the calculated weapon impact location of the Target must be within this region. The MA shall only start an engagement for targets inside of the Region(s). If the TimeValid field is configured, the Engagement Criteria is only active during that time. If the TimeValid field is left empty, the Engagement Criteria is valid until the Command is canceled or an updated.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EngagementCriteriaCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_EngagementCriteriaCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status for the MA_EngagementCriteriaCommandMT message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EngagementCriteriaStatus" type="uci:MA_EngagementCriteriaStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status for the active Engagement Criteria information that was configured by the MA_EngagementCriteriaCommandMT. It provides the EngagementCriteriaID and the optional parameters of the associated geozone (ApplicableRegion) and valid time (TimeValid) window for where and when the EngagementCriteriaID is valid. If the ApplicableRegion field is set, the ACP must be in this region when it fires, and the calculated weapon impact location of the Target must be within this region. The MA shall only start an engagement for targets inside of the Region(s). If the TimeValid field is set, the Engagement Criteria is only active during that time. If the TimeValid  field is left empty, the Engagement Criteria is valid until the Command is canceled or an updated.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Entity" type="uci:MA_EntityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents an object (vehicle, weapon, person, building, etc.) in the battlespace.  This message can represent blue players that are non-taskable UCI participants.  Its structure is applicable to both blue force and red force objects.  An Entity can be anything from a pre-briefed, EOB object derived from HUMINT to an actively tracked object fused from one or more dynamically tasked sensors.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EscortActivity" type="uci:MA_EscortActivityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Escort Capability of a Subsystem of a System. In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing. It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.). In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (EscortCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work Escort, 4) Escort scheduled for the future and 5) Escort that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons. A single EscortCommand or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the EscortActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EscortCapability" type="uci:MA_EscortCapabilityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Escort category of UCI Capability. This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Strike Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EscortCapabilityStatus" type="uci:MA_EscortCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Escort capabilities available to the system as advertised in the EscortCapability message. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EscortCommand" type="uci:MA_EscortCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Escort form of the general UCI Capability Command. It is used to manually invoke a Escort Capability to perform a new Escort Activity or change an existing interactive Escort Activity. It differs from a Escort Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan. Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages. Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them. However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema. Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_EscortCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_EscortCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an EscortCommand. This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected. This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled. The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the EscortActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ExecutionPriorityList" type="uci:MA_ExecutionPriorityListMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The Execution Priority List (EPL) allows an operator to set default values for the Rank field in the TaskMT and ActionMT (further referred to as task) messages based on the type. If the Rank field in those messages is empty and the type is in the EPL, the Rank from the EPL can be used. A Rank.Priority value of zero (0) is the highest priority value, and an empty field is the lowest. If the planned task message Rank field is empty and the task type is not in the EPL, the Rank field should be left empty and have the lowest priority. Only a single EPL can be active at any given time, but EPLs should be associated with OpZones or OpVolumes to indicate when they should be activated to override a default EPL.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ExecutionPriorityListActivationData" type="uci:MA_ExecutionPriorityListActivationDataMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message, when activated by the MA_ExecutionPriorityListCommand, defines what MA_ExecutionPriorityListMT message shall be active as default, or when the ACP is within specific geozones.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ExecutionPriorityListActivationStatus" type="uci:MA_ExecutionPriorityListActivationStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of MA with respect to the activated MA_ExecutionPriorityListMT ID, the activated MA_ExecutionPriorityListActivationData ID, and the Package or System ID its associated with.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ExecutionPriorityListCommand" type="uci:MA_ExecutionPriorityListCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message allows a C2 to activate a specific MA_ExecutionPriorityListActivationData message. The C2 will send this command message to a Package lead or an individual ACP directly. The Package Lead will distribute this command message to the rest of the ACPs. Any new command will override any previously sent message, even if the CommandID has changed. The command can be deactivated by sending a CommandState with CANCEL message. If the MA does not have a command, this is the same as having no Execution Priority List.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ExecutionPriorityListCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ExecutionPriorityListCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status for the MA_ExecutionPriorityListCommandMT message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Fault" type="uci:MA_FaultMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents a fault for the system. Intended to be used by services such as a health monitoring service for centralized fault management and fault isolation of a system. Allows announcing a fault state change (raised or cleared) from a service as opposed to a capability or component of a subsystem. Also conveys a service relationship in addition to a subsystem relationship. Extended to include a modified Fault Severity Enumeration for Mission Autonomy.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_FlightActivity" type="uci:MA_FlightActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is actively doing  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities for a Capability category (ESM, EA, etc.). In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_FlightCapability" type="uci:MA_FlightCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Flight category of UCI Capability. It "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Flight Capability so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations and for the Flight Capability, it could be a required route sequence, orbit/loiter point, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_FlightCapabilityStatus" type="uci:MA_FlightCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Flight capabilities available to the system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_FlightCommand" type="uci:MA_FlightCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Flight form of the general UCI Capability Command. It is used to manually invoke a Flight Capability to perform a new Flight Activity or change an existing interactive Flight Activity. It differs from a Flight Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan. Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages. Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them. However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema. Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_FlightCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_FlightCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a FlightCommand. This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected. This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled. The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the FlightActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_GatewayActivity" type="uci:MA_GatewayActivityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A Gateway Activity message describes what Data Processor Capability is doing. This messages includes configuration options available in the DataPlan* and DataDisseminationPlan* message sets. This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Data Processor Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_GatewayCommand" type="uci:MA_GatewayCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This messages includes configuration options available in the DataPlan* and DataDisseminationPlan* message sets. This message is a derivation of the UCI Capability command. Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and Data Processor commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, Data Processor command rank, Data Processor Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_GatewayCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_GatewayCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a GatewayCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the GatewayActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_IgnoreConstraintCommand" type="uci:MA_IgnoreConstraintCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The Ignore Zone Constraints command message allows an operator to specify individual tasks to ignore specific geozones. The task(s) are specified in the ReferenceType field and can be the ID of any of the following: Effect, Action, Task, Response, or Capability Command. The ListOfConstraintsToIgnore field allows setting one or more OpZone or OpVolume ID to be ignored. The IgnoreAllConstraints field allows the operator to override any and all constraints as an attempt to accomplish a task. The C2 can send a new command by setting the information as needed, along with a unique CommandID and the CommandState set to NEW. A command can be updated by C2 sending the message with the same CommandID as an active message and setting the CommandState to UPDATE. A command can be deactivated by C2 sending the message with the same CommandID as an active message and setting the CommandState to CANCEL.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_IgnoreConstraintCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_IgnoreConstraintCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation> This message indicates the status for the MA_IgnoreConstraintCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_InteroperabilityPolicy" type="uci:MA_InteroperabilityPolicyMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies how interoperability concerns should be handled by Mission Autonomy. Interoperability concerns arise when multiple valid approaches are possible for a given process, but different platforms taking different approaches can lead to incompatibilities in implementation. Specifying which approach should be taken in the A-GRA requirements would go against the A-GRA goal of permitting alternative approaches so long as interoperability is maintained between all compliant implementations. Instead, Interoperability Policy provides a place to align which implementation all platforms' Mission Autonomy implementations will take, ensuring interoperability but allowing different approaches to be chosen when and where desired. Platforms shall obey the implementation prescribed in the interoperability policy. InteroperabilityPolicy shall be updated identically to all platforms on startup. Updates supersede previous instances of the message; each instance of the message shall have a complete specification of the Interoperability policy.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_LeadershipMetrics" type="uci:MA_LeadershipMetricsMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies vehicle leadership fitness scores and the leader election method for use in determination of package leader.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_LeaderUpdateRequest" type="uci:MA_LeaderUpdateRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent between peers to initiate a package leader election, share vehicle leadership fitness scores, and/or assign/nominate a package leader.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_LeaderUpdateRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_LeaderUpdateRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a LeaderUpdateRequest with a defined Status field. The message shall contain the vehicle's response to being nominated/assigned to be the leader.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MessageAcknowledgmentConfiguration" type="uci:MA_MessageAcknowledgmentConfigurationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides the current configuration of the message </xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MessageAcknowledgmentConfigurationRequest" type="uci:MA_MessageAcknowledgmentConfigurationRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Allows for setting the Message Acknowledgment Configuration defaults and for individual systems.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MessageAcknowledgmentConfigurationRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_MessageAcknowledgmentConfigurationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the status of a request to update a system's Message Acknowledgment Configuration defaults.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MessagingSubscriptionSettingCommand" type="uci:MA_MessagingSubscriptionSettingCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to adjust the topic subscription settings for the Decentralized Messaging Service (DMS) interface between ACP's on a network.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MessagingSubscriptionSettingCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_MessagingSubscriptionSettingCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to change the topic subscription settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MessagingSubscriptionStatus" type="uci:MA_MessagingSubscriptionStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the active messaging subscriptions which may be activated, deactivated, or deleted via MA_MessagingSubscriptionSettingCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlan" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies an integrated set of sub-*Plans (TaskPlan, RoutePlan and RouteActivityPlan for example) that together are the mission plan for a single System. Mission planning can be done manually by operators, by planning services or a combination. This message references a set of coherent sub-*Plans and indicates the order in which they will be executed when activated. The sub-*Plans can be discretely created then integrated into the MissionPlan or created near simultaneously in a single or multiple step mission planning process that creates all sub-*Plans. A MissionPlan can be created, updated and approved somewhat independent of its sub-*Plans. For a hierarchical System of Systems, a higher tiered System's MissionPlan can reference the related/coherent MissionPlans of subordinate Systems.
*Plans can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a MissionPlan are given in other MissionPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanActivationCommand" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanActivationCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command activation of a *Plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanActivationCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanActivationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a MissionPlanActivationCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanActivationStatus" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanActivationStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of a mission plan and its subplans along the planning, conversion, upload, and activation chain. *Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given. Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a MissionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the MissionPlanCommand message. Creation/update of a MissionPlan may occur autonomously, without a MissionPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the MissionPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a MissionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the MissionPlanCommand message. Creation/update of a MissionPlan may occur autonomously, without a MissionPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the MissionPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more MissionPlans. Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages. This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given. Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the MissionPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons. The message allows an existing MissionPlan to be checked for validity given such changes. The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies. Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process. The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_MissionPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate a MissionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_MissionRequirementStatus" type="uci:MA_MissionRequirementStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of the Requirements of a given MissionPlan. It enables aggregation of Requirement planning, execution approval and execution status per MissionPlan. This message provides no more information than is already available directly in the corresponding Requirement status messages; TaskPlanningStatus, TaskExecutionApprovalStatus and TaskStatus for example. It is as an optional mechanism for reporting and monitoring the progress of a mission, from a Requirement perspective, through a single message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_NavigationReport" type="uci:MA_NavigationReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current navigation state of the System.  It is one of the "station-keeping" series of messages reported by most UCI Systems.  The station-keeping series is: PositionReport, SystemStatus, NavigationReport, [Capability]Status and [Capability]Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpCommand" type="uci:MA_OpCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Command for Op* types.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_OpCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an MA_OpCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OperatorRole" type="uci:MA_OperatorRoleMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This messages defines an Operator Role such that it can be referenced within other messages and queried for.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpLine" type="uci:MA_OpLineMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a line which may be taken into consideration by services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpPlan" type="uci:MA_OpPlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the OpPoints, OpLines, OpZones, OpVolumes and OpRouting allocated to a System. Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination. This message decouples Op* allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Op* allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on Op* allocation.

An OpPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. 

An OpPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. An OpPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple OpPlans and *Plans in general. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an OpPlan are given in other OpPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpPoint" type="uci:MA_OpPointMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a special class of points which may be taken into consideration by Services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpRouting" type="uci:MA_OpRoutingMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents routing constraints which may be taken into consideration by Systems and Services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpStatus" type="uci:MA_OpStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the status for an Op* type.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpVolume" type="uci:MA_OpVolumeMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a 3-dimensional zone which may be taken into consideration by services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OpZone" type="uci:MA_OpZoneMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a zone which may be taken into consideration by Services during their operation. Its primary use is airspace coordination.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OrbitActivityPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_OrbitActivityPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes an OrbitActivityPlan planning process. Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process. The message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages. A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types. For example, an OrbitActivityPlan could be constrained by an existing kinematic OrbitPlan, a previous OrbitActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be for added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OrbitActivityPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_OrbitActivityPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an OrbitActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the OrbitActivityPlanCommand message. Creation/update of an OrbitActivityPlan may occur autonomously, without an OrbitActivityPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the OrbitActivityPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_OrbitActivityPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_OrbitActivityPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PackageManagementCommand" type="uci:MA_PackageManagementCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to command one or more entities to form, join or leave a Package.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PackageManagementCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_PackageManagementCommandStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a PackageManagementCommand and indicates if the command was accepted and if not why it was rejected. The resulting status of the Package Change is reported in PackagePartnerStatusReport for each system in the original command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PackagePartnerStatusReport" type="uci:MA_PackagePartnerStatusReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the resulting status of a package change for a PackageManagementCommand for individual entities in a package. </xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlanModificationRequest" type="uci:MA_PlanModificationRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to modify a Plan or set of Plans.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlanModificationRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_PlanModificationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a PlanModificationRequest received by the applicable C2 system. In the event that the modification was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the modification was unsuccessful. If successful, a new Plan message or messages should be sent out.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlanningFunction" type="uci:MA_PlanningFunctionMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates all supported planning-related functions and interfaces of a System. The closely related PlanningFunctionStatus message indicates how planning-related functions and interfaces of the System are currently configured. Planning functions indicated here can be performed by human operators and/or machines/Services.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlanningFunctionSettingsCommand" type="uci:MA_PlanningFunctionSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message changes the configuration of planning-related functions and interfaces of a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlanningFunctionSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_PlanningFunctionSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides transactional status for the PlanningFunctionsSettingsCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlanningFunctionStatus" type="uci:MA_PlanningFunctionStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the configuration/status of currently enabled planning-related functions and interfaces of a System. The closely related PlanningFunction message indicates all supported planning-related functions and interfaces.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlatformOverrideCommand" type="uci:MA_PlatformOverrideCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands a global override of a platform parameter (speed, altitude, formation, or IFF) to be set or cleared on an ACP or team.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlatformOverrideCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_PlatformOverrideCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the acceptance or rejection of a platform override command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PlatformOverrideStatus" type="uci:MA_PlatformOverrideStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the active platform override settings applied to an ACP.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PositionReport" type="uci:MA_PositionReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the location and speed of a System or Resource. It is one of the "station-keeping" series of messages reported by most UCI Systems. The station-keeping series includes PositionReport, SystemStatus, NavigationReport, [Capability]Status and [Capability]Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PositionReportDetailed" type="uci:MA_PositionReportDetailedMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the location, speed, and direction of a System or Resource. It is not one of the "station-keeping" series of messages, but is designed to provide positional information at a much higher frequency than PositionReport, and allows for multiple redundant systems to provide reports in the same message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_PrioritizationList" type="uci:MA_PrioritizationListMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a list of battlespace objects or actors, prioritized for an enumerated purpose or perspective. It establishes an order/priority by which objects should be addressed/processed. This message has a spectrum of possible uses from the very broad focused to the very narrowly focused. For example, it could be used broadly to establish the priority of threats/targets as general guidance to all of the Systems in a System of Systems, agnostic of their individual Missions, Capabilities or Services. Alternatively, it could be very narrowly focused to establish a different priority of threats/targets for each phase of F2T2EA, filtered/tailored by System, Service, time or space.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RaftAppendEntriesRequest" type="uci:MA_RaftAppendEntriesRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a Raft AppendEntries request sent by the current leader to maintain authority via heartbeat, preventing subsequent elections and optionally to replicate log entries.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RaftAppendEntriesRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_RaftAppendEntriesRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to the preceding Raft AppendEntries request. It includes the follower's current term, which may cause the leader to update its term, and an optional success boolean indicating whether the follower's log contains a matching entry.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RaftVoteRequest" type="uci:MA_RaftVoteRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent by a candidate to request votes during a Raft leader election.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RaftVoteRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_RaftVoteRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to the preceding Raft leader election vote request. This request status message includes the current term and whether the vote was granted.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Response" type="uci:MA_ResponseMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines a Response to triggering event or events. It is effectively a Requirement to "be ready to" 1) instantiate and plan a Requirement (Effect, Action, Task or [Capability]Command tailored to the triggering even, 2) activate a previously created *Plan, 3) issue an alert, 4) do nothing. Responsibility for the Response can be allocated/assigned to a System via a ResponsePlan that is part of a MissionPlan. In other words, ResponsePlans allow Responses to be delegated across a SoS. Activating a ResponsePlan activates monitoring of the triggering events for each of its Responses. A Response is executing whenever its triggering events are being monitored. An executing Response causes no changes to the Mission unless a triggering event occurs. When a triggering event occurs, the options within the Response are evaluated, potentially leading to selection and instantiation of one or options within the Response.

***********************
For Planned C2, evaluating a Response option based on Requirements or *Plans generally means evaluating whole *Plans. To evaluate a Response option it is generally necessary to create, update, validate, assess and/or score *Plans that include it. Selecting and activating a Response option generally means approving and activating a *Plan that includes it.
***********************

A Response can continue to execute (triggering events can continue to be monitored) after a Response option has been initiated. An instantiation of a Response option is detailed in the ResponseActivity message. Response can be manually initiated via the ResponseCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponseActivity" type="uci:MA_ResponseActivityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Response Capability of a System. Response Activities start at the time when monitoring for the triggering event starts. A given trigger can occur multiple times in the course of a Mission. Therefore a Response Activity can indicate multiple current and past trigger instances.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponseCommand" type="uci:MA_ResponseCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Response form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponseCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ResponseCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an ResponseCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponsePlan" type="uci:MA_ResponsePlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Responses allocated to a System.  Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination.  This message decouples Response allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Response allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on Response allocation.

A ResponsePlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example the ResponsePlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to a response allocation service that the produces a ResponsePlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Responses.

Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the response allocation process and captured in the ResponsePlan to supplement the information in the allocated Responses themselves.

A ResponsePlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  A ResponsePlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple ResponsePlans and *Plans in general.  A ResponsePlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a ResponsePlan are given in other ResponsePlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponsePlanCommand" type="uci:MA_ResponsePlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to perform a response allocation plan process or function. The ResponsePlan message/object is the output of a response allocation plan process or function. The ResponsePlan output of one response allocation plan process function could be an input to the next function. UCI enables "discovery" of the response allocation plan processes and functions of a System via the ControlStatus message. This mechanism for discovery empowers a System performing response allocation planning for subordinate Systems of heterogeneous types and/or service/function/process architecture to selectively use, replace or supplement them.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponsePlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_ResponsePlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a ResponsePlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ResponsePlanCommand message. Creation/update of a ResponsePlan may occur autonomously, without a ResponsePlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ResponsePlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponsePlanValidation" type="uci:MA_ResponsePlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the ResponsePlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_ResponseStatus" type="uci:MA_ResponseStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for a Response.
***For Responses, "execution" only includes the explicit response management action indicated in the Response message; it does not include subsequent creation of *Plans, execution of Requirements, etc. that may or may not follow. For example, a Response is executing as soon as its triggers are being monitored. The triggers may never happen even if the Response is executing. If a trigger happens, the Response continues to execute but is now "instantiated" once, with details given in the ResponseActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RouteActivityPlan" type="uci:MA_RouteActivityPlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies planned activities for a System with a RoutePlan. A "Route Activity" is any *Capability-related activity planned in context of the kinematic RoutePlan of the System. Activities are the planned implementation details of Effects, Actions, Tasks, Capability Commands, etc. Route activity planning can be done manually by operators, by an activity planning service or a combination. This message decouples activity planning from allocation, kinematic and other types of planning; the RouteActivityPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on activity planning.
A RouteActivityPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. For example a RouteActivityPlan from a previous round of planning could be an input to an activity planning service that adds additional planned activities to it.

A single System can have multiple independent RouteActivityPlans for different Task, Capability, sensor, etc. types; that are developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture; at different times then independently updated and approved. Optionally, all types of activities for a System can be included in a single RouteActivityPlan.

A RouteActivityPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. A RouteActivityPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple RouteActivityPlans and *Plans in general. A RouteActivityPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a RouteActivityPlan are given in other RouteActivityPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RouteActivityPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_RouteActivityPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes a RouteActivityPlan planning process. Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process. The message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages. A few examples of the many inputs include:

1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types. For example, a RouteActivityPlan could be constrained by an existing kinematic RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.

2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be for added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.

3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.

4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RouteActivityPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_RouteActivityPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a RouteActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the RouteActivityPlanCommand message. Creation/update of a RouteActivityPlan may occur autonomously, without a RouteActivityPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the RouteActivityPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RouteActivityPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_RouteActivityPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the RouteActivityPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RouteDeconflictionAlert" type="uci:MA_RouteDeconflictionAlertMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message associates a set of path conflict meta data to a unique conflict identifier that can be used as a reference when proposing resolutions to the detected conflict.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RoutePlan" type="uci:MA_RoutePlanMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the planned kinematic route of a System. "Route" is defined as a series of WGS-84 waypoints and is intended for Systems such as aircraft and ground vehicles whose kinematic plans are best represented in an Earth-centered rotational frame. Route planning can be done manually by operators, by a route planning service or a combination. This message decouples kinematic planning from allocation, action and other types of planning; the kinematic RoutePlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on route planning.

A RoutePlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. For example a coarse RoutePlan created by the top System in a hierarchical System of System for overall Mission orchestration and deconfliction could be an input to a route planning service that the produces a detailed, flyable RoutePlan for a specific subordinate System.

A single System can have multiple independent RoutePlans for different types of planned routes such as primary, ingress, egress and contingency. The different types of routes can be developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture at different times then independently updated and approved. Optionally, all types of routes for a System can be included in a single RoutePlan.

A RoutePlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. A RoutePlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple RoutePlans and *Plans in general. A RoutePlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a RoutePlan are given in other RoutePlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_RxDataPayload" type="uci:MA_RxDataPayloadMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the hexBinary data payload received by the Decentralized Messaging Service (DMS).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_StrikeActivity" type="uci:MA_StrikeActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the availability of weapon capabilities and MDF state and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_StrikeCapability" type="uci:MA_StrikeCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Strike category of UCI Capability. This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Strike Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others. Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages. Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform. Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_SystemManagementRequest" type="uci:MA_SystemManagementRequestMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request changes to a system's status or configurable settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_SystemManagementRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_SystemManagementRequestStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a response to a SystemManagementRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_SystemNotification" type="uci:MA_SystemNotificationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a notification related to a System. There are endless possible events, states, state changes and behaviors of Systems to monitor/observe. In lieu of enumerating them all, this message categorizes them into "perspectives" such as kinematics, weather and identity. For example, a weather monitoring Service could produce a SystemNotification message advising that a particular System is entering an area with potential weather hazards. This message optionally references other messages that triggered or are related to the notification. For example, a SystemNotification with a weather perspective could reference a WeatherDataset message with detailed weather data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_SystemsNeededRequest" type="uci:MA_SystemsNeededRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the minimum number of systems needed to complete a desired set of tasks.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_SystemsNeededRequestStatus" type="uci:MA_SystemsNeededRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a SystemsNeededRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_Task" type="uci:MA_TaskMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a Task that a System will perform against a specific entity or geographical location. Tasks include flight, collection, strike, electronic attack, directed energy, laser designation, cargo delivery and communication relay. They are abstracted to a level of C2 and autonomy derived from UCI operational vignettes. Tasks enable services, operators and other actors to do automated, dynamic, machine-to-machine allocation of Tasks to Systems and subsequent mission planning. If necessary, authorization of Users to Task Systems can be controlled using the Authorization message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskCommand" type="uci:MA_TaskCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Task form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_TaskCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an MA_TaskCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskPlan" type="uci:MA_TaskPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Tasks allocated to a System. Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination. This message decouples Task allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Tasks allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on task allocation.

A TaskPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. For example the TaskPlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to a task allocation service that the produces a TaskPlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Tasks.

Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the task allocation process and captured in the TaskPlan to supplement the information in the allocated Tasks themselves.

A TaskPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. A TaskPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple TaskPlans and *Plans in general. A TaskPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a TaskPlan are given in other TaskPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskPlanCommand" type="uci:MA_TaskPlanCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to perform a task allocation plan process or function. The TaskPlan message/object is the output of a task allocation plan process or function. The TaskPlan output of one task allocation plan process function could be an input to the next function. UCI enables "discovery" of the task allocation plan process and function of a System via the ControlStatus message. This mechanism for discovery empowers a System performing task allocation planning for subordinate Systems of heterogeneous types and/or service/function/process architecture to selectively use, replace or supplement them.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_TaskPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a TaskPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the TaskPlanCommand message. Creation/update of a TaskPlan may occur autonomously, without a TaskPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the TaskPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskPlanValidation" type="uci:MA_TaskPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the TaskPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TaskStatus" type="uci:MA_TaskStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for a Task.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TxDataPayloadCommand" type="uci:MA_TxDataPayloadCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to send an encoded data payload offboard to either a single or multiple network endpoints.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_TxDataPayloadCommandStatus" type="uci:MA_TxDataPayloadCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to send an encoded data payload. </xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_WEZ" type="uci:MA_WEZ_MT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a single Weapon Engagement Zone (WEZ). The WEZ represents a less dynamic capability than the DLZ for air to air engagements and is not reliant on target state data to be produced.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_WEZ_Request" type="uci:MA_WEZ_RequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request the generation of a Weapon Engagement Zone (WEZ). The WEZ represents a less dynamic capability than the DLZ for air to air engagements and is not reliant on target state data to be produced. </xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MA_WEZ_RequestStatus" type="uci:MA_WEZ_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted MA_WEZ_Request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MessageTransmissionFilterRecord" type="uci:MessageTransmissionFilterRecordMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is intended to provide a consolidated list of records that will update the MessageTransmissionList</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MessageTransmissionList" type="uci:MessageTransmissionListMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains a list of message information IDs that a system should expose to other UCI compliant systems.  This allows system operator involvement in selection of objects for transmit. This enables selectable data sharing between UCI compliant systems.  For example, this message could be used to coordinate, amongst Services within a System, which UCI messages they should publish to UCI nodes outside the System</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionActivationRequest" type="uci:MissionActivationRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests that a Mission "session", previously defined by MissionDefinition, be activated or deactivated.  For example, a previously defined and saved Mission can be activated which triggers loading of the Mission, Service initialization, querying for persisted data, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionActivationRequestStatus" type="uci:MissionActivationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a request to action a MissionDefinition to a new state.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionContingencyAlert" type="uci:MissionContingencyAlertMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides an alert that a triggering condition for autonomous functions has occurred.  It also indicates the autonomous response if applicable.  The messages PlanningFunction*, Approval*, MissionContingencyAlert and ControlStatus are closely related.  They use a common taxonomy of autonomous functions (task allocation, route planning, mission activation and mission execution), triggers for those functions (new threats, new zones, failures, etc.) and results of those functions (dropped Tasks, changes in vulnerability, etc.).  ControlStatus indicates the full set of available autonomous functions/settings of a System.  PlanningFunction* indicates how the available settings are currently configured.  MissionContingencyAlert indicates an autonomous function/setting that has been triggered.  Approval* messages allow Operators to approve the results of autonomous responses as needed.  MissionContingencyAlert is a bit of a misnomer for this message as it uses the word "contingency" while the other messages use the word "autonomy"; this message could be called AutonomyAlert.  All autonomous functions represented by these messages issue MissionContingencyAlerts when triggered, per the active autonomy settings.  Additionally, MissionContingencyAlerts can be issued even if an autonomous response isn't taken, again per the active autonomy settings; if autonomous response is not allowed based on the autonomy settings, a recommended course of action is provided to the operator. This message is not intended to invoke autonomous functions itself but will provide:
1. Insight into the occurrence of triggering conditions of autonomous functions.
2. An indication that the system is in the process of autonomously addressing the contingency.
3. A recommended course of action if autonomy is not allowed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionDefinition" type="uci:MissionDefinitionMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines a mission that spans multiple vehicles and its associated parameters needed for planning and execution.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionEnvironmentOverride" type="uci:MissionEnvironmentOverrideMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is an indicator to planning services to modify their planning information from what is currently known.  Various items in planning objects can be changed.  Overrides modify the behavior of planning and not the original objects.  For example the ELNOT of an EOB Entity can be overridden to a different ELNOT during planning.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlan" type="uci:MissionPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies an integrated set of sub-*Plans (TaskPlan, RoutePlan and RouteActivityPlan for example) that together are the mission plan for a single System.  Mission planning can be done manually by operators, by planning services or a combination.  This message references a set of coherent sub-*Plans and indicates the order in which they will be executed when activated.  The sub-*Plans can be discretely created then integrated into the MissionPlan or created near simultaneously in a single or multiple step mission planning process that creates all sub-*Plans.  A MissionPlan can be created, updated and approved somewhat independent of its sub-*Plans.  For a hierarchical System of Systems, a higher tiered System's MissionPlan can reference the related/coherent MissionPlans of subordinate Systems.
*Plans can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a MissionPlan are given in other MissionPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanActivationApprovalStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanActivationApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the Activation Approval status of a Mission Plan and optionally the sub-plans.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanActivationCommand" type="uci:MissionPlanActivationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to command activation of a *Plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanActivationCommandStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanActivationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a MissionPlanActivationCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanActivationStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanActivationStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status of a mission plan and its subplans along the planning, conversion, upload, and activation chain.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for a MissionPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanCommand" type="uci:MissionPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes a MissionPlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  A MissionPlan is an aggregation of other *Plan types, therefore MissionPlanCommand planning processes (and this message) can result in multiple *Plan types/messages.  This message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, a MissionPlan could be constrained by a previous RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a MissionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the MissionPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of a MissionPlan  may occur autonomously, without a MissionPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the MissionPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more MissionPlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanMetricsReport" type="uci:MissionPlanMetricsReportMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies an aggregation of metrics on which to judge the success of a mission.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanMetricsReportRequest" type="uci:MissionPlanMetricsReportRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests post-mission or in-mission metrics for a MissionPlan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanMetricsReportRequestStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanMetricsReportRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a MissionPlanMetricsReportRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanningOrchestration" type="uci:MissionPlanningOrchestrationMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message enables a controlling system to define how its planning processes link to subordinate planning processes.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a MissionPlan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanValidation" type="uci:MissionPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the MissionPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:MissionPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing MissionPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:MissionPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate a MissionPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MissionRequirementStatus" type="uci:MissionRequirementStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of the Requirements of a given MissionPlan.  It enables aggregation of Requirement planning, execution approval and execution status per MissionPlan.  This message provides no more information than is already available directly in the corresponding Requirement status messages; TaskPlanningStatus, TaskExecutionApprovalStatus and TaskStatus for example.  It is as an optional mechanism for reporting and monitoring the progress of a mission, from a Requirement perspective, through a single message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MultistaticEmitterData" type="uci:MultistaticEmitterDataMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability for the AMTI, SAR, and SMTI capabilities to identify the location and associated waveform information of specific emitters while performing multistatic operations.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="MvAnomalyEvent" type="uci:MvAnomalyEventMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability for a system to report a notification of significant changes in visual magnitude of a Resident Space Object (RSO). This message is the result of the processing of information reported by other systems thus giving this a confidence of the observation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="NavigationReport" type="uci:NavigationReportMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current navigation state of the System.  It is one of the "station-keeping" series of messages reported by most UCI Systems.  The station-keeping series is: PositionReport, SystemStatus, NavigationReport, [Capability]Status and [Capability]Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ObservationMeasurementReport" type="uci:ObservationMeasurementReportMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to provide kinematic measurement data/products from a sensor, along with associated data collected in tandem (e.g., per-measurement radar cross-section data) with the kinematic measurements. This message supports measurements for both known and unknown objects, and may optionally be associated with a known object.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ObservationReport" type="uci:ObservationReportMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to provide 3rd party observation information about a known object. This message is meant to provide additional data on the object as observed by other than the object owner.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpaqueCapability" type="uci:OpaqueCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Opaque category of UCI Capability.  Opaque messages advertise the minimum set of parameters necessary for disclosing general status and limit capacities to updating subsystem settings by updating Mission Data Files (MDFs). Users of the Opaque Class of messages should only use the minimum set of fields necessary to satisfy subsystem and platform interactions.  The UCI Implementation is limited at this time to the OpaqueCapability and OpaqueSettingsCommand message pairs.   Opaque capabilities cannot be considered when using performing task allocation or route planning using the MissionPlanCommand message. Opaque capabilities should only be used when the type of payload cannot be published. Opaque capabilities should not be represented at different classification levels using the same CapabilityID. If the OpaqueCapability exists at the low level, and the same capability need to be represented at a higher level then the higher level would need its own CapabilityID and capability messages. This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Opaque Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Manager  and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpaqueCapabilityStatus" type="uci:OpaqueCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Opaque capabilities available to the system. This message represents the Opaque category of UCI Capability.  Opaque messages advertise the minimum set of parameters necessary for disclosing general status and limit capacities to updating subsystem settings by updating Mission Data Files (MDFs). Users of the Opaque Class of messages should only use the minimum set of fields necessary to satisfy subsystem and platform interactions. The UCI Implementation is limited at this time to the OpaqueCapability and OpaqueSettingsCommand message pairs.   Opaque capabilities cannot be considered when using performing task allocation or route planning using the MissionPlanCommand message. Opaque capabilities should only be used when the type of payload cannot be published. Opaque capabilities should not be represented at different classification levels using the same CapabilityID. If the OpaqueCapability exists at the low level, and the same capability need to be represented at a higher level then the higher level would need its own CapabilityID and capability messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpaqueSettingsCommand" type="uci:OpaqueSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the Opaque Capabilities of a Subsystem. This message represents the Opaque category of UCI Capability.  Opaque messages advertise the minimum set of parameters necessary for disclosing general status and limit capacities to updating subsystem settings by updating Mission Data Files (MDFs). Users of the Opaque Class of messages should only use the minimum set of fields necessary to satisfy subsystem and platform interactions. The UCI Implementation is limited at this time to the OpaqueCapability and OpaqueSettingsCommand message pairs.  SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Opaque capabilities cannot be considered when using performing task allocation or route planning using the MissionPlanCommand message. Opaque capabilities should only be used when the type of payload cannot be published. Opaque capabilities should not be represented at different classification levels using the same CapabilityID. If the OpaqueCapability exists at the low level, and the same capability need to be represented at a higher level then the higher level would need its own CapabilityID and capability messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpaqueSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:OpaqueSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of an Opaque settings command. This message represents the Opaque category of UCI Capability.  Opaque messages advertise the minimum set of parameters necessary for disclosing general status and limit capacities to updating subsystem settings by updating Mission Data Files (MDFs). Users of the Opaque Class of messages should only use the minimum set of fields necessary to satisfy subsystem and platform interactions. The UCI Implementation is limited at this time to the OpaqueCapability and OpaqueSettingsCommand message pairs. Opaque capabilities cannot be considered when using performing task allocation or route planning using the MissionPlanCommand message. Opaque capabilities should only be used when the type of payload cannot be published. Opaque capabilities should not be represented at different classification levels using the same CapabilityID. If the OpaqueCapability exists at the low level, and the same capability need to be represented at a higher level then the higher level would need its own CapabilityID and capability messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Operator" type="uci:OperatorMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This messages provides details of a human operator associated with a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorActionCommand" type="uci:OperatorActionCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used by the operator to provide an action command in response to a notification with controlled actions. This message is meant to enable an operator to communicate information to a service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorActionCommandStatus" type="uci:OperatorActionCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the processing state of the indicated OperatorActionCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorAnnotation" type="uci:OperatorAnnotationMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is intended to allow an operator a method to add notes and annotations during a mission. The contents of this message are intended to be recorded in a mission data log and not intended to be used to produce system or service responses.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorLocationOfInterest" type="uci:OperatorLocationOfInterestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents an operator- or other user -created geospatial location of interest that may be the precursor to a Task, Entity, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorNotification" type="uci:OperatorNotificationMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to notify an operator of something that may require their attention.  It can be used to notify an operator that an error or issue is imminent; it is not assumed that this message represents an error that has occurred.  This message is meant for a service to communicate information to an operator display.  It is not to be used for machine to machine automation or to drive functionality directly.  Notifications that require machine action should be given via other messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorNotificationAck" type="uci:OperatorNotificationAckMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used by an operator console to indicate an acknowledgment of a notification.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorNotificationOverride" type="uci:OperatorNotificationOverrideMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to indicate a notification override. Overridden (or disabled) notifications are suppressed by the system. This message is meant to be issued by an operator and received by the alert system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OperatorRole" type="uci:OperatorRoleMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This messages defines an Operator Role such that it can be referenced within other messages and queried for.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpLine" type="uci:OpLineMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents an operational "line" which may be taken into consideration by Services during their operation or used for situational awareness.  In practice, operational lines can be geometric lines, areas or in some cases volumes.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpNotification" type="uci:OpNotificationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides notification of interactions of assets with previously defined zones, volumes, lines, points and other operational constraints.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpPoint" type="uci:OpPointMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a special class of points which may be taken into consideration by Services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpRouting" type="uci:OpRoutingMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents routing constraints which may be taken into consideration by Systems and Services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpVolume" type="uci:OpVolumeMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a 3-dimensional zone which may be taken into consideration by services during their operation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OpZone" type="uci:OpZoneMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a zone which may be taken into consideration by Services during their operation. Its primary use is airspace coordination.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlan" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies planned activities for a System with an OrbitPlan.  An "Orbit Activity" is any *Capability-related activity planned in context of the kinematic OrbitPlan of the System.  Activities are the planned implementation details of Effects, Actions, Tasks, Capability Commands, etc.  Orbit activity planning can be done manually by operators, by an activity planning service or a combination.  This message decouples activity planning from allocation, kinematic and other types of planning; the OrbitActivityPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on activity planning.
An OrbitActivityPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example an OrbitActivityPlan from a previous round of planning could be an input to an activity planning service that adds additional planned activities to it.
A single System can have multiple independent OrbitActivityPlans for different Task, Capability, sensor, etc. types; that are developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture; at different times then independently updated and approved.  Optionally, all types of activities for a System can be included in a single OrbitActivityPlan.
An OrbitActivityPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  An OrbitActivityPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple OrbitActivityPlans and *Plans in general.  An OrbitActivityPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an OrbitActivityPlan are given in other OrbitActivityPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates state of approval for a OrbitActivityPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanCommand" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes an OrbitActivityPlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  The message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, an OrbitActivityPlan could be constrained by an existing kinematic OrbitPlan, a previous OrbitActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be for added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an OrbitActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the OrbitActivityPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of an OrbitActivityPlan  may occur autonomously, without an OrbitActivityPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the OrbitActivityPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in an execution state machine for one or more current and/or previous OrbitActivityPlans both as a whole and for individual Activities.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via the MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanStatus" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in a planning and plan validation state machine for a OrbitActivityPlan.  Planning and validation may occur autonomously or be commanded.  In the former case, there is no command status message indicating the resulting state.  This message provides current status regardless of how status changes were triggered.  This message is not to be used to status individual actions within the OrbitActivityPlan, only the whole plan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanValidation" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing OrbitActivityPlan.  It includes an option to modify the Plan to make it valid if it is found to be invalid.  A OrbitActivityPlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, space weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing OrbitActivityPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate an OrbitActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalDebrisEstimate" type="uci:OrbitalDebrisEstimateMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the details of an orbital debris assessment. Orbital debris is the result of one or more resident space objects (RSO) physically breaking into pieces. An orbital debris estimate may or may not be the result of a specific OrbitalDebrisEstimateRequest; services could provide continuous assessment of the space domain and automatically provide debris information. This message may either embed the results of the estimate directly, or reference a file containing the results. The estimate message provides details characterizing the debris cloud.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalDebrisEstimateRequest" type="uci:OrbitalDebrisEstimateRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the creation of a debris estimate for the defined objects. A request type can either refer to a conjunction assessment, two specific objects, or the breakup of a single object. The request also specifies the form the estimate may take; the estimate is either provided as a file or detailed within the OrbitalDebrisEstimate.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalDebrisEstimateRequestStatus" type="uci:OrbitalDebrisEstimateRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of an OrbitalDebrisEstimateRequest received by the applicable C2 service. Upon completion of the assessment, the OrbitalDebrisEstimateID should be provided.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalSurveillanceActivity" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message describes the current activities being executed by an OrbitalSurveillance capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalSurveillanceCapability" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates that a subsystem is capable of performing an Orbital Surveillance activity. Orbital Surveillance involves the use of sensors of various different phenomenologies to search for and/or collect information on a target, multiple targets or region of space to meet a specific Information Objective.  The focus of an Orbital Surveillance activity (identified under the specification of Target) can be location based, object based, or zone based.
Examples of information objectives achieved through sensor collects include achieving or maintaining a certain level of orbit accuracy, verifying that there are no unknown objects in a specific region of space or with certain orbital parameters, determining the structural elements of a target down to a specified resolution, determining the current operational configuration of a target, or something as specific as providing a baseline characterization of a target in a specific phenomenology to be used for future change-detection.
This message advertises a subsystem's mostly static characteristics for a mission-ready Orbital Surveillance Capability to other systems, which enables proper commanding of the capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalSurveillanceCapabilityStatus" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Orbital Surveillance capabilities available to the system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalSurveillanceSensorActivity" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceSensorActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message describes the current activities being executed by an OrbitalSurveillanceSensor capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalSurveillanceSensorCapability" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceSensorCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates that a subsystem is capable of performing an Orbital Surveillance Sensor activity. Orbital Surveillance Sensor involves the use of a sensor of a given phenomenology to search for and/or collect information on a target, multiple targets or region of space to meet one or more Information Objectives.  The focus of an Orbital Surveillance Sensor activity (identified under the specification of Target) can be orbital targets or powered flight targets.
    Examples of information objectives achieved through sensor collects include achieving or maintaining a certain level of orbit accuracy, verifying that there are no unknown objects in a specific region of space or with certain orbital parameters, determining the structural elements of a target down to a specified resolution, determining the current operational configuration of a target, or something as specific as providing a baseline characterization of a target in a specific phenomenology to be used for future change-detection.
    This message advertises a subsystem's mostly static characteristics for a mission-ready Orbital Surveillance Sensor Capability to other systems, which enables proper commanding of the capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitalSurveillanceSensorCapabilityStatus" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceSensorCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Orbital Surveillance Sensor capabilities available to the system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitChangeActivity" type="uci:OrbitChangeActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is actively doing. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked; it is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for a Capability category (ESM, EA, etc.). In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability.  For the space domain, only Operational Level messages are provided.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitChangeCapability" type="uci:OrbitChangeCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Orbit Change category of UCI Capability.  It "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Orbit Change Capability so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations and for the Orbit Change Capability, it could be a proximity operation, a rendezvous or similar change.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitChangeCapabilityStatus" type="uci:OrbitChangeCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Orbit Change capabilities available to the system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitConjunctionAssessment" type="uci:OrbitConjunctionAssessmentMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the details of an Orbit Conjunction Assessment. An Orbit  Conjunction can take several forms, including: collisions, near misses, RF interference and laser interference. An Orbit Conjunction Assessment may or may not be the result of a specific OrbitConjunctionAssessmentRequest; services could provide continuous assessment of the space domain and automatically provide conjunction information upon detection. A single message may report multiple conjunctions. Each Conjunction element only reports the interaction between two objects; if the assessment includes conjunctions between more than two objects, they must be organized into pairs.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitConjunctionAssessmentRequest" type="uci:OrbitConjunctionAssessmentRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests that a conjunction assessment be conducted using the defined objects. A request type can be as simple as comparing two distinct objects (SPECIFIED_ONLY), and as complex as assessing all known RSOs against all known RSOs (ALL_ON_ALL). The message may specify objects (e.g. a satellite), a Working SOB (e.g. set of space objects), and/or a particular Debris Cloud.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitConjunctionAssessmentRequestStatus" type="uci:OrbitConjunctionAssessmentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of an OrbitConjunctionAssessmentRequest received by the applicable C2 service. Upon completion of the assessment, the OrbitConjunctionAssessmentID should be provided.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitMetrics" type="uci:OrbitMetricsMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides metrics on predicted performance details of an Orbit Plan and/or corresponding Orbit Action Plan. Metrics may be provided for any combination of parts for the Orbit Plan or Orbit Action Plan. Metrics include endurance and sub-categories include propulsion endurance, maneuverability endurance, and operational endurance. This message is intended for use prior to execution of a plan; for actual results of the plan, systems should use those status and activity messages produced during execution to derive metrics.
The intent of the *Plan Metrics messages are to provide calculated metrics that are based on existing plan input data. This is to continue the current paradigm of how metrics were represented in existing/previous MissionPlan and other sub plans. There is a distinction between the *Plan Metrics and the Assessment message, in that Assessment gives much more specific data and can consider other supporting systems and their capabilities in the assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitMetricsRequest" type="uci:OrbitMetricsRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the generation of Orbit metrics for a specific orbit plan and, optionally, its associated orbit action plan and/or action parts. For details on what metrics are provided, see OrbitMetrics.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitMetricsRequestStatus" type="uci:OrbitMetricsRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a OrbitMetricsRequest received by the applicable C2 system. In the event that metric reporting was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the process was unsuccessful.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitModificationRequest" type="uci:OrbitModificationRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests an orbit modification, which allows requests for atomic changes to an Orbit plan outside of normal mission/orbit planning, where transmission of whole plans could be an issue. The status of each request is reported in the OrbitModificationRequestStatus message. The result of a completed request is a new OrbitPlan message incorporating the modifications and incrementing the OrbitVersionID.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitModificationRequestStatus" type="uci:OrbitModificationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This is a message to report status on an OrbitModificationRequest. A successful modification request should result in a new OrbitPlan message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlan" type="uci:OrbitPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the planned kinematic details of a particular System's orbit; "orbit" is defined as a series of orbital element sets, ephemeris, vector covariance and/or explicit maneuvers between them.  The message is intended for Systems such as spacecraft whose kinematic plans are best represented in inertial/orbital frames.  Orbit planning can be done manually by operators, by a route planning service or a combination.  This message decouples kinematic planning from allocation, action and other types of planning; the kinematic OrbitPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on route planning.
An OrbitPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example a coarse OrbitPlan created by the top System in a hierarchical System of System for overall Mission orchestration and deconfliction could be an input to a orbit planning service that the produces a detailed, executable OrbitPlan for a specific subordinate System.
A single System can have multiple independent OrbitPlans for different orbit planning considerations such as orbit station keeping, orbital maneuvers and attitude adjustment.  The different planning considerations can be handled by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture at different times then independently updated and approved.  Optionally, all kinematic orbit planning considerations for a System can be included in a single OrbitPlan.
An OrbitPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  An OrbitPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple OrbitPlans and *Plans in general.  An OrbitPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an OrbitPlan are given in other OrbitPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:OrbitPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for a OrbitPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanCommand" type="uci:OrbitPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes an OrbitPlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  The message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:
1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, an OrbitPlan could be constrained by a previous OrbitPlan, a previous OrbitActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.
2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.
3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.
4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:OrbitPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update an OrbitPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the OrbitPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of an OrbitPlan may occur autonomously, without an OrbitPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the RoutePlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:OrbitPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more OrbitPlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanStatus" type="uci:OrbitPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in a planning and plan validation state machine for an OrbitPlan.  Planning and validation may occur autonomously or be commanded.  In the former case, there is no command status message indicating the resulting state.  This message provides current status regardless of how status changes were triggered.  This message is not to be used to status individual actions within the OrbitPlan, only the whole plan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanValidation" type="uci:OrbitPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the OrbitPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:OrbitPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing OrbitPlan.  It includes an option to modify the Plan to make it valid if it is found to be invalid.  A OrbitPlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing OrbitPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrbitPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:OrbitPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate an OrbitPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="OrderOfBattle" type="uci:OrderOfBattleMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Representation of an Order of Battle. Order of Battle is a representation of organizational structure defined as a unit and the sites and equipment that the unit is associated with. Order of Battle information provide situational awareness as to units and the unit's primary purpose as well as the associated location or locations and equipment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Package" type="uci:PackageMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the existence of multi-System "packages" also known as "force packages", "strike packages", swarms, etc.  A Package is defined as a collection of Systems and their Capabilities that perform in concert to achieve results they can't achieve individually. For a given System in a Package, only those Capabilities defined in the message are included in the Package.  System Capabilities not included in the Package will behave as if the System was not part of the Package. Any functional change in the behavior of Systems and Capabilities due to inclusion in a Package may not be discoverable via UCI. Such differences are typically defined outside of the UCI schema. This message simply indicates the existence and configuration of the Package and is reported by a single lead or surrogate lead of the Package.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PackageStatus" type="uci:PackageStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status and configuration of a multi-System "package" (see PackageDefintion message for more details). Package status is intended to be reported by a single lead or surrogate lead of the package.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanModificationRequest" type="uci:PlanModificationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to modify a Plan or set of Plans.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanModificationRequestStatus" type="uci:PlanModificationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a PlanModificationRequest received by the applicable C2 system. In the event that the modification was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the modification was unsuccessful. If successful, a new Plan message or messages should be sent out.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanningFunction" type="uci:PlanningFunctionMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates all supported planning-related functions and interfaces of a System.  The closely related PlanningFunctionStatus message indicates how planning-related functions and interfaces of the System are currently configured.  Planning functions indicated here can be performed by human operators and/or machines/Services.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanningFunctionSettingsCommand" type="uci:PlanningFunctionSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message changes the configuration of planning-related functions and interfaces of a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanningFunctionSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:PlanningFunctionSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides transactional status for the PlanningFunctionsSettingsCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanningFunctionStatus" type="uci:PlanningFunctionStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the configuration/status of currently enabled planning-related functions and interfaces of a System.  The closely related PlanningFunction message indicates all supported planning-related functions and interfaces.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanScores" type="uci:PlanScoresMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides plan scoring against a set of operational constraints. Successful scoring requires that at least one of the provided plans utilized an OpConstraint Input; the score provided in PlanScores represents how well the produced plan conforms to the inputs. The specific performance requirements which constitute a 0 and 1 are defined by the scoring service. The message may either report the operational constraint scores of a single plan, or it may report an overall score of an operational constraint for a grouping of plans. If multiple plans require individual scoring then PlanScores messages are produced for each plan. Plan scores may or may not be produced as a result of a PlanScoresRequest message; some systems might produce score information automatically as part of their planning process.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanScoresRequest" type="uci:PlanScoresRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests scoring plans against the specified operational constraints. Request can either request scores for a single plan, or it may request an overall score of a group of plans. If multiple plans require individual scoring, a request must be made for each plan. For details about what scores represent, see PlanScores.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PlanScoresRequestStatus" type="uci:PlanScoresRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a PlanScoresRequest received by the applicable C2 system. In the event that the scoring was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the scoring was unsuccessful.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_Activity" type="uci:PO_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked; it is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for a Capability category (ESM, EA, etc.). This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the PO Capability of a Subsystem of a System. In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (PO_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work PO, 4) PO scheduled for the future and 5) PO that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons. A single PO_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the PO_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_Capability" type="uci:PO_CapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the PO (Passive Optical which includes search and track) category of UCI Capability. This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready PO Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others. Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages. Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform. Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:PO_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the PO capabilities available to the system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_Command" type="uci:PO_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the PO (Passive Optical which includes search and track) form of the general UCI Capability Command. It is used to manually invoke a Passive Optical Capability to perform a new PO Activity or change an existing interactive PO Activity. It differs from a PO Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan. Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages. Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them. However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema. Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_CommandStatus" type="uci:PO_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a PO_Command. This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected. This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled. The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the PO_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_SettingsCommand" type="uci:PO_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the PO Capabilities of a Subsystem. [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command. Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PO_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:PO_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of its corresponding PO_SettingsCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PositionReport" type="uci:PositionReportMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the location and speed of a System or Resource.  It is analogous to a simplified MIL-STD-6016 PPLI message.  It is one of the "station-keeping" series of messages reported by most UCI Systems.  The station-keeping series includes PositionReport, SystemStatus, NavigationReport, [Capability]Status and [Capability]Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PositionReportDetailed" type="uci:PositionReportDetailedMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the location, speed, and direction of a System or Resource. It is not one of the "station-keeping" series of messages, but is designed to provide positional information at a much higher frequency than PositionReport, and allows for multiple redundant systems to provide reports in the same message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PrioritizationList" type="uci:PrioritizationListMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a list of battlespace objects or actors, prioritized for an enumerated purpose or perspective.  It establishes an order/priority by which objects should be addressed/processed.  This message has a spectrum of possible uses from the very broad focused to the very narrowly focused.  For example, it could be used broadly to establish the priority of threats/targets as general guidance to all of the Systems in a System of Systems, agnostic of their individual Missions, Capabilities or Services.   Alternatively, it could be very narrowly focused to establish a different priority of threats/targets for each phase of F2T2EA, filtered/tailored by System, Service, time or space.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductClassificationPlan" type="uci:ProductClassificationPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the default system rules for determining at which classification a product should be labeled.  Classification labeling of a products can happen in 3 ways.
    
1.) Classification is specified as part of the collection task.  This method is used to tell the sensor subsystem to label the product with classification as part of product creation.  When this method is used, the Product Classification Plan can be used to determine the classification of the collection task based on the filter criteria such as zone or time.

2.) The operator creates a Product Classification Task to perform post-collect, post-download classification (or reclassification) of a product.  With this method, the operator can choose to classify based on the Product Classification Plan or can override the plan by specifying a classification.

3.) The classification of a product generated from product processing does not directly use this plan to determine classification.  The default behavior would be for the processing function to classify the new product with the highest classification of the new product's parent products.  The operator has the ability to override this default behavior using the ProcessingTask and ProcessingRequest messages.  If any post-creation classification/reclassification of processed products is desired, method 2 described above should be utilized.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductClassificationReport" type="uci:ProductClassificationReportMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a report of the classification of a product.  It has 2 primary uses:
1.)  To report (via publish/broadcast) the state of a product classification.  This is primarily for operator SA and is extremely useful for providing insight into dynamic classification changes of streaming products.  For example, a change in classification of an EOIR stream resulting from a Product Classification Plan zone transition.

2.)  For persistence of state to support initialization and recovery of a service that is applying security labels to products.  This allows for a service to retrieve the classification label of a product upon initialization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductClassificationRequest" type="uci:ProductClassificationRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message has 2 uses.
Message use 1:

This message is a request that a product be classified at a particular level.

A Product Classification Request directly invokes classification service functionality to classify/reclassify a product.  Execution of the classification request would result in a Product Classification Request Status followed by a Product Classification Report.

There are 2 ways this message can be generated.  1.) By the operator to command immediate reclassification of a product.  2.) Automated by a service as a result of execution of a Classification Task.

When specifying a Classification Request, the user can choose to utilize the Product Classification Plan to determine classification or can override the plan by specifying a classification.

If automatically generated as a result of Classification Task execution, the classification requirements would be populated with those specified in the Classification Task.  This allows the default plan to be utilized or overridden on a task by task basis.

Message use 2:

This message requests that a classification label is generated per the Product Classification Plan and returned in the Product Classification Request Status.  When the message is used in this way, the classification should be omitted from the Classification Requirements.  This message use allows for a service decomposition where the service applying the classification label is separate from the service that determines classification based on the classification plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductClassificationRequestStatus" type="uci:ProductClassificationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted ProductClassificationRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductClassificationTask" type="uci:ProductClassificationTaskMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a task for a product to be classified at a particular level.  Classification Tasks do not directly invoke functionality.  They are utilized to schedule post-collection and post-download classification/reclassification of products.  As a result, it is expected that a Product Classification Task would typically specify a download and/or collection task as a preceding dependent task.  Execution of a Classification Task would result in issue of a Classification Request to invoke classification service functionality.  When specifying a Classification Task, the user can choose to utilize the Product Classification Plan to determine classification or can override the plan by specifying a classification.  This allows the default plan to be overridden on a Task by Task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductClassificationTaskStatus" type="uci:ProductClassificationTaskStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of a Classification Task.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationConfiguration" type="uci:ProductDisseminationConfigurationMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message can be used for static configuration of services that route data and streams.  For example, configuration of a MLS guard (input and output ports) or configuration of a service that processes streams such as video.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationDestination" type="uci:ProductDisseminationDestinationMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a destination that products can be disseminated to and provides a user readable alias to that destination.  A destination does not imply that a product is actually being disseminated to the destination.  It is only used to describe the attributes of the dissemination destination.  Once created, it can be referenced by the Product Dissemination Plan, Product Dissemination Task, and Product Dissemination Request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationPlan" type="uci:ProductDisseminationPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines a system's default dissemination destinations of products by type.  It contains subplans which allow destinations to be established based on source, time, zones, and classification among other filter criteria.  Requests for the dissemination destination to archive and/or downgrade products are included.  ProductDisseminationPlans specify what default destinations to push products to in lieu of subscriptions, primarily for dissemination of products to non-UCI systems.

The Product Dissemination Plan can be overridden by the operator utilizing the Product Dissemination Task and/or Product Dissemination Request by inclusion of a destination.  A Dissemination Request with destination omitted implies a dissemination to a destination determined by the Dissemination Plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationReport" type="uci:ProductDisseminationReportMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a report of the dissemination of a product.  It has 2 primary uses:
1.)  To report (via publish/broadcast) the state of product dissemination.  This is primarily for operator SA and is extremely useful for providing insight into dynamic dissemination destination changes of streaming products.  For example, a change in dissemination destination of an EOIR stream resulting from a Product Dissemination Plan zone transition.

2.)  For persistence of state to support initialization and recovery of a service that is performing product dissemination.  This allows for a service to retrieve the dissemination destination of a product upon initialization.  This is primarily applicable to streaming products that are continuously streaming to a dissemination destination.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationRequest" type="uci:ProductDisseminationRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request that a product be disseminated to one or more destinations.
A Product Dissemination Request directly invokes dissemination service functionality to disseminate a product.  Execution of the dissemination request would result in a Product Dissemination Request Status followed by a Product Dissemination Report.

There are 2 ways this message can be generated.  1.) By the operator to command immediate dissemination of a product.  2.) Automated by a service as a result of execution of a Dissemination Task.

When specifying a Dissemination Request, the user can choose to utilize the Product Dissemination Plan to determine destination or can override the plan by specifying a destination.

If automatically generated as a result of Dissemination Task execution, the Dissemination requirements would be populated with those specified in the Dissemination Task.  This allows the default plan to be utilized or overridden on a task by task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationRequestStatus" type="uci:ProductDisseminationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted ProductDisseminationRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationTask" type="uci:ProductDisseminationTaskMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a task for a product to be disseminated to one or more destinations.
Dissemination Tasks do not directly invoke functionality.  They are utilized to schedule post-collection, post-download, and post-processing dissemination of products.  As a result, it is expected that a Product Dissemination Task would typically specify a download and/or processing task as a preceding dependent task.

Execution of a Dissemination Task would result in issue of a Dissemination Request to invoke dissemination service functionality.

When specifying a Dissemination Task, the user can choose to utilize the Product Dissemination Plan to determine the dissemination destination or can override the plan by specifying a destination.  This allows the default plan to be overridden on a task by task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDisseminationTaskStatus" type="uci:ProductDisseminationTaskStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the status of a Dissemination Task.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDownloadPlan" type="uci:ProductDownloadPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current system rules for determining which products are eligible for automatic download.
Downloads can be manually planned and requested on a task by task basis utilizing the Download Task and Download Request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDownloadReport" type="uci:ProductDownloadReportMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report (via publish/broadcast) that a product has been downloaded.  It is primarily for operator SA.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDownloadRequest" type="uci:ProductDownloadRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request that a file or files to be downloaded.  In some cases, a product may reside on a system in a location that is not reachable by other UCI services via a network URI.  This message is intended to request that a product be downloaded to a location where other services can reach it via a network URI.  An example of this is a product that has been stored on-board a vehicle but has not been downloaded.

A Product Download Request directly invokes functionality to download a product.  Execution of the download request would result in a Product Download Request Status followed by a Product Download Report.

There are 2 ways this message can be generated.  1.) By the operator to command immediate download of a product.  2.) Automated by a service as a result of execution of a Download Task.

If automatically generated as a result of Download Task execution, the download requirements would be populated with those specified in the Download Task.  This allows the default plan to be overridden on a task by task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDownloadRequestStatus" type="uci:ProductDownloadRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted ProductDownloadRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDownloadTask" type="uci:ProductDownloadTaskMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a task that a file or files is to be downloaded.
Download Tasks do not directly invoke functionality.  They are utilized to schedule post-collection download of products.  As a result, it is expected that a Product Download Task would typically specify a collection task as a preceding dependent task.

Execution of a Download Task would result in issue of a Download Request to invoke download service functionality.

This message can be used to override the ProductDownloadPlan on a task by task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductDownloadTaskStatus" type="uci:ProductDownloadTaskStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a task that a file or files is to be downloaded.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductLocation" type="uci:ProductLocationMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the network location of a product.  See the annotations for ProductMetadata for an explanation of how it works together with this message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductMetadata" type="uci:ProductMetadataMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies UCI relevant metadata for a Product.  The term "Product" generally refers to an output of a Capability (SAR, PO, etc.) whose content and format are specified outside of the UCI standard.  A Product could be a file, digital stream, analog video, etc.  ProductMetadata and ProductLocation shouldn't be used for things that aren't Capability outputs.   It does not contain actual products nor does it specify their network location.  Network location is published in the ProductLocation message.  "Metadata" includes a summary of geospatial, time, classification, file name, file format and other characteristics of the product.  It also includes optional Task and/or [Capability]Command  specific metadata that link the product to the task or command that created it.  The optional Thumbnail element can be used in conjunction with the ProductLocation message to preview the product.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingFunction" type="uci:ProductProcessingFunctionMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to advertise the ability to process products. It specifies what type of inputs can be processed, limitations on the type of processing, and the available output products.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingFunctionStatus" type="uci:ProductProcessingFunctionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the product processing functions available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each function is currently available.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingPlan" type="uci:ProductProcessingPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the default system rules for determining the type of post-collect processing and processing parameters to apply to specific product types.
Default processing types and parameters can be overridden using the Processing Task and Processing Request messages.  A Processing Request with processing type and parameter omitted implies process via the Product processing Plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingReport" type="uci:ProductProcessingReportMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a report of the processing of a product.  It has 2 primary uses:
1.)  To report (via publish/broadcast) the state of product processing.  This is primarily for operator SA and is extremely useful for providing insight into dynamic processing type and parameter changes of streaming products.  For example, a change in processing of an EOIR stream resulting from a Product Processing Plan zone transition.

2.)  For persistence of state to support initialization and recovery of a service that is performing product processing.  This allows for a service to retrieve the processing type and parameters of a product upon initialization.  This is primarily applicable to streaming products that are continuously streaming and the processing type is continuously applied.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingRequest" type="uci:ProductProcessingRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a request that a new product be created by processing existing products or products that will be collected.
A Product Processing Request directly invokes processing service functionality to process a product.  Execution of the processing request would result in a Product Processing Request Status followed by a Product Processing Report.

There are 2 ways this message can be generated.  1.) By the operator to command immediate processing of a product.  2.) Automated by a service as a result of execution of a Processing Task.

When specifying a Processing Request, the user can choose to utilize the Product Processing Plan to determine processing type and parameters or can override the plan by specifying processing type and parameters.

If automatically generated as a result of Processing Task execution, the processing requirements would be populated with those specified in the Processing Task.  This allows the default plan to be utilized or overridden on a task by task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingRequestStatus" type="uci:ProductProcessingRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a previously submitted ProductProcessingRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingTask" type="uci:ProductProcessingTaskMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a task that a new product be created by processing existing products or products that will be created in the future.
Processing Tasks do not directly invoke functionality.  They are utilized to schedule post-collection and post-download processing of products.  As a result, it is expected that a Product Processing Task would typically specify a download and/or collection task as a preceding dependent task.

Execution of a Processing Task would result in issue of a Processing Request to invoke processing service functionality.

When specifying a Processing Task, the user can choose to utilize the Product Processing Plan to determine processing type or can override the plan by specifying processing type.  This allows the default plan to be overridden on a task by task basis.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ProductProcessingTaskStatus" type="uci:ProductProcessingTaskStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status for a task that a new product be created by processing existing products or products that will be collected.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PropagatorSettings" type="uci:PropagatorSettingsMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the set of propagator settings to be used when propagating a resident space object (RSO). A propagator consists of a force model and a numerical integration model. Models can be customized to meet the needs of the mission.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PropagatorSettingsDataRequest" type="uci:PropagatorSettingsDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a request of the set of propagator settings to be used when propagating a resident space object (RSO).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PropagatorSettingsDataRequestStatus" type="uci:PropagatorSettingsDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is generated in response to a PropagatorSettingsDataRequest. It provides the state of the request and allows services to return details of the force model and a numerical integration model.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="PulseData" type="uci:PulseDataMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a signals intelligence pulse stream.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="QueryDataRequest" type="uci:QueryDataRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a query for a new message and/or a filtered set of message or messages.  It is intended to support **service/application-level** queries for messages; it is not intended to replace message retry, persistence, etc. capabilities in the middleware and/or transport level or be used in lieu of the ResendDataRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="QueryDataRequestStatus" type="uci:QueryDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the results of a QueryDataRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterActivity" type="uci:RadarAltimeterActivityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the RadarAltimeter Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (RadarAltimeterCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work RadarAltimeter, 4) RadarAltimeter scheduled for the future and 5) RadarAltimeter that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single RadarAltimeterCommand or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the RadarAltimeterActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterCapability" type="uci:RadarAltimeterCapabilityMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the RadarAltimeter category of UCI Capability.  The RadarAltimeter measures platform height above terrain and vertical closing velocity through active radar detection.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready RadarAltimeter Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterCapabilityStatus" type="uci:RadarAltimeterCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the RadarAltimeter capabilities.   This includes whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterCommand" type="uci:RadarAltimeterCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the RadarAltimeter form of the general UCI Capability Command. It is used to manually invoke a RadarAltimeter Capability to perform a new RadarAltimeter Activity or change an existing interactive RadarAltimeter Activity. It is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan. Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages. Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of RadarAltimeterCommands including Capability precedence, RadarAltimeterCommand rank, and RadarAltimeterActivity rank.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterCommandStatus" type="uci:RadarAltimeterCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an RadarAltimeterCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity(ies) that result from the command will report status via the RadarAltimeterActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterReport" type="uci:RadarAltimeterReportMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a RadarAltimeter report from a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterSettingsCommand" type="uci:RadarAltimeterSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the RadarAltimeter Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered pre-settings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RadarAltimeterSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:RadarAltimeterSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the acknowledgement and/or acceptance of an RadarAltimeter settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RDMA_Initialize" type="uci:RDMA_InitializeMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message instructs the recipient (subscriber) that an RDMA is ready to be established.  The subscriber should use the information in this message to complete an RDMA connection using the libfabrics API/library.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RDMA_InitializeSetup" type="uci:RDMA_InitializeSetupMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the server open an RDMA connection using the supplied information.  The server should respond with an RDMA_Initialize message to confirm RDMA connection details and signal the RDMA connection is ready for connection after initializing the connection using the libfabrics API/library.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ReferenceActivity" type="uci:ReferenceActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Reference Capability of a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ReferenceCapability" type="uci:ReferenceCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Reference category of UCI Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ReferenceCapabilityStatus" type="uci:ReferenceCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Reference capabilities available to the system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ReferenceFrame" type="uci:ReferenceFrameMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to send reference frame settings position, attitude, and velocity.  This message could be used to publish reference frame settings to the air vehicle to support relative navigation.  It can also be used to provide a reference frame for OpPoints, OpLines or OpZones that are relative to a moving object.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelActivity" type="uci:RefuelActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Refuel Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (TankingCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work Tanking, 4) Tanking scheduled for the future and 5) Tanking that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single TankingCommand or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the TankingActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelCapability" type="uci:RefuelCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Refueling category of UCI Capability.  Refueling refers to Aerial Refueling where a tanker gives gas to a receiver.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Tanking Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelCapabilityStatus" type="uci:RefuelCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Tanking capabilities.   This includes whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states,  etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelCommand" type="uci:RefuelCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Refueling form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a Refuel Capability to perform a new Refuel Activity or change an existing interactive Refuel Activity.  It differs from a Refuel Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelCommandStatus" type="uci:RefuelCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a RefuelCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will be statused via the RefuelActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelSettingsCommand" type="uci:RefuelSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the Refuel Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RefuelSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:RefuelSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of a Refuel Settings Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RelationshipDesignation" type="uci:RelationshipDesignationMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message to represent one of the various types of relationships that occur in the course of system operations - Pairings (Associations or Couplings), Engagements, and Threat Warnings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementMetrics" type="uci:RequirementMetricsMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides metrics for [Requirement]Plan, generally metrics related to allocation of Requirements to Systems.  This message is intended for use prior to execution of a plan; for actual results of the plan, systems should use those status and activity messages produced during execution to derive metrics.
The intent of the *Plan Metrics messages are to provide calculated metrics that are based on existing plan input data. This is to continue the current paradigm of how metrics were represented in existing/previous MissionPlan and other sub plans. There is a distinction between the *Plan Metrics and the Assessment message, in that Assessment gives much more specific data and can consider other supporting systems and their capabilities in the assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementMetricsRequest" type="uci:RequirementMetricsRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the generation of metrics for [Requirement]Plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementMetricsRequestStatus" type="uci:RequirementMetricsRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a RequirementMetricsRequest.  In the event that metric reporting was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the process was unsuccessful.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementOptions" type="uci:RequirementOptionsMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies options for allocation of Requirements to Systems.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementOptionsCommand" type="uci:RequirementOptionsCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to develop/analyze options for allocating Requirements to Systems.  The results are given in the RequirementOptions message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementOptionsCommandStatus" type="uci:RequirementOptionsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a response to the RequirementOptionsCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RequirementSet" type="uci:RequirementSetMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the list of current, active Requirements from a Requirement source.  It provides a mechanism for Requirement creators to freely create and publish Tasks without them being included in a planning cycle initiated elsewhere.  When a Requirement creator has created a coherent set of Requirements that is ready for consideration for planning, the Requirements are summarized in this message and published as an indication to the operator or service that initiates planning to include them in the next planning cycle.  The ManagingSystemID element allows a Requirement creator to assign responsibility to its own System or other Systems.  For example, a System could assign planning responsibility for a set of Requirements to a sibling or subordinate System.  When a planning cycle occurs and a Requirement can't be allocated this message/object maintains the current, active To-Do list regardless of planning results from each Requirement source's perspective without relying on MissionPlan, MissionPlanCommand and Requirement states.  Each source of this message is expected to monitor for RequirementCancel requests and removed Requirements accordingly; canceled Requirements should be removed from the RequirementSet list in order to exclude them from the next planning cycle.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResendDataRequest" type="uci:ResendDataRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a request to resend a message or messages packaged within a ResendRequestStatus.  It is intended to support **service/application-level** bootstrapping of newly joining or recovering systems/services; it is not intended to replace similar capabilities at the middleware and/or transport level.  Recipients of the request are generally expected to respond by resending recent instances of the message type that are still currently relevant; an exhaustive resend of all previous instances is not necessarily expected.  This message is not intended to be used for, or result in, messages being sent for the first time.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResendDataRequestStatus" type="uci:ResendDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a response to a ResendRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Response" type="uci:ResponseMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines a Response to triggering event or events.  It is effectively a Requirement to "be ready to" 1) instantiate and plan a Requirement (Effect, Action, Task or [Capability]Command tailored to the triggering even, 2) activate a previously created *Plan, 3) issue an alert, 4) do nothing.  Responsibility for the Response can be allocated/assigned to a System via a ResponsePlan that is part of a MissionPlan.  In other words, ResponsePlans allow Responses to be delegated across a SoS.  Activating a ResponsePlan activates monitoring of the triggering events for each of its Responses.  A Response is executing whenever its triggering events are being monitored.  An executing Response causes no changes to the Mission unless a triggering event occurs.  When a triggering event occurs, the options within the Response are evaluated, potentially leading to selection and instantiation of one or options within the Response.

***********************
For Planned C2, evaluating a Response option based on Requirements or *Plans generally means evaluating whole *Plans.  To evaluate a Response option it is generally necessary to create, update, validate, assess and/or score *Plans that include it.  Selecting and activating a Response option generally means approving and activating a *Plan that includes it.
***********************

A Response can continue to execute (triggering events can continue to be monitored) after a Response option has been initiated.  An instantiation of a Response option is detailed in the ResponseActivity message.  Response can be manually initiated via the ResponseCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseActivity" type="uci:ResponseActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Response Capability of a System.  Response Activities start at the time when monitoring for the triggering event starts.  A given trigger can occur multiple times in the course of a Mission.  Therefore a Response Activity can indicate multiple current and past trigger instances.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseCancelCommand" type="uci:ResponseCancelCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to cancel a Response which has been allocated and planned.  A planning input such as Effect, Response, Task and DMPI can't easily be canceled or removed from the System due to race conditions between their various users and publishers, inevitability of the associated activity, etc.  Additionally, the operator or service initiating the cancellation might be different than the one that manages RequirementSets and/or issues *PlanCommand, introducing additional race conditions.  In order to avoid race conditions, receive positive feedback on the success of the cancellation and allow canceled messages/objects to remain in the system for historical purposes, an explicit ResponseCancelCommand is necessary.  A ResponseCancelCommand shall result in the Response being canceled unless execution was already inevitable.  If the Response was inevitable and started executing but can be stopped (such as a long collection Response) it shall cancel as soon as possible.  ResponseCancelCommand shall result in managers of RequirementSets to remove the associated Response, thereby removing the Response from the next planning cycle.  Services other than those that manage RequirementSets and execute activities aren't required to handle ResponseCancelCommand.  ResponseCancelCommand doesn't necessarily trigger a replan (there isn't a corresponding planning trigger), therefore a System could continue to navigate routes related to the canceled Response, even though the Response won't occur.  A subsequent replan could exclude the Response.  Status or feedback for this message is given through ResponseCancelCommandStatus but the resulting status of the Response is given through ResponseStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseCancelCommandStatus" type="uci:ResponseCancelCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a ResponseCancelCommand and indicates if the Response was canceled and if not why it was not canceled.  The resulting status of the Response is reported in ResponseStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseCapability" type="uci:ResponseCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Response category of UCI Capability.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseCapabilityStatus" type="uci:ResponseCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Response capabilities available to the system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseCommand" type="uci:ResponseCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Response form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseCommandStatus" type="uci:ResponseCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an ResponseCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseExecutionApprovalStatus" type="uci:ResponseExecutionApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the state of approval for execution of a Response.
***For Responses, "execution" only includes the explicit response management action indicated in the Response message; it does not include subsequent creation of *Plans, execution of Requirements, etc. that may or may not follow.  For example, approval for execution of a Response that includes generation of a Strike Task does not approve the instantiated Strike Task.  It only approves monitoring of the associated Response trigger and initial creation/instantiation of the Strike Task when/if the trigger occurs.***

ApprovalPolicy allow an authority to define policies to approve or reject a Response as it enters execution. Approval may or may not be required. If approval is required, there can be multiple approvers. See Approval* messages for additional details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlan" type="uci:ResponsePlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Responses allocated to a System.  Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination.  This message decouples Response allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Response allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on Response allocation.

A ResponsePlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example the ResponsePlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to a response allocation service that the produces a ResponsePlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Responses.

Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the response allocation process and captured in the ResponsePlan to supplement the information in the allocated Responses themselves.

A ResponsePlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  A ResponsePlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple ResponsePlans and *Plans in general.  A ResponsePlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a ResponsePlan are given in other ResponsePlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:ResponsePlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for a ResponsePlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanCommand" type="uci:ResponsePlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to perform a response allocation plan process or function. The ResponsePlan message/object is the output of a response allocation plan process or function.  The ResponsePlan output of one response allocation plan process function could be an input to the next function.  UCI enables "discovery" of the response allocation plan processes and functions of a System via the ControlStatus message. This mechanism for discovery empowers a System performing response allocation planning for subordinate Systems of heterogeneous types and/or service/function/process architecture to selectively use, replace or supplement them.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanCommandStatus" type="uci:ResponsePlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a ResponsePlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the ResponsePlanCommand message.  Creation/update of a ResponsePlan  may occur autonomously, without a ResponsePlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the ResponsePlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:ResponsePlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more ResponsePlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanningStatus" type="uci:ResponsePlanningStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines planning status of a single Response.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanStatus" type="uci:ResponsePlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the status/state of a ResponsePlan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanValidation" type="uci:ResponsePlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the ResponsePlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanValidationCommand" type="uci:ResponsePlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing ResponsePlan.  It includes an option to modify the Plan to make it valid if it is found to be invalid.  A ResponsePlan can become invalid due to changes in Response timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing ResponsePlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponsePlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:ResponsePlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate a ResponsePlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ResponseStatus" type="uci:ResponseStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for a Response.
***For Responses, "execution" only includes the explicit response management action indicated in the Response message; it does not include subsequent creation of *Plans, execution of Requirements, etc. that may or may not follow.  For example, a Response is executing as soon as its triggers are being monitored.  The triggers may never happen even if the Response is executing.  If a trigger happens, the Response continues to execute but is now "instantiated" once, with details given in the ResponseActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ControlCommand" type="uci:RF_ControlCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message controls the RF use of a Capability.  Control is specified in the form of RF profiles (RF_Profile message).  When multiple profiles are specified, they should be logically ANDed; the Capability is allowed to operate in the intersection the profiles.  A given RF_ControlCommand must always express the full set of profiles for the target Capability.  In other words, RF_ControlCommand is not additive/subtractive; each instance wholly supersedes the previous instance for a given Capability, regardless of whether the CommandID or ObjectState changes.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ControlCommandStatus" type="uci:RF_ControlCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response and status to an RF_ControlCommand.  Only the Service which hosts the Capability given in the corresponding RF_ControlCommand should respond with this status message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_Profile" type="uci:RF_ProfileMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A profile of either allowed or denied frequencies.  Multiple profiles may be combined to restrict the RF radiation of a capability or individual command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_Report" type="uci:RF_ReportMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates information about the past, current and/or future RF usage of a Capability.  Use of this message is highly dependent on the performance and RF coordination/deconfliction needs of a given System or System of Systems and is left for specific program related documentation and other System Engineering products to describe.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ResourceAllocation" type="uci:RF_ResourceAllocationMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Used for successful Resource Allocation, Rejection is provided by the RF_ResourceAllocationRequestStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ResourceAllocationRequest" type="uci:RF_ResourceAllocationRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Used with Shared RF Array systems to request a resource allocation from a Resource Manager. If not sent, the Resource manager bases its resource allocation off the Command and Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ResourceAllocationRequestStatus" type="uci:RF_ResourceAllocationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent from the Resource Manager in response to the RF_ResourceAllocationRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ThreadInstanceSetupCommand" type="uci:RF_ThreadInstanceSetupCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Command used by a subsystem (e.g. EA, ESM) or a centralized beam manager to preload controls on allocated resources.  This command configures the controls for the RFDC components and the Antenna subsystem, and assigns a priority to the requested controls.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RF_ThreadInstanceSetupCommandStatus" type="uci:RF_ThreadInstanceSetupCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Command used by Antenna and RFDC subsystems to acknowledge the preloaded controls on allocated resources that were sent by the subsystem (e.g. EA, ESM).</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlan" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies planned activities for a System with a RoutePlan.  A "Route Activity" is any *Capability-related activity planned in context of the kinematic RoutePlan of the System.  Activities are the planned implementation details of Effects, Actions, Tasks, Capability Commands, etc.  Route activity planning can be done manually by operators, by an activity planning service or a combination.  This message decouples activity planning from allocation, kinematic and other types of planning; the RouteActivityPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on activity planning.
A RouteActivityPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example a RouteActivityPlan from a previous round of planning could be an input to an activity planning service that adds additional planned activities to it.

A single System can have multiple independent RouteActivityPlans for different Task, Capability, sensor, etc. types; that are developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture; at different times then independently updated and approved.  Optionally, all types of activities for a System can be included in a single RouteActivityPlan.

A RouteActivityPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  A RouteActivityPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple RouteActivityPlans and *Plans in general.  A RouteActivityPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a RouteActivityPlan are given in other RouteActivityPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates state of approval for a RouteActivityPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanCommand" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes a RouteActivityPlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  The message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:

1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, a RouteActivityPlan could be constrained by an existing kinematic RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.

2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be for added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.

3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.

4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a RouteActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the RouteActivityPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of a RouteActivityPlan  may occur autonomously, without a RouteActivityPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the RouteActivityPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more RouteActivityPlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanStatus" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in a planning and plan validation state machine for a RouteActivityPlan.  Planning and validation may occur autonomously or be commanded.  In the former case, there is no command status message indicating the resulting state.  This message provides current status regardless of how status changes were triggered.  This message is not to be used to status individual actions within the RouteActivityPlan, only the whole plan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanValidation" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the RouteActivityPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing RouteActivityPlan.  It includes an option to modify the Plan to make it valid if it is found to be invalid.  A RouteActivityPlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing RouteActivityPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteActivityPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:RouteActivityPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate a RouteActivityPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteMetrics" type="uci:RouteMetricsMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides metrics on predicted performance details of a route plan and/or corresponding route action plan. Metrics may be provided for any combination of parts for the route or route action plans (e.g. provide metrics for all TASKS in RouteActivityPlan A for the INGRESS and EGRESS parts of RoutePlanA). Metrics categories include endurance (e.g. fuel), expendables (e.g. flares), and Weapons (e.g. missiles). This message is intended for use prior to execution of a plan; for actual results of the plan, systems should use those status and activity messages produced during execution to derive metrics.

The intent of the *Plan Metrics messages are to provide calculated metrics that are based on existing plan input data. This is to continue the current paradigm of how metrics were represented in existing/previous MissionPlan and other sub plans. There is a distinction between the *Plan Metrics and the Assessment message, in that Assessment gives much more specific data and can consider other supporting systems and their capabilities in the assessment.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteMetricsRequest" type="uci:RouteMetricsRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the generation of route metrics for a specific route plan and, optionally, its associated route action plan and/or action parts. For details on what metrics are provided, see RouteMetrics.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteMetricsRequestStatus" type="uci:RouteMetricsRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a RouteMetricsRequest received by the applicable C2 system. In the event that metric reporting was incomplete, this message should provide details on why the process was unsuccessful.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteModificationRequest" type="uci:RouteModificationRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This is a message to request a Route modification, which allows requests for atomic changes to a Route plan outside of normal mission/orbit planning, where transmission of whole plans could be an issue. The status of each request is reported in the RouteModificationRequestStatus message. The result of a completed request is a new RoutePlan message incorporating the modifications and incrementing the RouteVersionID.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RouteModificationRequestStatus" type="uci:RouteModificationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This is a message to report status on a RouteModificationRequest. A successful modification request should result in a new RoutePlan message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlan" type="uci:RoutePlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the planned kinematic route of a System.  "Route" is defined as a series of WGS-84 waypoints and is intended for Systems such as aircraft and ground vehicles whose kinematic plans are best represented in an Earth-centered rotational frame.  Route planning can be done manually by operators, by a route planning service or a combination.  This message decouples kinematic planning from allocation, action and other types of planning; the kinematic RoutePlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on route planning.

A RoutePlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example a coarse RoutePlan created by the top System in a hierarchical System of System for overall Mission orchestration and deconfliction could be an input to a route planning service that the produces a detailed, flyable RoutePlan for a specific subordinate System.

A single System can have multiple independent RoutePlans for different types of planned routes such as primary, ingress, egress and contingency.  The different types of routes can be developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture at different times then independently updated and approved.  Optionally, all types of routes for a System can be included in a single RoutePlan.

A RoutePlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  A RoutePlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple RoutePlans and *Plans in general.  A RoutePlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a RoutePlan are given in other RoutePlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:RoutePlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for a RoutePlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanCommand" type="uci:RoutePlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message invokes a RoutePlan planning process.  Each planning process is declared with a unique ID in the ControlStatus message; that ID is used in this message to invoke the process.  The message includes many inputs to the planning process but many others may come from a variety of other messages.  A few examples of the many inputs include:

1. Options for constraining the planning function in a variety of ways using existing Plans of the same type or by other Plan types.  For example, a RoutePlan could be constrained by a previous RoutePlan, a previous RouteActivityPlan and/or a TaskPlan.

2. Environment overrides to selectively modify details of the current planning environment in the context of a single planning command. For example, a known threat Entity in the environment could be removed and an expected Entity could be added to the environment in the context of a single planning command in support of "what-if" planning.

3. "Weightings" that influence the decisions of operators or planning algorithms performing the planning.

4. Temporal and/or kinematic begin and end points.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanCommandStatus" type="uci:RoutePlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a RoutePlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the RoutePlanCommand message.  Creation/update of a RoutePlan  may occur autonomously, without a RoutePlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the RoutePlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:RoutePlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more RoutePlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanStatus" type="uci:RoutePlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current state in a planning and plan validation state machine for a RoutePlan.  Planning and validation may occur autonomously or be commanded.  In the former case, there is no command status message indicating the resulting state.  This message provides current status regardless of how status changes were triggered.  This message is not to be used to status individual actions within the RoutePlan, only the whole plan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanValidation" type="uci:RoutePlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the RoutePlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanValidationCommand" type="uci:RoutePlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing RoutePlan.  It includes an option to modify the Plan to make it valid if it is found to be invalid.  A RoutePlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing RoutePlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="RoutePlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:RoutePlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate a RoutePlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_Activity" type="uci:SAR_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the SAR Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (SAR_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work SAR, 4) SAR scheduled for the future and 5) SAR that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single SAR_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the SAR_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_Capability" type="uci:SAR_CapabilityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) form of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready SAR Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:SAR_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the SAR capabilities.   This includes whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_Command" type="uci:SAR_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a SAR Capability to perform a new SAR Activity or change an existing interactive SAR Activity.  It differs from a SAR Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_CommandStatus" type="uci:SAR_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a SAR_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the SAR_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_SettingsCommand" type="uci:SAR_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the SAR Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SAR_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:SAR_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of a SAR settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SatelliteCharacteristics" type="uci:SatelliteCharacteristicsMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message describes the physical and performance characteristics of a satellite.  It's generally intended to describe a type of class of satellite.  Specific instances of a type or class of satellite, including its kinematics, are described in separate SOB* messages which can reference the mostly static characteristics described in this message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SecurityAuditRecord" type="uci:SecurityAuditRecordMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>***** THIS MESSAGE HAS BEEN FORMALLY IDENTIFIED FOR DEPRECATION AND WILL BE REMOVED FROM THE SCHEMA IN A SUBSEQUENT FORMAL RELEASE.  ****** This message indicates a set of current audit records for the service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SelfDefenseStatus" type="uci:SelfDefenseStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the self defense capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, and quantities of self defense capabilities.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceConfigurationChangeRequest" type="uci:ServiceConfigurationChangeRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message can be made in two ways. First, it could indicate a desire to apply a configuration parameter to a specified service. In this case, ServiceConfigurationChangeRequestID represents the transaction ID for the configuration Request. In the second form, it could represent a broadcast request to all services to react to a change to an affected item. For instance, if a configuration parameter request is made to a service, the service may respond that the configuration change was made successfully, but a configuration item was affected. This affected item would then be broadcast back to all services, which may be forced to re-process the configuration item.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceConfigurationChangeRequestStatus" type="uci:ServiceConfigurationChangeRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to the ServiceConfigurationChangeRequest. ServiceConfigurationChangeRequestID represents the transaction ID for the configuration Request. ServiceConfigurationChangeRequestStatus indicates the success/failure of the request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceConfigurationParams" type="uci:ServiceConfigurationParamsMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message allows services to specify what service specific configuration values can be manipulated by the operator during planning or operations. This message should only be used to advertise legitimate configurable items for a specific service that does not have a legitimate need to be separately documented in the UCI schema. For instance certain algorithmic parameters may be documented on implementation of a service which may be totally inapplicable to another service of the same type that was designed in a different manner. This message facilitates the creation of a generic configuration display that meets the needs of any type of service for which configurable items exist.  A service should publish this message upon initialization to notify the system of their configurable items.  Otherwise, this message is published on receipt of a ServiceConfigurationParamsDataRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceConfigurationParamsDataRequest" type="uci:ServiceConfigurationParamsDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a request to services to provide a list of service specific operator modifiable configuration items.  Admin configurable items that should not be changed by an operator during operations or planning should not be advertised utilizing this message. Services that do advertise configuration items via this message are also responsible for responding to requests to alter these configuration items and storing the change persistently.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceConfigurationParamsDataRequestStatus" type="uci:ServiceConfigurationParamsDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is generated in response to a ServiceConfigurationParamsDataRequest.  It provides the state of the request and allows services to return what configuration values can be manipulated by the operator during operations or planning. This message should only be used to advertise legitimate configurable items for a specific service that does not have a legitimate need to be separately documented in the UCI schema. For instance certain algorithmic parameters may be documented on implementation of a service which may be totally inapplicable to another service of the same type that was designed in a different manner. This message facilitates the creation of a generic configuration display that meets the needs of any type of service for which configurable items exist.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceErrorReport" type="uci:ServiceErrorReportMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a general report of an error that is of significance to other Systems, Services, etc. in the network.  This message is not intended for remote, centralized error logging; it is meant for "error awareness".</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceLifecycleCommand" type="uci:ServiceLifecycleCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a command to one or more services to begin, terminate, or restart operation. Services respond to this command with ServiceLifecycleCommandStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceLifecycleCommandStatus" type="uci:ServiceLifecycleCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a response to service control messages and indicate the success or failure of the operation requested.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceRegistrationRequest" type="uci:ServiceRegistrationRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Sent by a service requesting to be registered for specific mission purposes/functions/mission critical transitions.  Allows a service to announce what functions they provide (what its purposes are) that are not related to a Capability.  For example this could be used for mission processing service orchestration along with a system level orchestrator that understands the system needs in executing specific Mission behaviors.  Allows multiple services to be available (registered) to perform functions that are not typical capabilities such as Mission Planning, Routing, Assessment Models, and Task Allocation.  Then the system level orchestrator can determine which of multiple services available for a specific function should be performing the function based on current and/or specific mission objectives.  The registered/unregistered state of the service could be enforced by the orchestrator via the ServiceSettingsCommand message.  Another example of use is if a service becomes disabled, the service orchestration manager could enable another service for the purpose or several others to provide the purposes of the disabled service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceRegistrationRequestStatus" type="uci:ServiceRegistrationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Status returned to a service requesting registration for specific mission purposes/functions/mission critical transitions.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceSettingsCommand" type="uci:ServiceSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands a Service to update its Settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:ServiceSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status to a ServiceSettingsCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceStatus" type="uci:ServiceStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status of a service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceStatusDataRequest" type="uci:ServiceStatusDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the current status of a service.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="ServiceStatusDataRequestStatus" type="uci:ServiceStatusDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a ServiceStatusRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SharedApertureSupportCapabilityStatus" type="uci:SharedApertureSupportCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides Support Capability status for any of the Support Capabilities associated with aperture sharing, the ResourceAllocator, Antenna, and RFDC.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SignalReport" type="uci:SignalReportMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report a set of related Signals detected by a signals intelligence (ESM, ELINT and/or COMINT) Subsystem.  The meaning of "related" is intentionally ambiguous.  They could be related through a single common parent Activity, through their association to an emitter Entity or for other reasons.  For signals intelligence Subsystems, detections can be reported at different levels of abstraction corresponding to the PulseData, SignalReport and/or Entity messages.  Additionally, different C2 use cases can result in one or more of the messages being produced.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_Activity" type="uci:SMTI_ActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the SMTI Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (SMTI_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work SMTI, 4) SMTI scheduled for the future and 5) SMTI that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single SMTI_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the SMTI_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_Capability" type="uci:SMTI_CapabilityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the SMTI (Surface Moving Target Indication) category of UCI Capability.  SMTI detects non-airborne items through active radar search and track.  In UCI, SMTI is defined as not-Air MTI; it includes both ground MTI (GMTI) and maritime MTI (MMTI).  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready SMTI Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_CapabilityStatus" type="uci:SMTI_CapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the SMTI capabilities.   This includes whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_Command" type="uci:SMTI_CommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the SMTI (Radar Surface Moving Target Indicator, where "surface" includes ground and maritime backgrounds) form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a SMTI Capability to perform a new SMTI Activity or change an existing interactive SMTI Activity.  It differs from a SMTI Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_CommandStatus" type="uci:SMTI_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an SMTI_Command.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the SMTI_Activity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_SettingsCommand" type="uci:SMTI_SettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the SMTI Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SMTI_SettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:SMTI_SettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the acknowledgment and/or acceptance of an SMTI settings command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SOB_C2_Record" type="uci:SOB_C2_RecordMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the attributes of a C2 node (such as a ground station) associated with a satellite or multiple satellites.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SOB_CorrelationRecord" type="uci:SOB_CorrelationRecordMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a potential correlation (match) between an existing SOB_C2_Record/SOB_SatelliteRecord and another message occurred.  For example an Entity* message from a source that provides situational awareness on Resident Space Objects (RSO) might have an orbit, RF emissions and/or an identity that matches those of an SOB_C2_Record/SOB_SatelliteRecord.  This message records the correlation along with a snapshot of select data for later review and analysis followed by potential updates to the "truth" or "reference" catalog of SOB_C2_Record/SOB_SatelliteRecords.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SOB_SatelliteRecord" type="uci:SOB_SatelliteRecordMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the attributes of the satellite or the launch vehicle. This information includes, but not limited to, the identity of the satellite or launch vehicle, orbit information, time stamps, operational status, type of mission, TLE data, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SpaceWeather" type="uci:SpaceWeatherMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents weather as a collection of reports and/or warnings, each associated with a specific space weather event.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SpectralDensityReport" type="uci:SpectralDensityReportMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the RF energy spectral density received by an ESM or other RF sensor or communications subsystem.  In the hierarchy of UCI messages used for reporting received RF energy and signals, this message is between raw IQ data and PulseData.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StoreCarriageCapabilityStatus" type="uci:StoreCarriageCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the Carriage Capability status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StoreLoadoutConfiguration" type="uci:StoreLoadoutConfigurationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents the different possible loadouts for the aircraft. Each loadout is a valid combination of items that could be installed on the aircraft at the same time.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StoreManagementCommand" type="uci:StoreManagementCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands various operations performed on a weapon store.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StoreManagementCommandStatus" type="uci:StoreManagementCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of its corresponding StoreManagementCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StoreManagementReport" type="uci:StoreManagementReportMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the state of various weapon store conditions.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StoreManagementStatus" type="uci:StoreManagementStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message advertises store weapon types.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeActivity" type="uci:StrikeActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Strike Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (StrikeCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work Strike, 4) Strike scheduled for the future and 5) Strike that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single StrikeCommand or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the StrikeActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeCapability" type="uci:StrikeCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Strike category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Strike Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeCapabilityStatus" type="uci:StrikeCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the availability of weapon capabilities and MDF state and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeCommand" type="uci:StrikeCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Strike form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a Strike Capability to perform a new Strike Activity or change an existing interactive Strike Activity.  It differs from a Strike Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeCommandStatus" type="uci:StrikeCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a StrikeCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the StrikeActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeConsentRequest" type="uci:StrikeConsentRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request consent to perform a Strike Activity.  Status for this command message is provided via the StrikeConsentRequestStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeConsentRequestStatus" type="uci:StrikeConsentRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of its corresponding StrikeConsentRequest, which requests consent to perform a Strike Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeSettingsCommand" type="uci:StrikeSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the Strike Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="StrikeSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:StrikeSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of its corresponding StrikeSettingsCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemBIT_Command" type="uci:SubsystemBIT_CommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to command BIT or BIT-related functions.  Some Subsystems only support commanded/initiated BIT via SubsystemStateCommand.  This message is intended for command/initiated BIT that isn't a Subsystem state and generally doesn't interrupt other Subsystem functions.  See SubsystemBIT_Configuration for further details regarding the difference between types of commanded/initiated BIT.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemBIT_CommandStatus" type="uci:SubsystemBIT_CommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report command status in response to the corresponding SubsystemBIT_Command received by the subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemBIT_Configuration" type="uci:SubsystemBIT_ConfigurationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the Built In Test (BIT) messaging interfaces and specific tests supported by a Subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemBIT_Status" type="uci:SubsystemBIT_StatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status and results of Subsystem Built In Tests (BIT) along with faults.  This message is used for BIT status regardless of how the BIT is initiated.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemCalibrationCommand" type="uci:SubsystemCalibrationCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to command calibration or calibration-related functions.  Some Subsystems only support commanded/initiated calibration via SubsystemStateCommand.  This message is intended for command/initiated Calibration that isn't a Subsystem state and generally doesn't interrupt other Subsystem functions.  See SubsystemCalibrationConfiguration for further details regarding the difference between types of commanded/initiated Calibration.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemCalibrationCommandStatus" type="uci:SubsystemCalibrationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report command status in response to the corresponding SubsystemCalibrationCommand received by the subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemCalibrationConfiguration" type="uci:SubsystemCalibrationConfigurationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the Calibration messaging interfaces and specific calibrations supported by a Subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemCalibrationStatus" type="uci:SubsystemCalibrationStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current status and results of Subsystem Calibration.  This message is used for Calibration status regardless of how the Calibration is initiated.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemConfiguration" type="uci:SubsystemConfigurationMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a Subsystem's current operating configuration.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemConfigurationDataRequest" type="uci:SubsystemConfigurationDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is for requesting a Subsystem's current operating configuration.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemConfigurationDataRequestStatus" type="uci:SubsystemConfigurationDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a SubsystemConfigurationRequest message.  Indicates a Subsystem's current operating configuration.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemEraseCommand" type="uci:SubsystemEraseCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands a Subsystem to erase data from memory.  This command should only be used for Subsystems that can perform erase processing independent of Shutdown processing.  The Subsystem remains in one of its Operational states during its erase processing.  If the Subsystem can only perform erase processing during its transition to its terminal SHUTDOWN state, then use the SubsystemStateCommand with CommandState = SHUTDOWN and optional EraseParameter field to include erase processing while shutting down.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemEraseCommandStatus" type="uci:SubsystemEraseCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides status to a SubsystemEraseCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemMaintenanceCommand" type="uci:SubsystemMaintenanceCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message commands a subsystem to perform maintenance test.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemMaintenanceCommandStatus" type="uci:SubsystemMaintenanceCommandStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to provide subsystem maintenance operation results.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemMaintenanceConfiguration" type="uci:SubsystemMaintenanceConfigurationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the maintenance test messaging interfaces and specific tests supported by a Subsystem. These maintenance tests fall outside standard BIT or calibration tests.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemMaintenanceStatus" type="uci:SubsystemMaintenanceStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides the ability to report maintenance operation results.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemSettingsCommand" type="uci:SubsystemSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to command various subsystem settings to be enabled, disabled, or reset, including data recording.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:SubsystemSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report command status in response to the corresponding SubsystemSettingsCommand received by the subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStateCommand" type="uci:SubsystemStateCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to command a subsystem to transition to another of its states if the subsystem is currently in a state to support transition to the commanded state.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStateCommandStatus" type="uci:SubsystemStateCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to report command status in response to the corresponding SubsystemStateCommand received by the subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStatus" type="uci:SubsystemStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a Subsystem's current operating state.  For configuration data use SubsystemConfiguration message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStatusDataRequest" type="uci:SubsystemStatusDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests the current status of a Subsystem.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStatusDataRequestStatus" type="uci:SubsystemStatusDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a SubsystemStatusRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStatusDetailed" type="uci:SubsystemStatusDetailedMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains the current detailed status of the reporting Subsystem.  This message is for reporting subsystem unique data that cannot be reported with other messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStatusDetailedDataRequest" type="uci:SubsystemStatusDetailedDataRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request that a Subsystem report its detailed status. A Subsystem will send the SubsystemStatusDetailedRequestStatusMT message when responding to this request.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStatusDetailedDataRequestStatus" type="uci:SubsystemStatusDetailedDataRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The status resulting from the handling of a previously received SubsystemStatusDetailedRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SubsystemStream" type="uci:SubsystemStreamMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a free form text stream associated with a setting of a Subsystem.  This message should not, under any circumstances, be used for C2 purposes or reporting of results of UCI C2 such as Task results, Command results, Service request results, etc.  This message should only be used for reporting of Subsystem-unique data that can't be reasonably standardized and/or is temporary for development purposes.  Examples include debug data, logs, etc. that are needed in real time and therefore can't simply be recorded through file I/O into a Subsystem-unique data file for post-mission use.  The stream is expected to be displayed, sent through an instrumentation link and/or centrally recorded along with other message traffic.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SurvivabilityRiskLevel" type="uci:SurvivabilityRiskLevelMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to specify the relative survivability risks that a mission planner is permitted per a given priority.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemDeploymentActivity" type="uci:SystemDeploymentActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked; it is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for a Capability category.  However, a SystemDeploymentActivity message is different from other [Capability]Activity messages in that it describes the activities of a carrying system that is capable of deploying other systems that have capabilities of their own.  While these systems are not deployed, they are considered a part of the deploying system.  When the ActivityState is active, it means a system is being deployed by the parent system.  The Kinematics (Planned or Actual) are unique to the SystemDeploymentActivity.  The PlannedKinematics element represents the orbit plan the planning service provided for the deployed system and starts upon deployment.  The ActualKinematics becomes key if it is different from what was planned and could be the result of a replan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemDeploymentCapability" type="uci:SystemDeploymentCapabilityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the SystemDeployment category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready SystemDeployment Capability so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.  The SystemDeploymentCapability is unique from other UCI Capability messages in that it represents the capabilities of a carrying system that can deploy another system.  It describes the kinds of deployment the carrying system is able to perform for deploying another system and the systems it can deploy.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemDeploymentCapabilityStatus" type="uci:SystemDeploymentCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the System Deployment capabilities available to the carrying system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemEstimationRequest" type="uci:SystemEstimationRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to invoke generation of estimated system state in terms of position and status.  The request includes which messages the estimator service is expected to produce and at what frequency (periodic or single).  The message also optionally allows the user to specify a mission plan segment as the starting point for the estimation.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemEstimationRequestStatus" type="uci:SystemEstimationRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a response to a request to generate an estimated representation of a system.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemManagementRequest" type="uci:SystemManagementRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to request changes to a system's status or configurable settings.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemManagementRequestStatus" type="uci:SystemManagementRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a response to a SystemManagementRequest message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemMetadata" type="uci:SystemMetadataMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies supplemental data for Systems.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemNotification" type="uci:SystemNotificationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message provides a notification related to a System.  There are endless possible events, states, state changes and behaviors of Systems to monitor/observe.  In lieu of enumerating them all, this message categorizes them into "perspectives" such as kinematics, weather and identity.  For example, a weather monitoring Service could produce a SystemNotification message advising that a particular System is entering an area with potential weather hazards.  This message optionally references other messages that triggered or are related to the notification.  For example, a SystemNotification with a weather perspective could reference a WeatherDataset message with detailed weather data.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalElementSet" type="uci:SystemOrbitalElementSetMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents what is commonly referred to as the two line element (TLE) for a System in orbit around Earth.  It describes a satellite's orbit using general perturbations theory (SGP, SGP4, PPT3) and Keplerian elements.  An orbital element set (OES) contains six classical orbital elements and a time element (epoch) which can be converted into position and velocity vectors to generate the geometry and motion of where the satellite is located and where it is going.  An OES is a refined collection of data, continually updated over the life of the satellite.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalElementSetRequest" type="uci:SystemOrbitalElementSetRequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests an orbital element set for a System, applicable for a particular time window.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalElementSetRequestStatus" type="uci:SystemOrbitalElementSetRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to an SystemOrbitalElementSetRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalEphemeris" type="uci:SystemOrbitalEphemerisMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents ephemeris for a System in the form of a series of predicted or actual positions and velocities of a satellite in an orderly sequence of time points (may be created at fixed or variable time spacing). Optionally includes covariance matrices that contains information about the uncertainties associated with the solution of the element corrections.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalEphemerisRequest" type="uci:SystemOrbitalEphemerisRequestMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests an ephemeris for an orbiting System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalEphemerisRequestStatus" type="uci:SystemOrbitalEphemerisRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a SystemOrbitalEphemerisRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalPositionReport" type="uci:SystemOrbitalPositionReportMT" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents orbital kinematics for an System in the form of a single Cartesian state vector of position and velocity that together with its time (epoch) uniquely determine the trajectory of an orbiting body in space. Requires selection of the frame of reference and should, in general, only be propagated using the same model used for the orbit determination.  Optionally includes a covariance matrix that contains information about the uncertainties associated with the solution of the element corrections.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalVCM" type="uci:SystemOrbitalVCM_MT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a "vector covariance matrix" or VCM which is a specialized state vector, along with extensive propagation parameters, tailored specifically for customers who are using an ASW system to propagate the vector.  A VCM should not be confused with the simpler and more common kinematic variance for position-position or position-velocity.  The VCM has all the additional information needed to correctly propagate the vector using the Special Perturbations Ephemeris Generator in ASW. The VCM includes a covariance matrix that provides the epoch covariance in equinoctial elements and only provides UVW variances. TLE and ephemeris for a System are given in other System* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalVCM_Request" type="uci:SystemOrbitalVCM_RequestMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests an orbital VCM for a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitalVCM_RequestStatus" type="uci:SystemOrbitalVCM_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the status in response to a request for an orbital VCM for a System.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemOrbitReport" type="uci:SystemOrbitReportMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current propulsive potential of the System. It is one of the space "station-keeping" series of messages for UCI spacecraft Systems. The space station-keeping series is: SystemOrbitalPositionReport, SystemStatus, SystemOrbitReport, [Capability]Status and [Capability]Activity.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemReadiness" type="uci:SystemReadinessMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the future status of a system in terms of its availability/readiness for a mission.  It might be reported by a surrogate if the given system can't report it when needed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemsNeededRequest" type="uci:SystemsNeededRequestMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a possible loadout for the system ID or model specified.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemsNeededRequestStatus" type="uci:SystemsNeededRequestStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a SystemsNeededRequest.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemStatus" type="uci:SystemStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the current, highest level summary status of a System, its identification and an indication of the Services, Subsystems and Capabilities it contains.  A System is anything that can self-report information about itself.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="SystemTimeAtReference" type="uci:SystemTimeAtReferenceMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is not a reference for time synchronization.  It is not an alternative to Network Time Protocol (NTP), GPS 1PPS or other means of time synchronization.  It is a complement.  It is a means of sharing the "system time" at the last time synchronization reference event/strobe.  "System time" is the time on whatever the reference clock for the System.  The reference clock could be a local clock or an external clock.  The reference event/strobe could be a discrete signal such as GPS 1PPS.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TacticalOrderActivity" type="uci:TacticalOrderActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the Tactical Order Capability of a System.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is actively doing. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked; it is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for a Capability category (ESM, EA, etc.). In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability.  For the space domain, only Operational Level messages are provided.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TacticalOrderCapability" type="uci:TacticalOrderCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Tactical Order category of UCI Capability.  It "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Tactical Order Capability so that it can be "discovered" and used. "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective. UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities. They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files. UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission. In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services. A [Capability]Capability message describes what a System is capable of doing if invoked. A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations and for the Tactical Order Capability, it could be an order to refuel, return to base or similar change.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TacticalOrderCapabilityStatus" type="uci:TacticalOrderCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the Tactical Order capabilities of a system. This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TagAssociation" type="uci:TagAssociationMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides a mechanism to make an association between an item and a tag.   Same design pattern as user defined categories that associate things on the internet.  Allows filtering of files by an operator based on flexible criteria, e.g. I want to see all files of any type that have the tags "Targeting" and "Preplanned".  Allows filtering of files by services based on flexible criteria, e.g. I want to discover all imagery that has the tags "Preplanned" and lacks the tag "Processed".</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Task" type="uci:TaskMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies a Task that a System will perform against a specific entity or geographical location.  Tasks include flight, collection, strike, electronic attack, directed energy, laser designation, cargo delivery and communication relay.  They are abstracted to a level of C2 and autonomy derived from UCI operational vignettes.  Tasks enable services, operators and other actors to do automated, dynamic, machine-to-machine allocation of Tasks to Systems and subsequent mission planning.  If necessary, authorization of Users to Task Systems can be controlled using the Authorization message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskCancelCommand" type="uci:TaskCancelCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is a request to cancel a Task which has been allocated and planned.  A planning input such as Effect, Action, Task and DMPI can't easily be canceled or removed from the System due to race conditions between their various users and publishers, inevitability of the associated activity, etc.  Additionally, the operator or service initiating the cancellation might be different than the one that manages RequirementSets and/or issues *PlanCommand, introducing additional race conditions.  In order to avoid race conditions, receive positive feedback on the success of the cancellation and allow canceled messages/objects to remain in the system for historical purposes, an explicit TaskCancelCommand is necessary.  A TaskCancelCommand shall result in the Task being canceled unless execution was already inevitable.  If the Task was inevitable and started executing but can be stopped (such as a long collection Task) it shall cancel as soon as possible.  TaskCancelCommand shall result in managers of RequirementSets to remove the associated Task, thereby removing the Task from the next planning cycle.  Services other than those that manage RequirementSets and execute activities aren't required to handle TaskCancelCommand.  TaskCancelCommand doesn't necessarily trigger a replan (there isn't a corresponding planning trigger), therefore a System could continue to navigate routes related to the canceled Task, even though the Task won't occur.  A subsequent replan could exclude the Task.  Status or feedback for this message is given through TaskCancelCommandStatus but the resulting status of the Task is given through TaskStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskCancelCommandStatus" type="uci:TaskCancelCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is sent in response to a TaskCancelCommand and indicates if the Task was canceled and if not why it was not canceled.  The resulting status of the Task is reported in TaskStatus.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskCommand" type="uci:TaskCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Task form of the general UCI Capability Command.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskCommandStatus" type="uci:TaskCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of an TaskCommand.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskExecutionApprovalStatus" type="uci:TaskExecutionApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the state of approval for execution of a task. ApprovalPolicy allow an authority to define policies to approve or reject a Task as it enters execution. Approval may or may not be required. If approval is required, there can be multiple approvers. See Approval* messages for additional details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlan" type="uci:TaskPlanMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message specifies the Tasks allocated to a System.  Allocation can be done manually by operators, by an allocation service or a combination.  This message decouples Task allocation from kinematic, action and other types of planning; Tasks allocations can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on task allocation.

A TaskPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example the TaskPlan for the top System in a hierarchical System of System could be an input to a task allocation service that the produces a TaskPlan per subordinate System with their subset of allocated Tasks.

Additional details such as timing, dependencies, decomposition, etc. can be added during the task allocation process and captured in the TaskPlan to supplement the information in the allocated Tasks themselves.

A TaskPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  A TaskPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple TaskPlans and *Plans in general.  A TaskPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a TaskPlan are given in other TaskPlan* messages.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanApprovalStatus" type="uci:TaskPlanApprovalStatusMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates status/state of approval for a TaskPlan.  Approval may or may not be required.  ApprovalPolicy aligned with the *Plans and states of planning allow a planning oversight authority to approve or reject plans as they're created or updated.  Approval may or may not be required.  If required, there may be many approvers.  See Approval* messages for additional details.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanCommand" type="uci:TaskPlanCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to perform a task allocation plan process or function. The TaskPlan message/object is the output of a task allocation plan process or function.  The TaskPlan output of one task allocation plan process function could be an input to the next function.  UCI enables "discovery" of the task allocation plan process and function of a System via the ControlStatus message. This mechanism for discovery empowers a System performing task allocation planning for subordinate Systems of heterogeneous types and/or service/function/process architecture to selectively use, replace or supplement them.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanCommandStatus" type="uci:TaskPlanCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to create/update a TaskPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine; see the TaskPlanCommand message.  Creation/update of a TaskPlan  may occur autonomously, without a TaskPlanCommand in which case the current state of the plan could be given in the TaskPlanStatus message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanExecutionStatus" type="uci:TaskPlanExecutionStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the execution status for one or more TaskPlans.  Plan execution begins after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.  This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanningStatus" type="uci:TaskPlanningStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message defines planning status of a single Task.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanStatus" type="uci:TaskPlanStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the status/state of a TaskPlan.

*Plan related status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services that provide the status and the different times it is given.  Those messages are *PlanStatus, *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanValidation" type="uci:TaskPlanValidationMT" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of the validation, including the current state in a validation state machine, and details of invalid items. Validation may occur periodically, or onesie twosie. See the TaskPlanValidationCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanValidationCommand" type="uci:TaskPlanValidationCommandMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates a command to validate/re-validate an existing TaskPlan.  It includes an option to modify the Plan to make it valid if it is found to be invalid.  A TaskPlan can become invalid due to changes in Task timing, weather, order of battle, kinematic movement constraints, operator manual edits and many, many other reasons.  The message allows an existing TaskPlan to be checked for validity given such changes.  The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies.  Different variables might be considered depending on the level of detail of the plan or stage in the overall planning process.  The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskPlanValidationCommandStatus" type="uci:TaskPlanValidationCommandStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the status of a command to validate a TaskPlan, including the current state in a command and processing state machine.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TaskStatus" type="uci:TaskStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the execution status for a Task.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TurretCommand" type="uci:TurretCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to manually command a turret's mode of operation or to directly tell it where to look.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TurretCommandStatus" type="uci:TurretCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of its corresponding TurretCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TurretReport" type="uci:TurretReportMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current operational state of the turret, including where it is pointing.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="TurretStatus" type="uci:TurretStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of a turret, including information regarding whether or not it is currently available to be commanded, based on hardware status, commanded operational states, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="Weather" type="uci:WeatherMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents weather as a collection of reports and/or warnings, each associated with a geospatial location.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherDataset" type="uci:WeatherDatasetMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents weather summarized from an external source that will be used for planning.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherObservation" type="uci:WeatherObservationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a weather observation from a System.  WeatherObservations are generally less formal than WeatherReports or WeatherWarnings, originating from Systems whose primary mission isn't weather related.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarActivity" type="uci:WeatherRadarActivityMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the WeatherRadar Capability of a Subsystem of a System.  In general, a [Capability]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities are doing.  It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.).  In other words, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability.  An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from manual commands (WeatherRadarCommand) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently in-work collection, 4) collection scheduled for the future and 5) collection that was scheduled and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons.  A single *Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the WeatherRadarActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarCapability" type="uci:WeatherRadarCapabilityMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents the Weather Radar category of UCI Capability.  This message "advertises" the mostly static characteristics of a mission-ready Weather Radar Capability (of a Subsystem of System) so that it can be "discovered" and used.  "Discovery" can be a misnomer depending on your perspective.  UCI Capability messages aren't meant to enable dynamic discovery of design details of never-before-seen Capabilities.  They don't eliminate the need for future software updates or configuration files with performance characteristics indexed by Subsystem model number.  UCI Capability messages are meant to support discovery of what changes for a Capability from mission to mission or even within the course of a mission, especially as it relates to command and control.  In UCI terminology, a Capability is something a System can do that is useful in Missions performed by a heterogeneous family of Systems and Services.  A [Capability]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked.  A [Capability]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing.  Capabilities are aligned with end effects of Mission operations such as imagery collection, active target search, passive threat detection, electronic attack and others.  Functions which support these end effects including turret control, carriage control and door control aren't considered UCI Capabilities but might be addressed by other UCI messages.  Capabilities are the standardized taxonomy of high level Mission end-effects functions a System can perform.  Further, the Capability taxonomy establishes the "level" at which the Mission Subsystems of a System: 1) can be controlled, 2) can be functionally re-integrated into new Systems without their application software changing, 3) are modeled by allocation and planning services including Task Allocation, Route Planning, Mission Monitoring/Contingency and Task Generation services, 4) are modeled by Capability management services including Capability Managers and Capability Controllers and 5) report their configuration, availability and status.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarCapabilityStatus" type="uci:WeatherRadarCapabilityStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the current status of the WeatherRadar capabilities available to the system.   This includes information regarding whether or not each capability is currently available based on hardware status, commanded operational states, associated RF profiles etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarCommand" type="uci:WeatherRadarCommandMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the Weather Radar form of the general UCI Capability Command.  It is used to manually invoke a Weather Radar Capability to perform a new Weather Radar Activity or change an existing interactive Activity.  It differs from a Weather Radar Task because it is intended for direct interaction with the host of the Capability, not a planning Service, and therefore is not expected to impact the route of the host System or otherwise interrupt the planned MissionPlan.  Additionally, Capability Commands are intended to allow multiple clients to use a single Capability as a shared resource; see the Control* series of messages.  Capability Commands can be used in parallel with Tasks, but are generally deferential to them.  However, exceptions are allowed and even expected; the relationship between Tasks and Capability Commands is not rigidly dictated by the UCI schema.  Several mechanisms are provided in other messages to tailor the relationship and relative importance of Tasks and [Capability]Commands including Capability precedence, Task rank, [Capability]Command rank, [Capability]Activity rank and control Authorization.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarCommandStatus" type="uci:WeatherRadarCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates the result of a WeatherRadarCommand.  This message will reflect whether the command was received, accepted and/or rejected.  This message will only be used to status the command until the recipient determines whether or not it can be started or scheduled.  The details of the specific activity or activities that result from the command will report status via the WeatherRadarActivity message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarObservation" type="uci:WeatherRadarObservationMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message contains weather data as observed by a system at a given location and time.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Status-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarSettingsCommand" type="uci:WeatherRadarSettingsCommandMT" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is used to change the "settings" associated with all of the WeatherRadar Capabilities of a Subsystem.  [Capability]SettingsCommands generally affect the subsequent operation of all applicable Capabilities of the Subsystem; they are considered presettings, independent of [Capability]Command.  Unlike most [Capability]Commands they are understood to remain set until changed.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WeatherRadarSettingsCommandStatus" type="uci:WeatherRadarSettingsCommandStatusMT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message reports the receipt, acceptance, or rejection of its corresponding WeatherRadarSettingsCommand message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: Command-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WorkingEOB" type="uci:WorkingEOB_MT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message represents a "working" Electronic Order of Battle (EOB); a subset of the whole EOB bound by a corresponding OpZone.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WorkingEOB_Request" type="uci:WorkingEOB_RequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests creation of a new WorkingEOB from an optionally filtered subset of a System's EOB catalog or from an existing WorkingEOB.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WorkingEOB_RequestStatus" type="uci:WorkingEOB_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a WorkingEOB_Request message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WorkingSOB" type="uci:WorkingSOB_MT" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates an order of battle (OB) set of Resident Space Objects (RSO) and their characteristics that are under consideration by a planning service.  The OB is "working" in the sense that it is tailored/curated to suit the planning needs of a particular set of services, a particular mission, a particular course of action of a mission, etc.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: DataRecord-1.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WorkingSOB_Request" type="uci:WorkingSOB_RequestMT" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message requests creation of a new WorkingSOB from an optionally filtered subset of a System's SOB catalog or from an existing WorkingSOB.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:element name="WorkingSOB_RequestStatus" type="uci:WorkingSOB_RequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message is the response to a WorkingSOB_Request message.</xs:documentation>
			<xs:documentation>UCI_PRIMITIVE: ActionRequest-2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
	</xs:element>
	<xs:complexType name="AboutType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Model" type="uci:VisibleString32Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a widely known and coordinated model name for the associated item, generally established at the time of production.  For example, "AN/APG-79".</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SerialNumber" type="uci:VisibleString256Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the serial number or other unique identifier assigned to an instance of the Model, generally established at the time of production.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SoftwareVersion" type="uci:VisibleString256Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the installed software/OFP version, for items whose software can be updated post-production without changing the Model.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="BootloaderSoftwareVersion" type="uci:VisibleString256Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the installed bootloader version.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="HardwareVersion" type="uci:VisibleString32Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the installed hardware version, for items whose hardware can be updated post-production without changing the Model.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="Acceleration3D_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Acceleration relative to WGS-84 ellipsoid.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="NorthAcceleration" type="uci:AccelerationType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The acceleration component along the true north direction.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EastAcceleration" type="uci:AccelerationType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The acceleration component along the east direction.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="DownAcceleration" type="uci:AccelerationType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The acceleration component along the down direction.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Timestamp" type="uci:DateTimeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the date and time at which the acceleration data was measured, last known to be correct and/or predicted to occur.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AccelerationAccelerationCovarianceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AnAn" type="xs:double">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>North-North acceleration covariance with units of meters squared per seconds to the fourth power.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AnAe" type="xs:double">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>North-East acceleration covariance with units of meters squared per seconds to the fourth power.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AnAd" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>North-Down acceleration covariance with units of meters squared per seconds to the fourth power. Required when the Acceleration includes a down component.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AeAe" type="xs:double">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>East-East acceleration covariance with units of meters squared per seconds to the fourth power.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AeAd" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>East-Down acceleration covariance with units of meters squared per seconds to the fourth power. Required when the Acceleration includes a down component.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AdAd" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Down-Down acceleration covariance with units of meters squared per seconds to the fourth power. Required when the Acceleration includes a down component.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AcceptedProductInformationType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AcceptedSensorType" type="uci:SensorTypeEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The type of sensor data that the System can process.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AcceptedInputProduct" type="uci:ProductFilterType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The type of products that the System can ingest, e.g. NITF, JP2, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AccessAssessmentFilterType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AssessmentName" type="uci:VisibleString32Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a name/type of AccessAssessment algorithm required to match the filter.  If omitted, any name/type of AccessAssessment algorithm satisfies the filter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AssessedCapabilities" type="uci:CapabilityTaxonomyUniversalType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates assessed Capabilities of the AccessAssessment that are required to match the filter.  If omitted, any assessed Capability satisfies the filter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Subject" type="uci:AssetFilterType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Subjects of the AccessAssessment that are required to match the filter.  If omitted, any Subject satisfies the filter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AssessedObject" type="uci:AssetFilterType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Assessed Objects of the AccessAssessment that are required to match the filter.  If omitted, any Assessed Object satisfies the filter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AccessEvent" type="uci:AccessEventFilterType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Access Event details necessary to match the filter.  If omitted, any Access Event satisfies the filter.  Multiple instances are logically ORed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AccessAssessmentID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
