	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="Az_El_BeamPointingType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Beam pointing type in which Azimuth and/or Elevation can be specified to provide a direction for beam pointing.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest. Azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer ("down the right wing").This is only optional when this information is not available.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest. Elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when below. This is only optional when this information is not available.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="Az_El_BeamPointingWithRangeType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Beam pointing type in which Azimuth and/or Elevation can be specified to provide a direction for beam pointing.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest. Azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer ("down the right wing").This is only optional when this information is not available.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest. Elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when below. This is only optional when this information is not available.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SlantRange" type="uci:DistanceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the line-of-sight distance between two points.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AzElReferenceType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Defines a line of sight ray in a world inertial or body frame or reference.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Reference" type="uci:LOS_ReferenceEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies reference frame for which the sibling Azimuth and Elevation elements apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the angle from the x-axis towards the y-axis as measured on the xy plane. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a global horizontal plane at the observation point. The first line is from the observation point to true north. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the global horizontal plane. An azimuth of zero indicates true north with values increasing clockwise. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleHalfType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the angle from the xy-plane towards the z-axis. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the horizontal plane, negative when below. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when below.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AzElRequiredReferenceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Defines a line of sight based on Body, NED, Inertial, or Capability Reference Frame.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Reference" type="uci:LOS_ReferenceEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies reference frame for which the sibling Azimuth and Elevation elements apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AngleType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Azimuth angle associated with the reference enum in the sibling element. Azimuth is the angle from the x-axis towards the y-axis as measured on the xy plane.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleHalfType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Elevation angle associated with the reference enum in the sibling element. Elevation is the angle from the xy-plane towards the z-axis.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="AzimuthElevationRangePointType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The Range, Azimuth, and Elevation point.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Time" type="uci:DateTimeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the time the point was defined, or a future time the point will be at.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AnglePositiveType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest.
                        Azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a global horizontal plane at the observation point.
                        The first line is from the observation point to true north.
                        The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the global horizontal plane.
                        An azimuth of zero indicates true north with values increasing clockwise.
                        The terms azimuth and bearing are often used interchangeably but they are technically different.
                        Azimuth values are angles between 0 and 2*Pi radians (0 and 360 degrees) relative to north, in this case true north.
                        Bearing values are a combination of a reference direction (north or south) and an angle between 0 and Pi radians (0 and 180 degrees)
                        towards the east or west direction.
                        For example, an azimuth of Pi/2 radians (90 degrees) is equivalent to a bearing of "North 90 East" or "South 90 East".
                        UCI uses azimuth because it is easier for machine/software use.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest.
                        Elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point.
                        The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth.
                        The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest.
                        An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal plane.
                        Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the horizontal plane, negative when below.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Range" type="uci:DistanceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the line-of-sight distance between the observation point and point of interest.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BaseEOB_RecordType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="SecurityInformation" type="uci:SecurityInformationType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the classification of the inputs to the EOB record, containing a variety of security information such as how the data is stored, protected, and destroyed. The security level of the message itself is indicated in the message header.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EOB_RecordID" type="uci:EOB_RecordID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the ID of the EOB record.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Timestamps" type="uci:OrderOfBattleTimestampsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates relevant temporal information of this EOB record.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Identity" type="uci:IdentityType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the identity of the EOB Record in the nomenclature of the Electronic Order of Battle (EOB).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Position" type="uci:EOB_FixedPositionType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the geospatial position of the EOB item.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Mobility" type="uci:MobilityEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the mobility of the EOB item.  Omission is equivalent to the enumerate UNDETERMINED.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EOB_Codes" type="uci:EOB_CodesType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates "codes" that characterize the EOB record.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SiteOperationalStatus" type="uci:OB_OperationalStatusEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Degree in which the entity is ready to perform the overall mission. Default value is 'OPR'.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RecordOwner" type="uci:AlphanumericDashSpaceUnderscoreString3Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Creator or mastership of this record.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RemarksText" type="uci:VisibleString512Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Remarks as entered by the analyst regarding this record.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BasicEncyclopediaNumberType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Basic Encyclopedia Number, this identifies specific location of interest to an analyst.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="WorldAreaCode" type="uci:AlphanumericString4OnlyType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>World Area Code defines where the installation is located.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RecordOriginator" type="uci:RecordOriginatorStringType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Identifying agency code.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OneUpNumber" type="uci:OneUpNumberStringType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Further defines agency code.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BasicManeuverConstraintsType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates constraints for basic maneuver sensor collects.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="MaxCapableDeltaVelocity" type="uci:SpeedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The maximum delta-v capability of object being tracked (m/s). Used to provide bounds on the required sensors and timing.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MinDeltaVelocityOfInterest" type="uci:SpeedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Minimum delta-v of interest. For example, small station-keeping maneuvers may not be of interest but a large drift maneuver or inclination change would be of interest.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BCRS_KinematicsType" uci:version="001.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates kinematics vectors in the Barycentric Celestial Reference System (BCRS).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Epoch" type="uci:DateTimeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the time when the kinematic data is valid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Position" type="uci:BCRS_PositionType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a position vector in the Barycentric Celestial Reference System (BCRS).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Velocity" type="uci:OrbitalKinematicsVelocityType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a velocity vector in the Barycentric Celestial Reference System (BCRS).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Acceleration" type="uci:OrbitalKinematicsAccelerationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an acceleration vector in the Barycentric Celestial Reference System (BCRS).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Attitude" type="uci:QuaternionType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the quaternion attitude in the Barycentric Celestial Reference System (BCRS).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Covariance" type="uci:CovarianceMatrixType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The covariance matrix along with its associated category that defines the elements represented in the covariance matrix in the Barycentric Celestial Reference System (BCRS).  See the individual category enumerations for detailed explanations.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BCRS_PositionType" uci:version="000.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a position vector in the BCRS Celestial Reference System.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="X" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the x value of position in the BCRS Reference System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Y" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the y value of the position in the BCRS Reference System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Z" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the z value of the position in the BCRS Reference System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BDI_CollectionType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Quality" type="uci:PercentType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The percentage of target destroyed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AccuracyConfidence" type="uci:PercentType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Confidence of the data obtained by the BDI collection.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BeamBandWidthAndSampleRateType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="BeamBandWidth" type="uci:FrequencyRangeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Minimum and maximum frequency range, which may change (hop) over the life of the allocation, but which should be requested and allocated using a "maximum difference" between Min and Max, that is, scoping the maximum size of the data set, prior to decimation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="BeamSampleRate" type="uci:SampleRateRangeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>For an individual beam from the MFA, the maximum (and optionally, minimum) sample rate the DigitalPayload requests for the scheduled access times.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BeamID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type for UCI IDs that correspond to beams.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BeamPointingReferenceType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Beam pointing reference types which includes Antenna, Body, Inertial, or Geodetic.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="Antenna" type="uci:Az_El_BeamPointingType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Beam pointing in reference to antenna boresight. It is likely that some antenna may be steerable only in azimuth and some may only be steerable in elevation, thus both are optional.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Body" type="uci:Az_El_BeamPointingType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Beam pointing in reference to body of the platform. It is likely that some antenna may be steerable only in azimuth and some may only be steerable in elevation, thus both are optional.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Inertial" type="uci:Az_El_BeamPointingWithRangeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Beam pointing in reference to inertial frame of reference. It is likely that some antenna may be steerable only in azimuth and some may only be steerable in elevation, thus both Azimuth and Elevation are optional. Range may only be usable for some use case (perhaps with an Omni antenna) but not useful with an AESA antenna, thus it is optional.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Geodetic" type="uci:Point2D_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Beam pointing in reference to a geodetic point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BeamShapingType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type defines limited Beam Shaping Direction to the Antenna.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="BeamWeighting" type="uci:BeamWeightingType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Select the Weighting Function and sidelobe levels.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="BeamWidth" type="uci:BeamWidthType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Set the desired Beam 3 dB Beam Width.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BeamWeightingType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="BeamWeighting" type="uci:BeamWeightingEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Uniform enumeration is the same as setting sidelobe level at approximately -13 dB.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="BeamShapeSidelobeWeighting" type="uci:DecibelType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Required when BeamWeighting set to Taylor. Sidelobe level supported by the antenna is specified in its antenna configuration file (e.g. Antenna Information File).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BeamWidthType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AzimuthBeamWidth" type="uci:AnglePositiveType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>3 dB Azimuth Beam Width.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ElevationBeamWidth" type="uci:AnglePositiveType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>3 dB Elevation Beam Width.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BehaviorType" uci:version="003.001.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:CapabilityTaxonomyUniversalBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Activity" type="uci:ActivityByType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates a battlespace activity.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BIT_ID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BlankingLimitType" uci:version="001.001.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="TxPercentBlankLimit" type="uci:PercentType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Maximum amount of time blanking is permitted by the sibling SubsystemID.  Set to 0 if blanking of the Activity will not be permitted.  The Tx Subsystem shall not set the TxPercentBlankingLimit for each Subsystem ID to any less than the RF_ResourceAllocation's TxPercentBlankLimit for those Subsystems.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="BlankingSource" type="uci:BlankingSourceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>ID of Subsystem or Digital Payload which can blank the Tx Activity the amount specified by the sibling TxPercentBlankLimit.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ActivityID" type="uci:ActivityID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Activity ID of the sibling Subsystem which can blank the Tx Activity the amount specified by the sibling TxPercentBlankLimit. This ID provides a finer level of control on blanking.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BlankingSourceType" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="SubsystemID" type="uci:SubsystemID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Maximum amount of time blanking is permitted by the sibling SubsystemID.  Set to 0 if blanking of the Activity will not be permitted.  The Tx Subsystem shall not set the TxPercentBlankingLimit for each Subsystem ID to any less than the RF_ResourceAllocation's TxPercentBlankLimit for those Subsystems.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="DigitalPayload" type="uci:DigitalFunctionType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>In a digital system, the MFA can issue blanking (when a local mode makes an interruption), or the DigitalPayloads can blank one another.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BlueVehicleType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="EntityIdentity" type="uci:IdentityType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Defines the type of vehicle that the separation parameters apply to.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Model" type="uci:VisibleString32Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Defines the type of vehicle that the separation parameters apply to. This field should match the Model defined in SystemStatus.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BodyFaceType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a body face and radial offset.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="DesiredFace" type="uci:BodyFaceEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the desired face of the body.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RadialOffset" type="uci:DistanceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a radial offset distance from the center of the face.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Theta" type="uci:AnglePositiveType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the angular offset. 0 degrees is aligned with the X axis of the body face.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BoresightVelocityType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="X" type="uci:SpeedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The X direction is in the plane of the sensor/antenna face in the platform direction of motion. Units are meters/second.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Y" type="uci:SpeedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The Y direction is normal to the sensor/antenna face.  The positive direction is left of the platform direction of motion.  Units are meters/second.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Z" type="uci:SpeedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The Z direction is X cross Y.  Units are meters/second.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="BoundaryType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="Polygon" type="uci:PolygonType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the footprint boundary as a ground/surface polygon, any part of which can be reached given the remaining endurance.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Ellipse" type="uci:LocatedEllipseType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the footprint boundary as a ground/surface polygon, any part of which can be reached given the remaining endurance.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ByRequirementPolicyType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ApprovalPolicyBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Requirement" type="uci:RequirementTriggerType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Requirements for which the policy applies.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ByResultPolicyType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ApprovalPolicyBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Result" type="uci:PlanningByResultTriggerType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the result conditions.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ByTriggerPolicyType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ApprovalPolicyBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="TriggerSource" type="uci:PlanningTriggerSourceEnum">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the initiator of the replan; the source that detected the sibling Trigger (if applicable).</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Trigger" type="uci:PlanningByCaseTriggerType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates an initial "by case" trigger for autonomous mission planning functions.  This element is required when the sibling TriggerSource indicates an autonomous replanning trigger.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="ByTriggerByResultPolicy" type="uci:ByResultPolicyType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>This element allows the trigger approval policy to be overridden based on the planning activity result.  If multiple instances are given, each should be of a different result type as indicated by the child Result element.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CalibrationID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CallSignType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates an associated call sign.  Multiple call signs are allowed for different communications systems, registries or other reasons.  The use of ForeignKeyType is an indication that call signs aren't universally defined and come from multiple systems/sources but they are expected to be unique within the context of their source.  For a call sign in UCI, the CallSignType.Key element indicates the call sign itself and the CallSignType.SystemName element indicates the system that assigned it and ensures it is unique.  For example, a call sign that came from a Link-16 system might have Key=Phantom01 and SystemName=Link16Voice.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ForeignKeyType" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CannotComplyType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Reason" type="uci:CannotComplyEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the reason why an action (Task, [Capability]Command, [SupportingCapability]Command, [Capability]Activity, etc.) was rejected, interrupted, unallocated, failed and/or generally can't be completed satisfactorily.  See enumeration annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Description" type="uci:VisibleString1024Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a human-readable text description of the reason for the "can't comply".</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AssociatedID" type="uci:ID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an ID corresponding to another object that is associated with or the cause of the "can't comply".</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityAssessmentActionType" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="PlannedTaskID" type="uci:TaskID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Estimated Capability usage (in terms of Tasks) at the specified assessment position.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EstimatedCapabilityCommand" type="uci:CapabilityCommandBaseType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates estimated Capability usage (in terms of Capability commands) at the specified assessment position.  This element is of an abstract polymorphic extension type; the CapabilityCommandBaseType PET (PolymorphicExtensionType) used here is an abstract base type that is the parent type for Capability category specific child types.  This abstract element must be replaced by a Capability category-specific type to be valid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EstimatedSupportingCapabilityCommand" type="uci:SupportCapabilityCommandBaseType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates estimated Supporting Capability usage (in terms of Supporting Capability commands) at the specified assessment position.  This element is of an abstract polymorphic extension type; the SupportCapabilityCommandBaseType PET (PolymorphicExtensionType) used here is an abstract base type that is the parent type for Supporting Capability category specific child types.  This abstract element must be replaced by a Supporting Capability category-specific type to be valid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityAssessmentActivityDetailsType" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ActivityApproved" type="xs:boolean">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This flag, when set to true, indicates that estimated activity is for a task / command which has been either accepted by the subsystem or is on the mission plan, in an approved state, and is scheduled to be commanded to the subsystem. When this flag is false, it indicates that the estimated activity is a proposed task / command and is associated with an ad hoc task or command given in a CapabilityAssessmentRequest message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Activity" type="uci:CapabilityAssessmentActivityType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contains either the estimated activity details of the capability or the estimated capability details for supporting capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityAssessmentActivityType" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="EstimatedCapabilityActivity" type="uci:ActivityBaseType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates estimated Capability usage (in terms of Capability Activity report) at the specified assessment position. The ActivityBaseType used for this element should be replaced by a Capability category-specific type to be valid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EstimatedSupportingCapabilityReport" type="uci:SupportCapabilityStatusBaseType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates estimated Supporting Capability usage (in terms of Supporting Capability status) at the specified assessment position. The SupportCapabilityStatusBaseType used for this element should be replaced by a Supporting Capability category-specific type to be valid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityAssessmentDataType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The results of an assessment that determines capability usage during along a RoutePlan.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="SystemID" type="uci:SystemID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of the System to which the assessment applies.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Assessment" type="uci:CapabilityAssessmentPointType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A Capability assessment is a position along the assessed RoutePlan coupled with the estimated Capability use from both a command (Tasks and Capability commands) and status (Activity) perspective.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityAssessmentPointType" uci:version="003.001.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A point and/or point along a RoutePlan at which a capability is expected to be used by a System.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="InertialState" type="uci:InertialStateRelativeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the approximate future geospatial position of the System and time at which the sibling Command or Commands begin, for assessment purposes only.  The position reflects the System's future progress along its planned route at the time of the command, and does not imply the System will navigate to that location.  When multiple sibling Command or Commands are given, this element indicates the time and position of the first in the sequence. Position data may be specified as either a geospatial point or relative to a separately defined reference frame.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RoutePlanReference" type="uci:RoutePlanReferencesType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>If the sibling InertialState is in reference to a RoutePlan, this element indicates the RoutePlan containing that point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Action" type="uci:CapabilityAssessmentActionType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a planned Task or estimated [Capability]Command assessed to occur at the sibling Position.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ActivityDetails" type="uci:CapabilityAssessmentActivityDetailsType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contains a list of estimated activities.  Each activity contains a flag to indicate whether this activity has been approved vs a not yet approved activity being included in the assessment.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityBaseType" abstract="true" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AvailableMDF" type="uci:MDF_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Mission Data File (MDF) that is available for use.  All MDFs currently available for use should be listed.  An MDF is a collection of data, organized into files, that is used to tailor, configure or otherwise affect the behavior of a Capability.  MDFs can vary from mission to mission or even by phases of a single mission.  They are generally developed in pre-mission planning and are unique for each Subsystem model.  Their content is not standardized by UCI, but the mechanism for advertising their availability and activating them is.  Their content is abstracted by the UCI concept of Capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCommandBaseType" abstract="true" uci:version="002.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The base type for all [Capability]Command messages.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:CommandBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the Capability instance being commanded.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Ranking" type="uci:CapabilityCommandRankingType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the command's rank relative to other commands and rank-related overrides and tailoring.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="TemporalConstraints" type="uci:CapabilityCommandTemporalConstraintsType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates temporal constraints for the command.  If omitted, the command should be scheduled immediately while satisfying other applicable constraints.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="OverrideRejection" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates that the Command shall be performed even though it was previously rejected.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Traceability" type="uci:TraceabilityType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>C2 sources work at different levels of detail and abstraction, progressing from what to do to how specifically to do it.  Inputs from abstract tasking sources and Tasks can be decomposed into discrete, actionable Tasks and/or Capability commands.  This element provides traceability to the external tasking or Task from which this Capability command was derived.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Classification" type="uci:SecurityInformationType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>This element provides the classification label that the Subsystem should place on messages and other data resulting from the Command.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCommandFrequencyType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RF_ProfileID" type="uci:RF_ProfileID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an additional RF_Profile which further constrains the allowed frequencies to be used for the Command.  The allowed frequencies for the Command are the intersection of the allowed frequencies in this RF_Profile, RF_Profiles imposed by RF_ControlCommand on the associated Capability, MDF settings, threat avoidance, etc.  If the sibling RF_ControlOverride is set to TRUE, this command is only constrained by the RF_Profiles listed here.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RF_ControlOverride" type="xs:boolean">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an override of all RF_ControlCommand and/or RF_Profile constraints for the Command and the associated Capability.  The Capability is free to use its full frequency spectrum with no limitations.  This element is only accepted from a Primary Controller (as defined in the ControlRequest message).  In the event it is sent by another controller, the command is rejected.  If an RF Profile is specified and this value is TRUE, the command overrides RF_Profiles specified by the RF_ControlCommand and only uses those RF_Profiles listed in this command.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCommandRankingType" uci:version="000.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Rank" type="uci:ComparableRankingType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the rank of the Command amongst other Commands and Tasks.  For a given "network" or family of Services, it is assumed that all creators of Commands and Tasks will use Rank in a consistent and coordinated fashion.  Then, implementers can use Rank to determine the relative importance of all their Command and Task inputs.  Rank does not dictate temporal ordering; it gives guidance on which Commands and Tasks to favor when resources are limited.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="InterruptOtherActivities" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates whether (TRUE) or not (FALSE) this command should interrupt other scheduled (currently executing, inevitable or future) activities in order to satisfy this command, regardless of consequences such as loss of the interrupted activity's output products.  This element is interpreted depending on whether the command source is a primary or secondary controller.  Secondary controllers can only interrupt activities of lower Rank; when this element is FALSE the command is "nice" in that it allows activities of lower Rank to complete.  Primary controllers interrupt activities of any Rank from any controller when this element is TRUE and defer to Rank when FALSE.  Omission of this optional element is equivalent to FALSE.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityPrecedenceOverride" type="xs:unsignedInt" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a temporary override value for Capability precedence for the Capability associated with the command.  The temporary override applies for this command instance only.  Capability precedence is generally controlled via the associated [Capability]SettingsCommand message.  Only primary controllers are allowed to change or override Capability precedence.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCommandStatusBaseType" abstract="true" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The base type for all [Capability]CommandStatus messages.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:CommandStatusBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Activity" type="uci:ResultingActivityType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates an Activity that satisfies the associated Command. This element is required when the sibling State element indicates ACCEPTED.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCommandTemporalConstraintsType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="TemporalCriticality" type="uci:SchedulingCriticalityEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates temporal scheduling of the Command is critical in order for the Command's results to be useful.  This element amplifies the sibling elements by explicitly stating which *must* be met.  If omitted, scheduling the command to satisfy the sibling elements isn't critical; the sibling element can be used as guidance if given but the command should still be scheduled even if they aren't all satisfied.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="StartTimeWindow" type="uci:DateTimeRangeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the start time window for the command, including repetition.  The beginning of the range represents a start-no-earlier-than time.  The end of the range represents a start-no-later-than time.  If omitted, the command should be scheduled for immediate start within other scheduling constraints.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="WindowOnFirstOnly" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates whether the sibling StartTimeWindow applies to only the first command of a repeating series (TRUE) or all (first and all subsequent) commands in a repeating series (FALSE).  Optional for non-repeating commands.  Equivalent to TRUE when omitted for repeating commands.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EndTimeWindow" type="uci:DateTimeRangeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the end time window for the command, including repetition.  The beginning of the range represents an end-no-earlier-than time.  The end of the range represents an end-no-later-than time.  If omitted, end time is left to the discretion of the command recipient.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityConfigurationID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type for UCI IDs that correspond to System Loadouts.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityConfigurationType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type defines a configuration option for a set of independently configurable subsystems.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ConfigurationID" type="uci:CapabilityConfigurationID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Identifier that refers to this specific system loadout.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SubsystemID" type="uci:SubsystemID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a C2 relevant Subsystem of the System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a C2 relevant Capability of the System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityConsentRequestStatusBaseType" abstract="true" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The base type for all [Capability]ConsentRequestStatusMDTs.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:RequestStatusBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="ApprovalState" type="uci:ApprovalStatusEnum">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the current state of the *ConsentRequest.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="ApprovalRejectedReason" type="uci:CannotComplyType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the reason when an approval is rejected.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaDataType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Contains the detailed capability coverage area resultant data.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the sensor Capability instance that this CCA is associated with. Multiple instances of this element should only be given when each Capability is of the same homogeneous type and multiple instances are being employed to complete the sensor capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Basis" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaBasisEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element describes the basis of the dataset used an/or being used for the SCA. A CCA may be generated dynamically or statically.  A CCA calculated periodically or upon changing conditions in a dynamic dataset (current platform position, measured wind, etc,) results in a dynamic SCA.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaFactor" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaFactorEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="5">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates limiting factors used in computing the Capability Coverage Area. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ValidTime" type="uci:TimeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the time for which the sibling Region or Regions is valid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SensorPosition" type="uci:Point3D_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the geospatial position of the sensor and time at which the sibling Region or Regions is valid. This should take into account sensor installation relative to the platform. This is calculated at the sibling ValidTime time.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SensorOrientation" type="uci:OrientationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the orientation of the sensor and time at which the sibling Region or Regions is valid. This is relative to true north. It should take into account platform orientation and sensor installation relative to the platform. This is calculated at the sibling ValidTime time.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Region" type="uci:ZoneType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Defines a region in space where the Capability is applicable.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type that refers to a specific operator annotation.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaMDT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents the characteristics that identify and describe a Capability Coverage Area.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaID" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is an ID to uniquely identify the Capability Coverage Area (CCA).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestID" type="uci:RequestID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This optional field is to be used when this message is in response to a CapabilityCoverageAreaRequest message. It will identify the ID of the request message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaDetails" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaDataType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contains the detailed capability coverage area resultant data.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaMT" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See the annotation in the associated message for an overall description of the message and this type.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:MessageType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="ObjectState" type="uci:ObjectStateEnum" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the state of the internal software object that the data in the message is based upon.  The state of the software object is determined by the publisher.  The publisher could be the original/master publisher or an intermediary re-publisher such as a persistence/data manager.  This element is not a commanded CRUD operation.  It is not an indication of the state of an instance of a message.  It is not a mechanism for partial, incremental, or fragmentary publication of a message.  It is a signal from a publisher to subscribers.  For REMOVED state, in particular, some subscribers will mirror the publisher and treat the message as if the object no longer exists, but others, such as mission recorders, might not (in order to support retaining historical records of activity, for example).</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="MessageData" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaMDT">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>This represents the characteristics that identify and describe a Capability Coverage Area.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestDataType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Contains the detailed capability coverage area resultant data.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the sensor Capability instance that this CCA is associated with. Multiple instances of this element should only be given when each Capability is associated with the same subsystem and multiple instances are being employed to complete the sensor capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaFactor" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaFactorEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="5">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates limiting factors used in computing the Capability Coverage Area. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SensorPosition" type="uci:Point3D_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the geospatial position of the sensor and time at which the sibling Region or Regions is valid. This should take into account sensor installation relative to the platform. This is calculated at the sibling ValidTime time.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SensorOrientation" type="uci:OrientationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the orientation of the sensor and time at which the sibling Region or Regions is valid. This is relative to true north. It should take into account platform orientation and sensor installation relative to the platform. This is calculated at the sibling ValidTime time.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestMDT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents the characteristics that identify and describe a Capability Coverage Area Request.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:RequestBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="RequestFrequencyType" type="uci:RepeatEnum">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates whether this request for Capability Coverage Area is a request for a single reply, or a request for periodic calculations with a corresponding request to cease periodic calculations.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="ResultsInCapabilityCoverageAreaMessage" type="xs:boolean">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>If TRUE this element specifies that the response to this message should be a CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatus and a CapabilityCoverageArea message. If FALSE the CapabilityCoverageArea data that is generated should be bundled inside the response. In some cases there isn't a native message and the results can only be reported via CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatus.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestDetails" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestDataType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Contains the detailed capability coverage area resultant data.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestMT" uci:version="001.001.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See the annotation in the associated message for an overall description of the message and this type.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:MessageType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="MessageData" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestMDT">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>This represents the characteristics that identify and describe a Capability Coverage Area Request.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatusMDT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents the characteristics that identify and describe a Capability Coverage Area Request.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:RequestStatusBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="CapabilityCoverageAreaDetails" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaDataType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Contains the detailed capability coverage area resultant data. Included in this message if :ResultsInCapabilityCoverageAreaMessage in the requesting message is set to FALSE.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatusMT" uci:version="002.001.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See the annotation in the associated message for an overall description of the message and this type.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:MessageType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="MessageData" type="uci:CapabilityCoverageAreaRequestStatusMDT">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>This represents the characteristics that identify and describe a Capability Coverage Area Request.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityCrossReferenceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Capability associated with this Component.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SupportCapabilityID" type="uci:SupportCapabilityID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of the Support Capability corresponding to this message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="CapabilityDeclarationBaseType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The capability base type containing all fields required for all capabilities.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of this Capability.  This ID is used to invoke the Capability via Command messages, report its activity via Activity messages, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="DomainPairing" type="uci:EnvironmentPairingEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="48">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the domain/environment which the Capability is employed from and the "target" domain/environment.  See enumerated type annotations for further details. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ProductOutput" type="uci:ProductOutputType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Product that can be an output of the Capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PackageOperation" type="uci:PackageOperationEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates if and how the operation of the Capability can be coordinated with a partner Capability of another System in a multi-System Package. Omitting this element is equivalent to the enumerate NO_PACKAGE_OPERATION. The actual Package configuration including per-Capability partnering between Systems is given in the PackageStatus message. For example, a System that can perform cooperative geolocation could advertise with this element in the ESM_Capability message. A System that can navigate in a swarm with other Systems could advertise with this element in the FlightCapability message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
