				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Rates" type="uci:ConeAngleRatesType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the rates of the collected Cone Angle Measurements</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConeAngleUncertaintyType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Variance" type="uci:ConeAngleVarianceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the variance associated with the collected Cone Angle measurements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Covariance" type="uci:ConeAngleCovarianceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the covariance associated with the collected Cone Angle measurements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConeAngleVarianceRatesType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CosconeY_Rate" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The variance of the rate of change of the inner product of the target pointing vector with the sensor Y-axis. In the case of linear arrays, the Y-axis is the same as the orientation of the linear array. In the case of two-dimensional arrays, the Y-axis is the "Horizontal" axis, making it coarsely equivalent to the variance of the Azimuth rate with units of per second squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CosconeZ_Rate" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The variance of the rate of change of the inner product of the target pointing vector with the sensor Z axis. This term is only used for two-dimensional arrays. The Z-axis is the "Down" axis of the array, perpendicular to both the X-axis (boresight) and the Y-axis (horizontal), making it coarsely equivalent to the variance of the Elevation rate with units of per second squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConeAngleVarianceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CosconeY" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The variance of the inner product of the target pointing vector and the Y-axis of the face of the array. In the case of linear arrays, the Y-axis is the same as the orientation of the linear array. In the case of two-dimensional arrays, the Y-axis is the "Horizontal" axis, coarsely equivalent to Azimuth. This term is unitless.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CosconeZ" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The variance of the inner product of the target pointing vector and the Z-axis of the face of the array. The Z-axis is the "Down" axis of the array, perpendicular to both the X-axis (boresight) and the Y-axis (horizontal), coarsely equivalent to Elevation. This term is only used for two-dimensional arrays and is unitless.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Rates" type="uci:ConeAngleVarianceRatesType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the variance of the rates associated with the collected Cone Angle measurements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConeType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type describes the geometric parameters of a cone.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="HalfAngle" type="uci:AngleHalfType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Angle in radians from the Attitude projection acting as the cone's centerline to the edge of the cone.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Range" type="uci:DistanceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the range from the vertex to the end of the cone.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConfigurationParameterCategoryType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Category" type="uci:VisibleString32Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies the category of a set of parameters. Parameters of the same category could be grouped together in a generic UI.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Parameter" type="uci:ConfigurationParameterType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Name refers to the name of the parameter, value represents the current value of the parameter and default represents the default value of the parameter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConfigurationParameterRangeType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Min" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The minimum value for a FLOAT or INT configuration parameter value.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Max" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The maximum value for a FLOAT or INT configuration parameter value.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConfigurationParameterType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ParameterID" type="uci:ParameterID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Identifier that uniquely refers to configuration parameters.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Name" type="uci:VisibleString256Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Name of the configuration parameter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Value" type="uci:VisibleString256Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Current value of the configuration parameter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="DefaultValue" type="uci:VisibleString256Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Default value of the configuration parameter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="UserModifiable" type="xs:boolean">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element can indicate whether a configuration parameter's value is allowed to be directly modified by the operator.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Type" type="uci:MessageElementDataTypeEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Defines the atomic type that "value" should be interpreted as.  Useful for distinguishing between floating point numbers and integers.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Units" type="uci:VisibleString32Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This value should be populated for any configuration parameter that has units.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="TypeSpecificRestrictions" type="uci:ConfigurationParameterValueRestrictionsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Optional list of valid options for this parameters.  This can either be an enum (list of string values) or a range for numbers.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ObjectClass" type="uci:MissionEnvironmentObjectClassType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Defines the class of object for which these parameters support individual settings on specific instances. When specifying the override, both the ID and value of the specific object instance are specified. If an object class is not specified, specific object class settings are not supported.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MissionEnvironment" type="uci:EmptyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>If present, indicates this may be used as in input to MissionEnvironmentOverride.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConfigurationParameterValueRestrictionsType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="Enumeration" type="uci:VisibleString32Type" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies the list of supported enumerate values.  This value should be populated if and only if the Type element has been set to ENUM.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Range" type="uci:ConfigurationParameterRangeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies the range of the parameter.  This value only applies to a parameter type of FLOAT and INT.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConfigureInteroperabilityReportType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ConfigureInteroperabilityReportIndex" type="xs:unsignedInt">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element contains the value of ConfigureInteroperabilityReport_Index (CIR_Index) that a transmitting subsystem will use to describe the transmission associated with the Activity. The RF_Report message describes the transmission and refers to the CIR_Index. Blanking functions send blanking commands by CIR_Index. ResourceAllocator generates the index in a an incrementing fashion 1, 2, 3... at resource allocation time, assuring that identifiers are unique across resource allocations. CIR_Indices may be re-used after an allocation has been de-allocated.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RF_Transmission" type="uci:RF_TransmissionType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>RF transmit parameters associated with the sibling CIR Index. This informs the recipient subsystem that the associate CIR Index (and/or Activity ID) is transmitting at a specific frequency range, at a specific ERP, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConflictLocationType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ConflictPoint" type="uci:Point2D_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The point along the route where the first intersection occurs with the conflicting object.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ConflictedRouteSegment" type="uci:RoutePlanSegmentReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The first route segment in the route where the intersection occurs with the conflicting object.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConflictType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ConflictID" type="uci:ID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Unique ID for this conflict.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ConflictingObjectID" type="uci:ID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The object that is causing the conflicted system to be in a contingency state.  This item is optional if and only if the conflicting object is not known.  For example, when a FAILED Task is received by a parent system (FAILED task state is used to escalate the issue to the parent node).  This may generate a contingency alert at the parent node where the conflicting object is unknown.  In other words, a parent system receives a FAILED Task, but doesn't necessarily know why the Task has failed.  This could cause the parent system to generate an alert with a sibling AffectedRequirement without a ConflictingObject.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ConflictLocation" type="uci:ConflictLocationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is the first location along the planned route where the conflict will occur. This data is optional and primarily applies to conflicts with pop-up threats or zones where the conflict occurs at a future point along the route. It is not expected that this data will be populated for Capability and other System failures which affect a System's ability to perform its assigned Tasking. The primary intent of this data is for display purposes to provide operator SA.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ConflictTime" type="uci:DateTimeRangeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the time window in which the conflict is expected to occur.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AffectedRequirements" type="uci:RequirementsReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the ID of Requirement or Requirements which can no longer be performed due to the effect of the conflicting object. IDs are given if and only if they can no longer be accomplished with the current mission plans. As a result, a contingency generated by a pop-up threat (or other environment change) might be handled via a simple reroute which does not affect any Requirements. Alternatively, if a Capability fails, affected Requirements are more likely known and should be included in this element.  A Service would only list the Requirements if the Service KNOWS there is a problem with it.  For example, a Service should not populate this value with all Tasks in an affected MissionPlan simply because it is uncertain as to the effect of a reroute on the associated Tasks.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainedEntityType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="SurrogateIdentifier" type="uci:RadarEmitterIdentityType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A surrogate allows a similar type that is known to the existing system to take precedence over the reported or cross referenced ELNOT/DIEQP type. For threat ambiguities, the surrogate information takes precedence over all of the individual threat types.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MissionPlanningWeightingFactor" type="uci:PercentType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element defines a percentage that mission planning services should use to decrease the effectiveness of a threat. A value of 100 percent means that threat template data should be used for this threat exactly as defined in configuration data. A value of 0 percent means that the threat should not be considered at all. If this element is not populated then it is assumed to be 100 percent.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MissionPlanningCandidate" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Consider this entity a valid entity for mission planning related services. "false" indicates intentional ignore or lack of configuration parameters in the system for this entity.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EntityID" type="uci:EntityID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the unique identifier of the entity.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainedEntryExitType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="GateIndex" type="xs:unsignedInt" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Turns an edge into a "gate" through which bi-directional passage of a system is allowable. This is intended to be used with polygon zones only. The index is 0 based, with the 0 index representing the edge between the first and second points. The last index (number of points - 1) refers to the edge between the last and first point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainedOpLineType" uci:version="000.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Category" type="uci:OpLineCategoryEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the type of operational line. Includes amplification field for LINK 16 J3.0 Reference: MIL-STD-6016.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OpLineID" type="uci:OpLineID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the unique identifier of the operational line.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainedOpPointType" uci:version="003.001.003.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Category" type="uci:OpPointCategoriesType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the type of operational point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CategoryUniqueData" type="uci:OpPointCategoriesUniqueDataType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element contains any parameters specific to sibling Category.  Not all OpPoint Categories require unique parameters, only the ones that do are in OpPointCategoriesUniqueDataType.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OpPointID" type="uci:OpPointID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the unique identifier of the operational point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainedOpVolumeType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="VolumeCategory" type="uci:OpZoneCategoryEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the type of volume.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CategoryUniqueData" type="uci:OpZoneCategoryType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element contains any parameters specific to sibling VolumeCategory.  Not all OpZoneCategoryEnum values require unique parameters, only the ones that do are in OpZoneCategoryType.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OpVolumeID" type="uci:OpVolumeID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the unique identifier of the volume.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainedOpZoneType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ZoneCategory" type="uci:OpZoneCategoryEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the type of zone.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CategoryUniqueData" type="uci:OpZoneCategoryType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element contains any parameters specific to sibling ZoneCategory.  Not all OpZoneCategoryEnum values require unique parameters, only the ones that do are in OpZoneCategoryType.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OpZoneID" type="uci:OpZoneID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates the unique identifier of the zone.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainingPlanPartsType" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:PlanPartsType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="CommAllocation" type="uci:CommAllocationPartsType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates applicable CommAllocation parts.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ConstrainingPlansType" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ConstrainingPlanType" type="uci:PlanTypeEnum" maxOccurs="11">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a type of Plan that constrains the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan (OrbitPlan, RoutePlan, etc.).  A *PlanCommand that includes the ancestor ReplanReason element occurs only during live and/or autonomous operations.  During live/autonomous operations, a constrained *PlanCommand is understood to be constrained by the current/latest *Plan.  Therefore, a *PlanCommand with a ReplanReason can indicate a constraining *Plan without listing the unique ID; this element alone is sufficient to identify the constraining plan.  During non-live/non-autonomous operations, when the sibling ReplanReason element is not given, the sibling *Plan element corresponding to this element should include the specific *Plan unique ID and constraining details corresponding to this element.  If omitted during non-live operations, the corresponding sibling *Plan element should also be omitted. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MissionPlanID" type="uci:MissionPlanID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a MissionPlan that constrains the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan (OrbitPlan, RoutePlan, etc.).  For a given kind of System, *Plans might always be created in a particular order.  For other Systems, *Plans might initially be created in a particular order with updates in another order or orders.  For other Systems, the order might be completely variable depending on conditions, priority, operator preference, etc.  Therefore, any kind of *Plan might be a constraint on the creation or update of any other kind.  When omitted, the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan isn't constrained by a MissionPlan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="TaskPlan" type="uci:TaskPlanConstraintType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a TaskPlan that constrains the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan (OrbitPlan, RoutePlan, etc.).  For a given kind of System, *Plans might always be created in a particular order.  For other Systems, *Plans might initially be created in a particular order with updates in another order or orders.  For other Systems, the order might be completely variable depending on conditions, priority, operator preference, etc.  Therefore, any kind of *Plan might be a constraint on the creation or update of any other kind.  When omitted, the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan isn't constrained by a TaskPlan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RoutePlan" type="uci:RoutePlanConstraintType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a RoutePlan that constrains the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan (OrbitPlan, RoutePlan, etc.).  For a given kind of System, *Plans might always be created in a particular order.  For other Systems, *Plans might initially be created in a particular order with updates in another order or orders.  For other Systems, the order might be completely variable depending on conditions, priority, operator preference, etc.  Therefore, any kind of *Plan might be a constraint on the creation or update of any other kind.  When omitted, the existing/referenced/commanded *Plan isn't constrained by a RoutePlan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
