	<xs:complexType name="ExternalRequestedDataID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type for UCI IDs that corresponds to an external data record.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ExtrapolatedEntityType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="EntityID" type="uci:EntityID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of the extrapolated Entity.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Kinematics" type="uci:KinematicsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates extrapolated or otherwise estimated kinematics for the Entity.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FacilityIdentificationType" uci:version="000.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides identification of an object associated with the Air Force Space Command.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="SiteIdentifier" type="xs:unsignedShort">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the site ID.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SensorIdentifier" type="xs:unsignedShort">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the ID of the sensor.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ForeignFacilityKey" type="uci:ForeignKeyType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The ID for this facility is associated with a key that exists in another system, protocol, or network.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FalseAlarmType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CFAR_Threshold" type="uci:DecibelType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element defines the Constant False Alarm Rate specified as the SNR (in dB) above which a target must meet or exceed (e.g., a pixel in an MTI range-Doppler image) in order to be declared a detection.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FalseAlarmLevel" type="xs:unsignedByte" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the acceptable false alarm level for outputs from the Command.  This element can be used to adjust the sensitivity of the Capability in initiating tracks and reporting Entities.  Lower numbers indicate a more restrictive capability, i.e. fewer results. See FalseAlarmLevelUpperLimit for further details.  If omitted tracking range selection is left to the discretion of the command recipient. A common range is [0,15].</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FaultDataType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Reports the data that caused the fault.  Contains key/value pair along with format and unit indicated for the data value.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Key" type="uci:VisibleString1024Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is the amplifying data for the fault indicating what the value represents.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Value" type="uci:VisibleString1024Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is the amplifying data for the fault indicating what the value is.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Format" type="uci:VisibleString64Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The format of the value e.g. string, float, int, ...</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Units" type="uci:VisibleString64Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The unit for the value e.g. Deg F, Hz, ...</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FaultID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>A fault id should be unique for every fault.  DescriptiveLabel should have as much detailed information as possible about the fault.  The name should be something short that fits nicely in a display where as description can be long and help the user really understand the fault.  For example, DescriptiveLabel - "Temp Over Heat in Oil pump engine 1 inlet", Name - "Temp Over Heat Eng 1".</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Name" type="uci:VisibleString64Type" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>A short name used to display to an operator if the fault code is not meaningful to a human and in the case where the fault description is too long.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FaultMDT" uci:version="000.000.001.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents a fault for the system.  Intended to be used by services such as a health monitoring service for centralized fault management and fault isolation of a system.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:DataRecordBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="FaultInformation" type="uci:SubsystemFaultType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Contains the fault information for the message.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FaultMT" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This represents a fault for the system.  Intended to be used by services such as a health monitoring service for centralized fault management and fault isolation of a system.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:MessageType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="ObjectState" type="uci:ObjectStateEnum" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the state of the internal software object that the data in the message is based upon.  The state of the software object is determined by the publisher.  The publisher could be the original/master publisher or an intermediary re-publisher such as a persistence/data manager.  This element is not a commanded CRUD operation.  It is not an indication of the state of an instance of a message.  It is not a mechanism for partial, incremental, or fragmentary publication of a message.  It is a signal from a publisher to subscribers.  For REMOVED state, in particular, some subscribers will mirror the publisher and treat the message as if the object no longer exists, but others, such as mission recorders, might not (in order to support retaining historical records of activity, for example).</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="MessageData" type="uci:FaultMDT">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Reports a fault for the system.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FieldOfRegardExtentsType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Azimuth and elevation extents (ranges).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AzimuthExtent" type="uci:AnglePairType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Min and max azimuth extents.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ElevationExtent" type="uci:AnglePairType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Min and max elevation extents.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FieldOfRegardType" uci:version="003.001.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type identifies the physical limits of a sensor's Field of Regard (FOR).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Field" type="uci:SensorFieldOfRegardType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the field shape of the field of regard.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="NearRange" type="uci:SlantRangeConstraintsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The MAX is the advertised range (SPEC), and the MIN is the absolute edge of the capability of the Radar. Taking a SAR between the MIN and MAX indicates degraded quality.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FarRange" type="uci:SlantRangeConstraintsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The MIN is the advertised range (SPEC), and the MAX is the absolute edge of the capability of the Radar. Taking a SAR between the MIN and MAX indicates degraded quality.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Mnemonic" type="uci:VisibleString256Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Mnemonic representation of the Field of Regard.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="TypeOfFOR" type="uci:FieldOfRegardTypeEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The type this Field of Regard is for.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FileFormatType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="MIME" type="uci:MIME_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Describes the digital format of a file.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="NonMIME" type="uci:ForeignKeyType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a file format that is not registered as a MIME type.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FileHeaderRevA_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="FileSecurityClassification" type="uci:NITF_FileSecurityClassificationType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. REQUIRED FIELD. NITF File Header FSCLAS from ACTDF Overall Classification section Classification field. This field shall contain a valid value representing the classification level of the entire file. Valid values are T (=Top Secret), S (=Secret), C (=Confidential), R (=Restricted), U (=Unclassified).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationSystem" type="uci:CharOrSpacePairsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCLSY from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Security Classification System. This field shall contain valid values indicating the national or multinational security system used to classify the file. Country Codes per FIPS PUB 10-4 shall be used to indicate national security systems. The designator "XN" is for classified data generated by a component using NATO security system marking guidance. This code is outside the FIPS 10-4 document listing, and was selected to not duplicate that document's existing codes. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no security classification system applies to the file.  NOTE: If FSCLAS is T, S, C, or R, then FSCLSY must be populated with a valid code for the security classification system used.  NOTE: If any of the following fields are populated with anything other than spaces, then FileClassificationSystem (FSCLSY) must be populated with a valid code for the security classification system used: FSCODE, FSREL, FSDCTP, FSDCDT, FSDCXM, FSDG, FSDGDT, FSCLTX, FSCATP, FSCAUT, FSCRSN, FSSRDT, and FSCTLN.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileCodewords" type="uci:NITF_CodewordsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCODE from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Codewords. This field shall contain a valid indicator of the security compartments associated with the file. Values include zero to four of the digraphs (2 CAP alphas) found in MIL-STD-2500x Table A-4, separated by a single ECS space (0x20): The selection of a relevant set of codewords is application specific. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no codewords apply to the file.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileControlAndHandling" type="uci:NITF_ControlAndHandlingType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCTLH from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Control and Handling. This field shall contain valid additional security control and/or handling instructions (caveats) associated with the file. Values include one of the digraphs found in MIL-STD-2500x Table A-4, where the OTHER CODEWORDS row apparently allows other than strictly 2 CAP alphas, including ECS-A special characters - that digraph may indicate single or multiple caveats. The selection of a relevant caveat or caveats is application specific. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no additional control and handling instructions apply to the file.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileReleasingInstructions" type="uci:NITF_ReleasingInstructionsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSREL from ACTDF Overall Classification section Releasable To. This field shall contain a valid list of country and/or multilateral entity codes to which countries and/or multilateral entities the file is authorized for release. Valid items in the list are one to 7 country codes as found in FIPS PUB 10-4 separated by a single ECS space (0x20). If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no file release instructions apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDeclassificationType" type="uci:NITF_DeclassificationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSDCTP from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Declassification Type. This field shall contain a valid indicator of the type of security declassification or downgrading instructions which apply to the file. Valid values are DD (=declassify on a specific date), DE (=declassify upon occurrence of an event), GD (=downgrade to a specified level on a specific date), GE (=downgrade to a specified level upon occurrence of an event), O (=OADR), and X (= exempt from automatic declassification). If this field is not present, it implies that no file security declassification or downgrading instructions apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDeclassificationDate" type="uci:NITF_DateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSDCDT from ACTDF Overall Classification section Declass Date. This field shall indicate the date (CCYYMMDD) on which a file is to be declassified if the value in File Declassification Type is DD. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no file declassification date applies.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDeclassificationExemption" type="uci:NITF_DeclassificationExemptionType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSDCXM from ACTDF Declass Exception. This field shall indicate the reason the file is exempt from automatic declassification if the value in File Declassification Type is X. Valid values are X1 to X8 and X251 to X259. X1 to X8 correspond to the declassification exemptions found in DOD 5200.1-R, paragraphs 4-202b(1) to (8) for material exempt from the 10-year rule. X251 to X259 correspond to the declassification exemptions found in DOD 5200.1-R, paragraphs 4-301a(1) to (9) for permanently valuable material exempt from the 25-year declassification system. If this field is all ESC spaces (0x20), it shall imply that a file declassification exemption does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDowngrade" type="uci:NITF_DowngradeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSDG from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Downgrade. This field shall indicate the classification level to which a file is to be downgraded if the values in File Declassification Type are GD or GE. Valid values are S (=Secret), C (=Confidential), R (=Restricted). If this field contains an ECS space (0x20), it shall imply that file security downgrading does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDowngradeDate" type="uci:NITF_DateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSDGDT from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Downgrade Date. This field shall indicate the date (CCYYMMDD) on which a file is to be downgraded if the value in File Declassification Type is GD. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that a file security downgrading date does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationText" type="uci:VisibleLatin1String43Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCLTX from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Classification Text. This field shall be used to provide additional information about file classification to include identification of a declassification or downgrading event if the values in File Declassification Type are DE or GE. It may also be used to identify multiple classification sources and/or any other special handling rules. Values are user defined free text. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that additional information about file classification does not apply.Note that chars in the range 0xA0 to 0xFF should be avoided.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationAuthorityType" type="uci:NITF_ClassificationAuthorityType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCATP from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Classification Authority Type. This field indicates the type of authority used to classify the file. Valid values are O (= original classification authority), D (= derivative from a single source), and M (= derivative from multiple sources). If this field contains an ECS space (0x20), it implies that file classification authority type does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationAuthority" type="uci:VisibleLatin1String40Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>File Classification Authority aka File Classified By. See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCAUT from ACTDF Overall Classification section Classified By. This field shall identify the classification authority for the file dependent upon the value in File Classification Authority Type. Values are user defined free text which should contain the following information: original classification authority name and position or personal identifier if the value in File Classification Authority Type is O; title of the document or security classification guide used to classify the file if the value in File Classification Authority Type is D; and Derive-Multiple if the file classification was derived from multiple sources and the value of the FSCATP field is M. In the latter case, the file originator will maintain a record of the sources used in accordance with existing security directives. One of the multiple sources may also be identified in File Classification Text if desired. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no file classification authority applies.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationReason" type="uci:NITF_ClassificationReasonType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCRSN from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Classification Reason. This field contains values indicating the reason for classifying the file. Valid values are A to G. These correspond to the reasons for original classification per E.O. 12958, Section 1.5.(a) to (g). If this field contains an ASCII space (0x20), it means that no file classification reason applies.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileSecuritySourceDate" type="uci:NITF_DateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSSRDT from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Security Source Date in form CCYYMMDD. This field shall indicate the date of the source used to derive the classification of the file. In the case of multiple sources, the date of the most recent source shall be used. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that a file security source date does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileSecurityControlNumber" type="uci:VisibleLatin1String15Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header FSCTLN from ACTDF Overall Classification section File Security Control Number. This field shall contain a valid security control number associated with the file. The format of the security control number shall be in accordance with the regulations governing the appropriate security channel or channels. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no file security control number applies.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OriginatorsName" type="uci:VisibleLatin1String24Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header ONAME from ACTDF Originator. This field shall contain a valid name for the operator who originated the file. If the field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall represent that no operator is assigned responsibility for origination.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OriginatorsPhone" type="uci:VisibleLatin1String18Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>See simple type documentation. NITF File Header OPHONE from ACTDF Originator Phone Number. Default is all spaces if not extant in ACTDF. This field shall contain a valid phone number for the operator who originated the file. If the field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall represent that no phone number is available for the operator assigned responsibility for origination.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="FileHeaderType" uci:version="000.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="CountryCode" type="uci:CountryCodeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>IAW MIL-STD-2500C, Country Codes per FIPS PUB 10-4 are used to indicate national security systems.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SceneSource" type="uci:SAR_CollectionReasonEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element defines the way in which the SAR collection was planned and is part of the NITF header.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OriginatingStationIdentifier" type="uci:AlphanumericPunctuatedString11Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall contain the identification code or name of the originating organization, system, station, or product. It shall not be filled with BCS spaces (0x20).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CollectionIdentifier" type="xs:unsignedInt" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Identifies the specific collection being referenced.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileTitle" type="uci:VisibleString80Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall contain the title of the file or spaces (0x20).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OriginatorsName" type="uci:AlphanumericSpaceString11Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contact information; the name of the file's originator.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OriginatorsPhone" type="uci:NumericSpaceString11Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contact information; the file's originator's phone number.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationSystem" type="uci:FIPS_ClassificationSystemType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall contain valid values indicating the national or multinational security system used to classify the file. Country Codes per FIPS PUB 10-4 shall be used to indicate national security systems. The designator "XN" is for classified data generated by a component using NATO security system marking guidance. This code is outside the FIPS 10-4 document listing, and was selected to not duplicate that document's existing codes. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no security classification system applies to the file.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileSecurityControlNumber" type="uci:VisibleString16Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contains a valid security control number associated with the file.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileControlAndHandling" type="uci:VisibleString3Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall contain valid additional security control and/or handling instructions (caveats) associated with the file. Values include digraphs found in table A-4. The digraph may indicate single or multiple caveats. The selection of a relevant caveat or caveats is application specific. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that no additional control and handling instructions apply to the file.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDeclassificationType" type="uci:FileDeclassificationEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall contain a valid indicator of the type of security declassification or downgrading instructions which apply to the file. Valid values are DD (=declassify on a specific date), DE (=declassify upon occurrence of an event), GD (=downgrade to a specified level on a specific date), GE (=downgrade to a specified level upon occurrence of an event), O (=OADR), and X (= exempt from automatic declassification). If this field is not present, it implies that no file security declassification or downgrading instructions apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileClassificationAuthorityType" type="uci:FileHeaderClassificationAuthorityType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall indicate the type of authority used to classify the file. Valid values are O (= original classification authority), D (= derivative from a single source), and M (= derivative from multiple sources). If this field contains an ECS space (0x20), it shall imply that file classification authority type does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileDowngrade" type="uci:FileHeaderDowngradeStringType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall indicate the classification level to which a file is to be downgraded if the values in File Declassification Type are GD or GE. Valid values are S (=Secret), C (=Confidential), R (= Restricted). If this field contains an ECS space (0x20), it shall imply that file security downgrading does not
apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="FileSecuritySourceDate" type="uci:VisibleString9Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This field shall indicate the date of the source used to derive the classification of the file. In the case of multiple sources, the date of the most recent source shall be used. If this field is all ECS spaces (0x20), it shall imply that a file security source date does not apply.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
