				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_AzElRatesType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AzimuthRate" type="uci:AngleRateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Azimuth rate of measurement with units of radians per sec.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ElevationRate" type="uci:AngleRateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Elevation rate of measurement with units of radians per sec.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_AzElType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AnglePositiveType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest. Azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a global horizontal plane at the observation point. The first line is from the observation point to true north. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the global horizontal plane. An azimuth of zero indicates true north with values increasing clockwise. The terms azimuth and bearing are often used interchangeably but they are technically different. Azimuth values are angles between 0 and 2*Pi radians (0 and 360 degrees) relative to north, in this case true north. Bearing values are a combination of a reference direction (north or south) and an angle between 0 and Pi radians (0 and 180 degrees) towards the east or west direction. For example, an azimuth of Pi/2 radians (90 degrees) is equivalent to a bearing of "North 90 East" or "South 90 East". UCI uses azimuth because it is easier for machine/software use.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest. Elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the horizontal plane, negative when below.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Rates" type="uci:LOS_AzElRatesType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the rates of the collected Azimuth and/or Elevation Measurements</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_CovariancesRatesType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
