				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Bearing" type="uci:AnglePositiveType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the angle from true north to target point measured from a reference point to a second point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the angle between a level plane in the global frame and the line between the two points.  Angles above the plane are positive.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_UncertaintyType" uci:version="001.001.000.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="SlantRangeError" type="uci:DistanceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the error in SlantRange.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AzimuthError" type="uci:AnglePositiveType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the absolute value of the error in Azimuth.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ElevationError" type="uci:AnglePositiveType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the absolute value of the error in Elevation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_VariableA_Type" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Defines a line of sight ray in a world inertial or body frame of reference.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Reference" type="uci:LOS_ReferenceEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies whether the sibling Azimuth and Elevation elements are referenced to a body, NED, or inertial frame.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="uci:AnglePositiveType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the azimuth from an observation point to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in a global horizontal plane at the observation point. The first line is from the observation point to true north. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the global horizontal plane. An azimuth of zero indicates true north with values increasing clockwise. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, azimuth is defined as the angle between two lines in the plane containing the observer's roll and pitch axis (the "RP" plane). The first line is from the observation point along the observer's roll axis or "down the nose" in aircraft terminology. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest projected onto the RP plane. An azimuth of zero indicates directly ahead of the observer ("down the nose") with values increasing clockwise. An azimuth of Pi/2 indicates directly right of the observer ("down the right wing"). The terms azimuth and bearing are often used interchangeably but they are technically different. Azimuth values are angles between 0 and 2*Pi radians (0 and 360 degrees) relative to reference. Bearing values are a combination of a reference direction (north or south) and an angle between 0 and Pi radians (0 and 180 degrees) towards the east or west direction. For example, an azimuth of Pi/2 radians (90 degrees) is equivalent to a bearing of "North 90 East" or "South 90 East". UCI uses azimuth because it is easier for machine/software use.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="uci:AngleType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the elevation from an observation point to a point of interest. When the sibling Reference element indicates INERTIAL, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a global vertical plane at the observation point. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observation point's horizontal plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the horizontal plane, negative when below. When the sibling Reference element indicates BODY, elevation is defined as the angle between two lines in a plane containing the observer's yaw axis. The first line originates from the observation point and extends along the azimuth. The second line is from the observation point to the point of interest. An elevation of zero indicates the point of interest is in the observer's roll-pitch (RP) plane. Elevation values are positive when the point of interest is above the RP plane, negative when below.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_VariableB_Type" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Defines a line of sight ray in a world inertial or body frame of reference.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:LOS_VariableA_Type">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Roll" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the roll component of the Sensor or Subsystem.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Rates" type="uci:LOS_RatesType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the rates at which the LOS is moving.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_VarianceAndCovarianceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Variance" type="uci:LOS_VarianceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the variance associated with the collected LOS measurements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Covariance" type="uci:LOS_CovarianceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the covariance associated with the collected LOS measurements</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_VarianceRatesType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AzimuthRate" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Variance of azimuth angle rate measurement with units of radians squared per sec squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ElevationRate" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Variance of elevation angle rate measurement with units of radians squared per sec squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS_VarianceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Azimuth" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Variance of azimuth angle measurement with units of radians squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Elevation" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Variance of elevation angle measurement with units of radians squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Rates" type="uci:LOS_VarianceRatesType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the variance of the rates associated with the collected Azimuth and/or Elevation measurements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS3D_CovarianceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type contains the 1-sigma covariance of line of sight angles and slant range. The covariance is symmetric and therefore the covariance values expressed here are the minimum set.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RvRv" type="xs:double">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Row 1 Column 1 term of the result matrix. Vertical Vertical angle-angle covariance. As radian is unitless result is unitless.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RvRh" type="xs:double">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Row 1 Column 2 term of the result matrix. Vertical Horizontal angle-angle covariance. As radian is unitless result is unitless.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RhRh" type="xs:double">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Row 2 Column 2 term of the result matrix. Horizontal Horizontal angle-angle covariance. As radian is unitless result is unitless.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PsRv" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Row 1 Column 3 term of the result matrix. Slant Range Vertical position-angle covariance. The result is in units of meters.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PsRh" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Row 2 Column 3 term of the result matrix. Slant Range Horizontal position-angle covariance. The result is in units of meters.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PsPs" type="xs:double" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Row 3 Column 3 term of the result matrix. Slant Range Slant Range position-position covariance. The result is in units of meters squared.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="LOS3D_KinematicsType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type describes the kinematics of a point with respect to a coordinate system defined by a 3D LOS unit vector. The coordinate system consists of the LOS and the 2D plane normal to that LOS. The 2D plane may be rotated (rolled) about the LOS unit vector. The Vertical and Horizontal axis of the 2D plane correspond to the X and Y axes of the unrotated frame.  The direction of the LOS unit vector is defined with respect to an enumerated reference frame. See individual elements for further details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RelativeAnglesLOS3D" type="uci:RelativeAnglesLOS3D_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Describes LOS, angular rates and associated errors of the entity from the reference position in terms of a 3D LOS unit vector and a 2D coordinate frame normal to the LOS vector.  The 3D LOS unit vector coordinate frame is defined with respect to one of several standard reference frames. The angular rates, angular errors, and angular rate errors are defined with respect to a 2D LOS coordinate frame, normal to the LOS, with an optional roll rotation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RelativeSlantRangeLOS3D" type="uci:RelativeSlantRangeLOS3D_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Contains relative slant range line of sight information.  See annotations of individual fields for further details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS3D_OrientationCovariance" type="uci:LOS3D_CovarianceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the 1-sigma covariance of line of sight angles and slant range. The covariance is symmetric and therefore the covariance values expressed here are the minimum set.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
