			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Validator" type="uci:ValidatorType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The ID of the Validator responsible for this Status message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ValidationState" type="uci:PlanValidationStateEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the determined validation state of the plan. The variables a given planning service, operator or system considers when validating a plan varies. Different variables might be considered for a single given System depending on the level of detail of the plan. The scope and meaning of "valid" comes from context of the services, operator and systems creating the plan. The value in this element can change during validation processing. The final validity determination is given when the sibling ValidationStatus element reaches an end state.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CompletionStatus" type="uci:CompletionStatusType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates actual and predicted metrics for completion of the validation process.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="IncompleteItem" type="uci:IncompleteProcessingType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates items associated with command processing that failed to complete. For example if a particular required Capability wasn't available, it could be referenced here. This element/type is highly flexible. It can reference almost any message/concept in UCI as a planning issue. The wide range of messages/concepts it can reference makes it 1) difficult to use as a basis for software/services to resolve the issue but 2) an excellent general method for processing algorithms to convey issues to operators. This element shouldn't be used to report processing issues/results that are more directly indicated by other processing status elements. For example, for planning processing, other elements focus specifically on per-Requirement allocation results therefore this element shouldn't be used for per-Requirement allocation results.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="InvalidActivity" type="uci:InvalidActivityType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>If the ActivityPlan is invalid, indicates which actions are invalid.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ActivityPlanningResult" type="uci:MA_RequirementPlanningResultType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates planning results per Requirement. This element is only expected when the OrbitActivityPlan was found to be invalid and the associated OrbitActivityPlanValidationCommand indicated "modify to validate" was allowed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ModifiedPlanID" type="uci:OrbitActivityPlanID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a modified, valid version of the evaluated plan. This element should only be given when the evaluated plan was found to be invalid, the evaluation command indicated modification was allowed, and the modifications resulted in a valid plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
