						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="AllProductsAndMessagesProduced" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Flag indicating whether or not all products (e.g. ProductLocation) and messages (e.g. Entity) expected to be produced by this activity have been output.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Metrics" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceActivityDetailType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates performance metrics that provide the system insight regarding the progress of an activity, description of the results of an activity and related stats. This is a list to provide reporting for all revisits between activity messages.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="Traceability" type="uci:TraceabilityType" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>C2 sources work at different levels of detail and abstraction, progressing from what to do to how specifically to do it.  Inputs from abstract tasking sources and Tasks can be decomposed into discrete, actionable Tasks and/or Capability commands.  This element provides traceability to the external tasking or Task from which this Capability command was derived.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="OrbitalSurveillanceCapabilityMDT" uci:version="002.001.001.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates that a subsystem is capable of performing an Orbital Surveillance activity. Orbital Surveillance involves the use of sensors of various different phenomenologies to search for and/or collect information on a target, multiple targets or region of space to meet a specific Information Objective.  The focus of an Orbital Surveillance activity (identified under the specification of Target) can be location based, object based, or zone based.

Examples of information objectives achieved through sensor collects include achieving or maintaining a certain level of orbit accuracy, verifying that there are no unknown objects in a specific region of space or with certain orbital parameters, determining the structural elements of a target down to a specified resolution, determining the current operational configuration of a target, or something as specific as providing a baseline characterization of a target in a specific phenomenology to be used for future change-detection.

This message advertises a subsystem's mostly static characteristics for a mission-ready Orbital Surveillance Capability to other systems, which enables proper commanding of the capability.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:CapabilityBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="Capability" type="uci:OrbitalSurveillanceCapabilityType" maxOccurs="unbounded">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates an installed Orbital Surveillance Capability.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="OrbitalSurveillanceCapabilityMT" uci:version="002.000.002.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This message indicates that a subsystem is capable of performing an Orbital Surveillance activity. Orbital Surveillance involves the use of sensors of various different phenomenologies to search for and/or collect information on a target, multiple targets or region of space to meet a specific Information Objective.  The focus of an Orbital Surveillance activity (identified under the specification of Target) can be location based, object based, or zone based.

Examples of information objectives achieved through sensor collects include achieving or maintaining a certain level of orbit accuracy, verifying that there are no unknown objects in a specific region of space or with certain orbital parameters, determining the structural elements of a target down to a specified resolution, determining the current operational configuration of a target, or something as specific as providing a baseline characterization of a target in a specific phenomenology to be used for future change-detection.

This message advertises a subsystem's mostly static characteristics for a mission-ready Orbital Surveillance Capability to other systems, which enables proper commanding of the capability.</xs:documentation>
