					<xs:documentation>Provides an audit trail for EW relationships that are exchanged as responses to requests.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelationshipThreatType" uci:version="002.001.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Describes the nature of the threat.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Posture" type="uci:ThreatPostureEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>How close a hostile unit is to engaging.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="WeaponType" type="uci:ThreatEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The general type of weapon used by a hostile unit.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="WeaponSensor" type="uci:ThreatWeaponEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The type of sensor on the weapon used by a hostile unit.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Endurance" type="uci:EnduranceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The strength of the hostile unit.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelationshipType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Describes the relationship between two identified objects.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="EngagementStatus" type="uci:ExternalCommandExecutionStateEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Reports an active engagement between a friendly entity and a hostile.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Pairing" type="uci:PairingRelationshipEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Reports a pairing, coupling, or association between two objects.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Threat" type="uci:RelationshipThreatType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Reports an active threat between a hostile entity and a friendly.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ElectronicWarfare" type="uci:RelationshipElectronicWarfareType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Reports a suspected association between two emitters.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ControllingUnit" type="uci:RelationshipControllingUnitType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Reports a control relationship between the Source and Destination. This is separate from the control status reported in a ControlStatus message. The distinction is required to communicate control of vehicles that aren't UCI Systems. This would occur if the vehicle is controlled by voice or a data link that is not being received.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeAngleRateUncertaintyLOS3D_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type contains the one-sigma angular rate uncertainty with respect to a 2D coordinate system normal to the LOS. If the 2D coordinate system rotates about the LOS at a given roll rate, care should be taken to account for the rotation of the reference frame.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="LOS_CrossVerticalAngleRateError" type="uci:AngleRateType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the one-sigma error in the angle rate perpendicular to the line-of-sight along the cross-vertical axis.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_CrossHorizontalAngleRateError" type="uci:AngleRateType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the one-sigma error in  the angle rate perpendicular to the line-of-sight along the cross-horizontal axis.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeAnglesLOS3D_Type" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This Type defines a LOS in terms of a 3D LOS unit vector. The LOS angular rates and associated angle and rate errors are provided in a 2D coordinate system normal to the LOS vector.  This coordinate system may be rotated about the LOS unit vector defined by a roll angle. The 3D LOS unit vector's orientation is defined with respect to one of several standard reference frames. Additional details may be found in the element annotations.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="LOS_ReferenceFrame" type="uci:LOS_ReferenceEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the base reference frame for the LOS_UnitVector data. Four reference frame options are enumerated: Body, NED, Inertial, and Capability. The Body frame is defined relative to the host platform, the NED frame is a local North, East, Down frame, the Inertial frame is similar to the NED frame with the addition of a Wander Angle, the Capability frame is defined with respect to the subsystem providing the associated Capability. Additional details can be found in the annotations for each enumeration value.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This indicates the Capability that defines the reference frame for the LOS unit vector when the Capability Enumeration is used in the LOS_ReferenceFrame element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_UnitVector" type="uci:UnitVectorType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Line of sight vector from the system to the Entity. The meaning of the X,Y,Z components of the LOS vector depends upon the enumeration value of the sibling LOS_ReferenceFrame element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_ReferenceFrameRoll" type="uci:AngleType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Roll angle of the LOS reference frame around the unit vector to the entity.  Positive angle results in a clockwise rotation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D" type="uci:RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The angular uncertainty of the entity with respect to the unit vector.  This uncertainty is expressed in a 2D coordinate frame normal to the LOS Unit Vector with cross-vertical and cross-horizontal axes.  For the special case where the entity is in the X-Y plane of the reference coordinate frame defined by sibling element LOS_ReferenceFrame and sibling element LOS_ReferenceFrameRoll is zero or not provided, the cross-vertical error is the error along the z-axis (elevation error if NED or inertial frame), and the cross-horizontal error is the error in the x-y plane (bearing error if NED or inertial frame).   For the general case, the cross-vertical and cross-horizontal axes are rotated from the X-Y plane of the reference frame to the plane of the unit vector around the y-axis of the reference frame (rotation by the elevation angle for the NED and inertial reference frames) - and then rotated clockwise around the LOS unit vector by sibling field LOS_ReferenceFrameRoll.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_ReferenceFrameRollRate" type="uci:AngleRateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Roll rate of the LOS reference frame around the unit vector to the entity.  Positive angle results in a clockwise roll rate.  Refer to sibling RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D for description of the cross-vertical and cross-horizontal axes with zero roll rate.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_CrossVerticalAngleRate" type="uci:AngleRateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Angle Rate perpendicular to the line-of-sight along the cross-vertical axis, adjusted by sibling LOS_ReferenceFrameRollRate if present.  Refer to sibling RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D for description of the cross-vertical axis with zero roll rate.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_CrossHorizontalAngleRate" type="uci:AngleRateType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Angle Rate perpendicular to the line-of-sight along the cross-horizontal axis, adjusted by sibling LOS_ReferenceFrameRollRate if present.  Refer to sibling RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D for description of the cross-horizontal axis with zero roll rate.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RelativeAngleRateUncertaintyLOS3D" type="uci:RelativeAngleRateUncertaintyLOS3D_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The one-sigma uncertainty in the angular rate (sibling elements LOS_CrossVerticalAngleRate and LOS_CrossHorizontalAngleRate) of the entity with respect to the 2D coordinate system normal to the 3D LOS unit vector, adjusted by the roll rate (sibling element LOS_ReferenceFrameRollRate). See sibling RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D for a detailed description of the coordinate system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D_Type" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type contains the 1-sigma relative angular uncertainty with respect a 2D coordinate frame normal to the LOS. This uncertainty is expressed with respect to cross-vertical and cross-horizontal axes aligned to the X,Y axes of the unrotated 2D reference frame.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="LOS_CrossVerticalAngleError" type="uci:AnglePositiveType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the one-sigma error along the cross-vertical axis.  Refer to parent RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D_Type for description of the cross-vertical axis.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_CrossHorizontalAngleError" type="uci:AnglePositiveType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the one-sigma error along the cross-horizontal axis.  Refer to parent RelativeAngleUncertaintyLOS3D_Type for description of the cross-horizontal axis.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeNavigationType" uci:version="001.001.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ReferencePoint" type="uci:Point3D_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Reference point from which the offsets are determined.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OffsetNorth" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Vehicle location in meters in the north direction relative to the reference point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OffsetEast" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Vehicle location in meters in the east direction relative to the reference point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OffsetDown" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Vehicle location in meters in the vertical direction relative to the reference point.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeOffset2D_Type" uci:version="001.002.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Rotation" type="uci:RotationEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates frame rotation. See annotations of individual enumerations for additional details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="XY_Offsets" type="uci:OffsetXY_Enum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates how the X and Y Offsets are to be interpreted. See annotations of individual enumerations for additional details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="X" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Offset in the X direction from the reference frame origin.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Y" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Offset in the Y direction from the reference frame origin.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Z" type="uci:Z_ChoiceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Offset in the Z direction from the reference frame origin.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeOffset3D_Type" uci:version="001.001.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Rotation" type="uci:RotationEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates frame rotation. See annotations of individual enumerations for additional details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="XY_Offsets" type="uci:OffsetXY_Enum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates how the X and Y Offsets are to be interpreted. See annotations of individual enumerations for additional details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="X" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Offset in the X direction from the reference frame origin.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Y" type="uci:DistanceOffsetType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Offset in the Y direction from the reference frame origin.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Z" type="uci:Z_ChoiceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Offset in the Z direction from the reference frame origin.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativePositionType" uci:version="002.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a relative position with respect to a point of reference.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ReferencePoint" type="uci:Point2D_ReferenceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element is the point of reference from which the relative azimuth, range and elevation are specified. Generally, services are encouraged to include altitude and/or time data whenever it is known.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS" type="uci:LineOfSightChoiceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element defines the optional LOS portion to the point of reference. If the LOS element is not included, then the RelativePosition must be defined as an offset to the origin of the ReferenceFrameID referenced by the ReferencePoint. If the LOS element is included, then the position is defined by a LOS from the sibling ReferencePoint. The LOS can be defined with respect to one of two choices: a Line of Sight (absolute frame) or a 3D LOS pointing vector (relative frame). See the associated annotations for details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RelativeSlantRangeLOS3D_Type" uci:version="001.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type contains kinematic information about a point along a LOS consisting of the Slant Range, Range Rate and associated 1-sigma uncertainties. See annotations of individual fields for further details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="SlantRange" type="uci:DistanceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the line-of-sight distance between two points.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SlantRangeError" type="uci:DistanceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the error in SlantRange.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SlantRangeRate" type="uci:SpeedType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the relative velocity along the line-of-sight.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SlantRangeRateError" type="uci:DoubleNonNegativeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Discrete value indicating the one sigma error in SlantRangeRate.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ReleaseConsentType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>ReleaseConsentType expresses that release is authorized and to express special aspects of the launch.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ReleaseConsent" type="xs:boolean">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the operator's authorization to release or jettison a store.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RepresentationKey" type="uci:ForeignKeyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the launch representation identifier for the associated release consent.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RemarksType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a remark such as one added by an operator.  Both short and longer detailed information can be provided.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="DisplayName" type="uci:VisibleString32Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a display name or short summary in human readable text.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Detail" type="uci:VisibleString1024Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates detailed information in human readable text.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RemoteDestinationType" uci:version="003.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RemoteSystemID" type="uci:SystemID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the known system id of the remote destination.  Used to obtain information about the destination from published data available in the environment.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RemoteEntityID" type="uci:EntityID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the known entity id of the remote destination.  Used to filter entities to obtain further information on the destination.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RemotePosition" type="uci:Point4D_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the known position of the remote destination (typically used for terrestrial, non-orbiting objects).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RemoteTLE" type="uci:TLE_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>If applicable, provides the known Two Line Element set coordinate system position data (typically used for Earth orbiting objects).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RemoveRF_ThreadInstanceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RF_ThreadInstanceIndex" type="xs:unsignedInt">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Identifier of the RF_ThreadInstance associated with the resource allocation. ResourceAllocator generates the index in a an incrementing fashion 1, 2, 3... at resource allocation time, assuring that identifiers are unique across resource allocations. RF_ThreadInstanceIndices may be re-used after an allocation has been deallocated.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionConstraintsType" uci:version="003.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the Time Window and the Repetition which can be Time-Based or Event-Based.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Repetition" type="uci:RepetitionType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the nature of the repetitions.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="TimeWindow" type="uci:TimeWindowType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The time window for which the sibling Repetition applies.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionContinuousType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a Continuous Repetition with an optional maximum interrupt duration.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="MaximumInterruptDuration" type="uci:DurationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the maximum acceptable duration of interrupts in output products during the "active" time for the Requirement.  Formerly known as MaxContiguousDropoutAllowed.   If omitted, maximum interrupt duration is left to the discretion of the recipient.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionEventBasedType" uci:version="003.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides a set of elements to describe an event-based repetition.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Event" type="uci:RepetitionEventType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the event upon which the repetitions should be based.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EventOffset" type="uci:EventOffsetChoiceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is optionally used to offset the location/time of the sibling EventWindow that is in reference to the Event. This Offset defines the center of the sibling EventWindow.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EventWindow" type="uci:EventWindowChoiceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The desired window around the specified Event combined with any specified EventOffset.  The value is intended as +/- around the specified Event as adjusted by any offset defined by the sibling EventOffset field.

For example: to specify a collection window of 100 seconds after Engine Cutoff, one would specify an EventOffset of +50 seconds and an EventWindow of 50 seconds.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AlternateReferenceObject" type="uci:ReferenceAssetKinematicsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>If this option is populated schedule the repetition times based on the Event of this object rather than the object in the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RepetitionAttempts" type="xs:unsignedInt">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the number of times the sibling Event element should trigger repetition of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionEventType" uci:version="002.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides a choice between event types to act as a trigger for an event-based repetition.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="PositionChange" type="uci:RepetitionPositionChangeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>PositionChange is a way to specify a time based on an object change in position.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RouteEvent" type="uci:RouteEventEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>RouteEvent is a way to specify a time based on when the system/asset reaches a specific event in the life cycle of the route.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OrbitalEvent" type="uci:OrbitalEventEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>OrbitalEvent is a way to specify a time based on when a space object reaches a specific point in orbit or in life cycle.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionFiniteType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a finite repetition type with a set number of repetition attempts and an optional interval.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RepetitionAttempts" type="xs:unsignedInt">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the number of times the Requirement should be repeated before being considered complete.  This element can be used, for example, to perform just a single scan of an area or to limit the number of attempts to acquire a cue.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RepetitionInterval" type="uci:DurationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the duration between repetitions of the Requirement.  If omitted the interval is left to the discretion of the recipient within other overall temporal constraints.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionPeriodicType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a periodic repetition with a specified interval.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RepetitionInterval" type="uci:DurationType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the duration between repetitions of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionPositionChangeType" uci:version="002.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides a choice of position delta types.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="LOS_BearingElevation" type="uci:LOS_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the change in LOS from commanded System to target, which triggers repetition of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LOS_AzEl" type="uci:LOS_InertialA_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the change in LOS from commanded System to target, which triggers repetition of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OrbitalRTN" type="uci:ThresholdOffOrbitTriggerDataType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the change in orbital RTN kinematics from the commanded System to target, which triggers repetition of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionTimeBasedType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Represents a Time-Based repetition.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="Continuous" type="uci:RepetitionContinuousType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement should be done continuously, within other temporal constraints of the Requirement. This element is oriented towards Capabilities that are continuous in nature and interruptible. This element generally shouldn't be used for Capabilities that are discrete and/or single shot in nature with a physics- and/or design-driven duration.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Finite" type="uci:RepetitionFiniteType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement should be repeated a specific number of times within the overall temporal constraints of the Requirement. This element is oriented towards capabilities that are discrete and/or single shot in nature.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Periodic" type="uci:RepetitionPeriodicType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement should be repeated periodically within the overall temporal constraints of the Requirement. Depending on perspective and/or Capability type, this element indicates the time duration between starts of successive repetitions of the Requirement, time between revisits to a target or area in a search volume or other meanings. A periodic command with no end time continues until canceled. This element is oriented towards Capabilities that are discrete and/or single shot in nature.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RepetitionType" uci:version="003.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Provides a choice between Time-Based and Event-Based Repetition.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="TimeBased" type="uci:RepetitionTimeBasedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates when the Requirement should be repeated on a Time basis.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EventBased" type="uci:RepetitionEventBasedType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Since it may be difficult to predict the exact time of an event, this element allows for the same input to be used in planning as the cycle moves from week-ahead planning to day-ahead planning.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ReplanReasonType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Trigger" type="uci:PlanningTriggerType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the trigger that initiated the replan.  Autonomous triggers are enabled/disabled via the PlanningFunction* message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AutonomousPlanningActionID" type="uci:AutonomousPlanningActionID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates autonomous planning action (referenced by UUID) that was triggered by the sibling Trigger.  The parameters of the autonomous planning actions are defined in the PlanningFunction* message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MissionContingencyAlertID" type="uci:MissionContingencyAlertID_Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of a MissionContingencyAlert associated with the replan.  The alert can provide additional context for the replan such as the ID of a failed Task, a LAR that couldn't be met, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="ReportToType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the persons/services/Systems/Units for which something is to be reported.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="AppliesTo" type="uci:AppliesToType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Provides detailed system, subsystem or service identifier information.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ContactDetails" type="uci:ContactDetailsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Provides detailed operator contact information.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Unit" type="uci:UnitIdentityType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Provides detailed unit information.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequestBaseType" abstract="true" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The base type for all *Request messages.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RequestID" type="uci:RequestID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of the request message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequestState" type="uci:RequestStateEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The state of the request message data.  Indicates if a request message is a NEW request, an UPDATE to a request, or a CANCEL of a request.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequestID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type for UCI IDs that correspond to command messages.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequestingFunctionID_ChoiceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type provides the correlation between an activity, a BIT, or a Calibration and a subsystem's RF_ResourceAllocationRequest.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="ActivityID" type="uci:ActivityID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Activity ID of the activity that is requesting resources.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="BIT_ID" type="uci:BIT_ID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>BIT ID of the internal subsystem test that requires resources.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CalibrationID" type="uci:CalibrationID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Calibration ID of the internal subsystem calibration that requires resources.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequestStatusBaseType" abstract="true" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The base type for all *RequestStatus messages.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RequestID" type="uci:RequestID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of the request message corresponding to this status message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequestProcessingState" type="uci:RequestProcessingStateEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the status of the request message.  Indicates how the receiver of the request has responded to the request message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequestProcessingStateReason" type="uci:CannotComplyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates details regarding the reason for and/or a contributing factor to the sibling RequestProcessingState element.  This element is only expected when the Request is in a potentially undesirable state such as rejected, canceled or accepted with less than optimum results expected.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationBaseType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Locked" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates whether the Requirement's allocation can be modified/removed in subsequent planning steps and/or replans.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ConstraintOverride" type="uci:RequirementConstraintsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates refined/overridden values of constraints for the Requirement.  Constraints can be refined/overridden in inputs (*PlanCommand) and/or outputs (*Plan) of planning processes.  Values given in this element refine/override those in the associated [Requirement] message.  Reasons for constraint overrides include refinement based on the allocated System, multi-Requirement coordination, etc.  Requirement constraints can be 1) given in a [Requirement] message, 2) overridden as a planning input in a [Requirement]PlanCommand message, 3) further refined/overridden during plan creation and reported in a [Requirement]Plan message to inform subsequent planning or execution.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="OtherOverride" type="uci:RequirementAllocationDetailsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates special/override values for miscellaneous [Requirement] message elements not included in the sibling ConstraintOverride element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationCommandType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ProposedEffect" type="uci:EffectAllocationType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a proposed Effect.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ProposedAction" type="uci:ActionAllocationType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a proposed Action.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ProposedTask" type="uci:TaskAllocationType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a proposed Task.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ProposedResponse" type="uci:ResponseAllocationType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a proposed Response.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationConstraintType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="SystemID" type="uci:SystemID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the SystemID of the System that is the target of the allocation constraint.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Identity" type="uci:IdentityType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a kind/type or instance of platform/system that is the target of the allocation constraint.  For example, this element could be used to allocate a Requirement to any fighter aircraft platform, or a specific kind of fighter aircraft, or a specific tail number of aircraft, or an aircraft with an aircrew member with a certain voice call sign.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityID" type="uci:CapabilityID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the CapabilityID of the Capability that is the target of the allocation constraint.  If multiple required Capabilities are given: 1) allocation to any one of the Capabilities is the minimum expectation, 2) decomposition of the Requirement into multiple child Requirements allocated across the multiple required Capabilities is acceptable, 3) there is no expectation that each Capability must perform the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationDetailsType" uci:version="001.000.001.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RequiredDMPI" type="uci:DMPI_AllocationType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a DMPI associated with the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequiredAO_Code" type="uci:AO_CodeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the AO/laser PRF and/or PIM code to be used with the Requirement.  This element is applicable for laser designation or laser guided weapon Requirements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationLockDetailsType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Lock" type="uci:RequirementsReferenceLockableType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specified Requirement(s) to lock.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Unlock" type="uci:RequirementsReferenceLockableType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specified Requirement(s) to unlock.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationParametersType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RequiredAllocation" type="uci:RequirementAllocationConstraintType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the system and optionally Capability that the Requirement (Effect, Action, Task, [Capability]Command, Response) MUST be allocated to during the allocation process.  The system can be an explicit UCI System or implied  UCI System (by Identity, hosted Capability, kinematics, etc.) in which case the associated UCI System must be determined as part of the allocation process.  If multiple child elements are given: 1) allocation to any one of the associated Systems is the minimum expectation, 2) decomposition of the Requirement into multiple child Requirements allocated across the associated Systems is acceptable, 3) there is no expectation that each associated System must perform the Requirement.  This element allows the Requirement creator to force an allocation to a specific system at the risk of 1) a less than optimal overall solution or 2) failure of the Requirement being allocated despite availability of another suitable system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PreferredAllocation" type="uci:RequirementAllocationConstraintType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the system and optionally Capability that the Requirement (Effect, Action, Task, [Capability]Command, Response) should be allocated to during the allocation process.  The system can be an explicit UCI System or implied  UCI System (by Identity, hosted Capability, kinematics, etc.) in which case the associated UCI System must be determined as part of the allocation process.  If multiple child elements are given: 1) allocation to any one of the associated Systems is the minimum expectation, 2) decomposition of the Requirement into multiple child Requirements allocated across the associated Systems is acceptable, 3) there is no expectation that each associated System must perform the Requirement.  This element allows the Requirement creator to suggest an allocation to a specific system at the risk of 1) a less than optimal overall solution or 2) failure of the Requirement being allocated despite availability of another suitable system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationWindowID_Type" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type for UCI IDs that correspond to Requirement allocation windows.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:ID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAllocationWindowType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="WindowID" type="uci:RequirementAllocationWindowID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the unique ID of the allocation window.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Begin" type="uci:DateTimeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the earliest time when execution of the Requirement could start for the window.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="End" type="uci:DateTimeType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the latest time when execution of the Requirement could end for the window.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Duration" type="uci:DurationRangeType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the estimated duration of time necessary to execute the Requirement, anywhere within the interval between the sibling Begin and End elements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementAssociationConstraintType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="AllOrNothing" type="uci:AssociatedRequirementsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a collection of Requirements which must all be planned and executed in order for any of them to be useful.  If all of the Requirements can't be planned and executed together then none should be.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EitherOr" type="uci:AssociatedRequirementsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a collection of Requirements in which only one should be planned and executed.  If any one of the Requirements in the collection can be planned and executed then the intent is satisfied.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="SameSystem" type="uci:AssociatedRequirementsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a collection of Requirements which must be allocated to the same System.  If all Requirements must be performed this should be used with an all-or-nothing association constraint.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementChoiceType" uci:version="001.000.002.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="ByType" type="uci:RequirementTaxonomyChoiceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a kind or type of Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ByInstance" type="uci:RequirementInstanceID_ChoiceType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a specific instance of a Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementConstraintsType" uci:version="003.002.003.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Rank" type="uci:ComparableRankingType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a constraint in the form of the Rank of the associated Requirement (Effect, Action, Task, [Capability]Command or Response).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="InterruptLowerRank" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates whether a) an in-work/planned item or items of a lower rank should be interrupted/replanned in order to address this item (TRUE) or b) this item should be simply queued/planned according to rank without interrupting in-work/planned items (FALSE).  For example, a Task being added to an existing TaskPlan (TaskPlanCommand constrained by an existing TaskPlan) would potentially cause unlocked Tasks of lower rank in the existing TaskPlan to be unallocated if 1) this element indicated TRUE and 2) the lower ranked Tasks prevented the higher ranked Task from being allocated.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Allocation" type="uci:RequirementAllocationParametersType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates allocation related constraints which can vary by Requirement at the discretion of the creator.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Timing" type="uci:RequirementTimingType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="4">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a temporal constraint on the Requirement. Allows for defining for each timing type (RequirementTimingEnum).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Kinematic" type="uci:RequirementKinematicConstraintsType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="4">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a constraint in the form of kinematic  position of Requirement actor or actors.  When more than one is given they are logically ANDed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Dependency" type="uci:RequirementDependencyType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a constraint in the form of a temporal dependency between Requirements; between Effect, Action, Task, [Capability]Command or Commands or Response.  If more than one is given, they are logically ANDed.

For example, a pre-strike image collection  (PO Task) might be required several minutes prior to a strike (Strike Task) to determine combat ID and target coordinates.  A post-strike image collection (PO Task) might be required several minutes after the strike to inform battle damage assessment.  The dependency between the three Tasks can be established with this element.  Assuming the Strike Task is the most difficult to plan/coordinate, the other two Tasks would likely each have dependencies on the Strike Task in order to orchestrate the desired "look-shoot-look" sequence.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="MaxProductDisseminationClassificationLevel" type="uci:DataProductClassificationLevelType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the maximum allowed classification level of the networks that the allocated system can disseminate products to for this task. The allocated system must have a dissemination route for data at this classification or lower.

For example, when trying to collect data to hand-off to an unclassified system or network, the allocated system has to be able to generate products at no higher than the specified level and have the authority to disseminate on a network at no higher than the specified classification level.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Analytic" type="uci:AnalyticConstraintsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a constraint in the form of an analytic measure that the Requirement must satisfy when planned/executed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AcceptableClassificationLevel" type="uci:SecurityInformationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a constraint on the highest acceptable classification level at which the Response can be planned and executed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CommsRequired" type="xs:boolean" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates whether or not communications are required when executing the Requirement.  In this context, "required" means the creator or a Requirement considers communications as necessary for C2 and/or Product dissemination related to this Requirement, independent of the type of vehicle that it is allocated to.  This element may result in consideration of communications allocations during Requirement allocation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AllowedRequirementTypes" type="uci:RequirementTaxonomyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates other types of Requirements that can be decomposed from, used to plan, used to implement, inherited from traced parents or otherwise associated with the subject Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ExcludedRequirementTypes" type="uci:RequirementTaxonomyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates other types of Requirements that cannot be decomposed from, used to plan, used to implement, inherited from traced parents or otherwise be associated with the subject Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ConstraintsNarrative" type="uci:VisibleString1024Type" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Free-text explanation of sibling constraints, additional extraordinary constraints, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="AllowedDomainPairing" type="uci:EnvironmentPairingEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="48">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an allowed domain/environment which the Requirement can be employed from and and allowed "target" domain/environment.  See enumerated type annotations for further details. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ExcludedDomainPairing" type="uci:EnvironmentPairingEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="48">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an excluded domain/environment which the Requirement can't be employed from an excluded "target" domain/environment.  See enumerated type annotations for further details. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementDependencyBaseType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="DependencyType" type="uci:RequirementDependencyEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates how the dependent item temporally relates to the sibling ReferenceRequirement.  The temporal relationship can further specified by sibling elements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="DependencyExtent" type="uci:OpConstraintTypeEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the extent to which the item is dependent on the ReferenceRequirement.  See enumerate annotations.  A SOFT dependency means the dependent item can/should finish or start even if some or all sibling dependency elements can't be satisfied.  A HARD dependency means the dependent item should only finish or start if all the sibling dependent elements can be satisfied.  For example, a dependent item can/should start even if it has a SOFT finish-to-start dependency on a ReferenceRequirement that can't be planned/started.  This element was adapted from the former "TaskRequired" element in the Task message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="EarliestTime" type="uci:DurationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the earliest time, relative to the finish or start of the ReferenceRequirement, to finish or start the dependent item.  If omitted, the dependent item's earliest finish or start should match the finish or start of the ReferenceRequirement.  For example, if the sibling DependencyType element indicates FINISH_TO_START then the earliest start time for the dependent item is the finish time of the ReferenceRequirement plus the  time duration given by this element.  Note that the duration given by this element could be negative; in the example above the dependent item's earliest start would be prior to the planned/predicted completion of the ReferenceRequirement by the duration given by this element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="LatestTime" type="uci:DurationType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the latest time, relative to the finish or start of the ReferenceRequirement, to finish or start the dependent item.  If omitted, the dependent item's latest finish or start should match the finish or start of the ReferenceRequirement.  For example, if the sibling DependencyType element indicates FINISH_TO_START then the latest start time for the dependent item is the finish time of the ReferenceRequirement plus the  time duration given by this element.  Note that the duration given by this element could be negative; in the example above the dependent item's latest start would be prior to the planned/predicted completion of the ReferenceRequirement by the duration given by this element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementDependencyType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:RequirementDependencyBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="ReferenceRequirement" type="uci:RequirementInstanceID_ChoiceType">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the dependent/reference Requirement.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementExecutionApprovalStatusBaseType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ExecutionApprovalState" type="uci:ApprovalStateEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The current execution approval state of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ApplicableMissionID" type="uci:MissionID_Type" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the set of Missions/MissionIDs the sibling elements apply to.  When statusing for a single Mission, the MissionID in the header should be used in lieu of this element.  This element and the MissionID in the message header should be omitted when reporting approval status for all Missions.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementExecutionPolicyType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="DefaultResponse" type="uci:DefaultResponseType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element indicates whether or not approval is required by default for Requirement execution.  The default can be overridden in specific conditions as indicated by the sibling ByRequirementPolicy element.  For example, an ApprovalPolicy could use this element to indicate that approval is required before executing Requirements by default and then use the sibling ByRequirementPolicy element to specify conditions when execution approval is not required such as for EA and Collection Tasks.  Alternately, this element could indicate that approval is not required by default and then use ByRequirementPolicy to specify conditions when is required, such as for Strike Tasks.  Creators of this message are expected to carefully avoid creating logically inconsistent policies and therefore no guidance is given on deconflicting illogical instances.  For example, an ApprovalPolicy that indicates approval is not required by default with conflicting ByRequirementPolicy overrides of 1) ApprovalRequired=TRUE for SAR Tasks and 2) ApprovalRequired=FALSE for SAR Tasks would be considered illogical.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ByRequirementPolicy" type="uci:ByRequirementPolicyType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates whether or not approval is required for a given Requirement type, overriding the default Requirement execution approval policy given in the sibling DefaultResponse element.  See the annotations in the sibling DefaultResponse element for an overview of how it relates to this element.  If multiple instances are given, each should be of a different Requirement type as indicated by the child Requirement element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementExecutionStatusDetailsType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ExecutingSystemID" type="uci:SystemID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the ID of the System executing the Requirement; the System whose status on execution of the Requirement is reported in sibling elements.  *Plans and Requirements must be managed such that only a single System executes a given Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ExecutionState" type="uci:RequirementExecutionStateEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Identifies the state of the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ExecutionStateReason" type="uci:CannotComplyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates details regarding the reason for and/or a contributing factor to the sibling State element.  This element is only expected when the State is in a potentially undesirable state such as failed or completed with less than optimum results.  It is strongly encouraged for transitions to failed or dropped.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ActualTiming" type="uci:RequirementTimingType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="4">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates actual timing status for the Requirement. Allows for defining for each timing type (RequirementTimingEnum).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="PercentCompleted" type="uci:PercentType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Percentage of assigned Requirement completion. The percentage increments until the Requirement has COMPLETED or FAILED. A percent complete of 0 indicates the Requirement has not yet begun execution, and a failed Requirement may have any percentage between 1 - 99.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Traceability" type="uci:RequirementStatusTraceabilityType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates traceability to the sources and implementation of the Requirement.  During the life cycle of execution of a Requirement, the traceability can change/update.  Traceability can vary depending on a given System's architecture.

For example, traceability to only a CommandID is reasonable if the architecture includes [Capability]Commands without *Plans or [Capability]Activities.  Both CommandID and ActivityID are reasonable for architectures where a single [Capability]Command could generate one or multiple [Capability]Activities, e.g. a SAR command could result in multiple activities to cover the area.  ActivityID only is reasonable for architectures where [Capability]Activities can originate automatically without *Plans or [Capability]Commands.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Metrics" type="uci:MetricsType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Specifies the actual metrics associated with the Requirements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementFailedTriggerType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:RequirementTriggerType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="DroppedTrigger" type="uci:MissionPlanningByCaseTriggerEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="24">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the trigger, prior to the Requirement failing, that caused the Requirement to be dropped or otherwise led to the failure. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementFilterType" uci:version="003.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RequirementType" type="uci:RequirementTaxonomyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a type of Requirement or Requirements that satisfies the filter. If omitted, any target satisfies the Requirement filter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequirementTarget" type="uci:AssetFilterType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates targets of the Requirement that are required to match the filter.  If omitted, any target satisfies the Requirement filter.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementGenerationDependencyType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:RequirementDependencyBaseType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="ReferenceRequirementIndex" type="xs:unsignedInt">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Identifies the RequirementIndex of the Requirement which the sibling elements are relative to.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementGuidanceType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Usage" type="uci:RequirementUsageEnum" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="3">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an intended form of Mission C2 for the Requirement.  In a System with multiple forms of Mission C2 and multiple operators, this element can be used to control which services, operators, etc. consider the Requirement as an input. List size for this element is based on "Select All That Apply" condition.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="TacticalPlanningAndExecution" type="uci:TacticalPlanningAndExecutionEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates resource allocation, timeliness and approval guidance constraints on the planning and execution of the Requirement by tactical nodes/Systems.  This guidance is generally given by operational C2 nodes to tactical C2 nodes per Requirement (per "tasking").  See enumerate annotations for further details.  The enumerates reflect well-established, short-hand terminology.  The guidance and enumerates functionally overlap with some UCI concepts such as Approval Policy but should be thought of as informing the creation of or tactical use of those other UCI concepts; the enumerates give up front guidance, prior to creation of Approval Policies, resource scheduling, timing constraints, etc.  This element can result in approval being required, in addition to what is required by the ApprovalPolicy of the System, but it cannot disable an approval required by an applicable ApprovalPolicy.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Failure" type="uci:FailureGuidanceEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates guidance on how to react to a failure to complete the Requirement.  For example, if a Strike Task failed and this element indicated RETRY then a tactical node would be expected to re-strike the target subject to other constraints.  See enumeration annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementInstanceID_ChoiceType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="EffectID" type="uci:EffectID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an instance of an Effect Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ActionID" type="uci:ActionID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an instance of an Action Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="TaskID" type="uci:TaskID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an instance of a Task Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CapabilityCommandID" type="uci:CommandID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an instance of a [Capability]Command Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="ResponseID" type="uci:ResponseID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates an instance of a Response Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementKinematicConstraintsType" uci:version="003.001.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="ApplicableObject" type="uci:RequirementObjectEnum">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates which Requirement actor the sibling kinematic constraints are applicable to.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Geo" type="uci:GeoFiltersQueryType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element gives a geographical filter to the query.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Orbital" type="uci:OrbitalFiltersQueryType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This element gives an orbital filter to the query.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementMetadataType" uci:version="002.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="Traceability" type="uci:TraceabilityType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates other Requirements (Effect, Action, Task, [Capability]Command or Response) or other sources of tasking the Requirement was derived from and/or relates to.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Source" type="uci:CreationSourceEnum" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the source of the Requirement (Effect, Action, Task, [Capability]Command or Response).  For example, this field allows indicating an operator created Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="Narrative" type="uci:RemarksType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates free-text, narrative explanation of the Requirement.  Both a short and longer detailed explanation can be provided.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementMetricsCategoryType" uci:version="001.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:choice>
			<xs:element name="CollectionMetrics" type="uci:CollectionTaskMetricsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The collection metrics from the execution of a mission plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="StrikeMetrics" type="uci:StrikeTaskMetricsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The strike metrics from the execution of a mission plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="CommRelayMetrics" type="uci:CommRelayTaskMetricsType">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The communication relay metrics from the execution of a mission plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:choice>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementMetricsID_Type" uci:version="001.001.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The ID type for UCI IDs that correspond to Requirement Metrics.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:VersionedID_Type" />
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementMetricsMDT" uci:version="002.001.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:sequence>
			<xs:element name="RequirementMetricsID" type="uci:RequirementMetricsID_Type">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a unique ID assigned to the RequirementMetrics.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequirementMetrics" type="uci:RequirementMetricsType" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates summary metrics for one or more Requirements of the sibling RequirementPlans.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequirementPlans" type="uci:PlansRequirementsReferenceType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates [Requirement]Plan whose metrics are given by sibling elements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
			<xs:element name="RequirementType" type="uci:RequirementTaxonomyType" minOccurs="0">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates which Requirement types included in the metrics analysis summarized in this message.  If omitted, all Requirement types in the analyzed plans were summarized.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:element>
		</xs:sequence>
	</xs:complexType>
	<xs:complexType name="RequirementMetricsMT" uci:version="002.000.003.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:complexContent>
			<xs:extension base="uci:MessageType">
				<xs:sequence>
					<xs:element name="ObjectState" type="uci:ObjectStateEnum" minOccurs="0">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>Indicates the state of the internal software object that the data in the message is based upon.  The state of the software object is determined by the publisher.  The publisher could be the original/master publisher or an intermediary re-publisher such as a persistence/data manager.  This element is not a commanded CRUD operation.  It is not an indication of the state of an instance of a message.  It is not a mechanism for partial, incremental, or fragmentary publication of a message.  It is a signal from a publisher to subscribers.  For REMOVED state, in particular, some subscribers will mirror the publisher and treat the message as if the object no longer exists, but others, such as mission recorders, might not (in order to support retaining historical records of activity, for example).</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
					<xs:element name="MessageData" type="uci:RequirementMetricsMDT">
						<xs:annotation>
							<xs:documentation>See annotations in child elements and messages/elements that use this type for details.</xs:documentation>
						</xs:annotation>
					</xs:element>
				</xs:sequence>
			</xs:extension>
		</xs:complexContent>
	</xs:complexType>
