				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ControlTypeEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This type establishes the high level forms of control allowed by UCI.   Systems have mission control.  Capabilities have primary, secondary and automated manager service control.  A Capability that supports primary/secondary/manager control accepts and adheres to the ControlSourcesCommand and ControlInterfacesCommand messages (which dictate which Systems/Services are approved sources of Capability C2 messages such as [Capability]Command and [Capability]SettingsCommand).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="MISSION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates mission control of a System.  A System can have only one mission controller at a time.  A System that supports UCI mission control can be tasked to perform a mission via the UCI Task, MissionPlanCommand and/or MissionPlan messages and their associated messages.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CAPABILITY_PRIMARY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates primary control of a Capability.  A Capability can have only one primary controller at a time.  A Capability's primary controller has all the power of a secondary controller and is exclusively responsible for handling ControlRequest for the Capability.  Additionally, the primary controller is exclusively responsible for issuing ControlSourcesCommand and ControlInterfacesCommand to the Capability; the Capability should only accept ControlSourcesCommand and ControlInterfacesCommand from the primary controller.  A primary controller is generally expected to have a human in the loop; secondary controllers shouldn't be a Capability Manager service (an automation service, also known as a Resource Manager).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CAPABILITY_SECONDARY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates secondary control of a Capability using Capability C2 messages such as [Capability]Command and [Capability]SettingsCommand).  A Capability can have multiple simultaneous secondary controllers.  A secondary controller is expected to have a human in the loop; secondary controllers shouldn't be a Capability Manager service (an automation service, also known as a Resource Manager).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CAPABILITY_MANAGER">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates control of a Capability using an automated Capability Manager service (also known as a Resource Manager) to issue Capability C2 messages such as [Capability]Command and [Capability]SettingsCommand).  A Capability can have multiple simultaneous Capability managers.  From the Subsystem/Capability perspective, a Capability manager and a secondary controller are equivalent.  The distinction between them exists to allow the primary controller to distinguish between human users and automation services.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="CoordinationRiskEnum" uci:version="000.001.000.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>The kind of risk to the execution of the coordinated Requirement set.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="DEPENDENT_REQUIREMENT_EARLY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Risk that a dependent Requirement may be executed too early.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DEPENDENT_REQUIREMENT_LATE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Risk that a dependent Requirement may be executed too late.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="REQUIRED_REQUIREMENT_CANCELED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Risk that a required Requirement is canceled.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="REQUIRED_REQUIREMENT_DROPPED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Risk that a required Requirement is dropped.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="REQUIRED_REQUIREMENT_FAILED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Risk that a required Requirement fails execution.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TIME_VIOLATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Risk that one or more Requirements are at risk of their execution time windows overlapping to the point that executing both would be impossible. This is especially likely if one or more Requirements are dependent on the timing of an external event.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="CorrelationIndicatorEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
