	<xs:simpleType name="LOS_MeasurementEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the reference for pointing of a Capability. For example, for an AMTI Capability this enum would indicate whether the AMTI scan volume is pointed relative to the System body or along a fixed inertial angle.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="BODY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates body frame.  The body coordinate frame axes is an orthogonal triad of axes X, Y, and Z with an origin fixed at the location determined to be optimal for the platform.  The x-axis is positive in the forward direction of the vehicle (e.g. aircraft nose).  The y-axis is positive to the right of the forward direction of the vehicle (e.g. aircraft right wing).  The z-axis is positive down through the vehicle.  The platform body coordinate system with respect to the NED coordinate system is described through the platform pitch, roll, and yaw Euler angles.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="NED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The NED coordinate system is a geographic frame with its origin at a defined point on the platform, generally the mounting point of an inertial system.  The NED coordinate frame is a North, East, Down frame: Down is normal to the WGS-84 reference ellipsoid, North points to the North pole, which is in the direction of the earth's inertial angular velocity projected onto the local horizontal plane, and East completes the orthogonal coordinate system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="INERTIAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The inertial coordinate system differs from the NED coordinate system by WanderAngle.  If WanderAngle is not provided or is zero, then the inertial coordinate frame is the NED coordinate frame.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CAPABILITY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The Capability reference frame is defined with respect to the subsystem (or component) that is providing the associated Capability. This allows the LOS to be defined in the natural coordinate system of the element providing the Capability. For example the rows and columns of the focal plane of an optical system providing a PO Capability could be used. Depending upon the use case, offsets and angles that define the orientation of this frame can be described by the InstallationDetails in the SubsystemConfiguration and ComponentConfiguration messages and/or LOS defined in associated Activity, Capability, Command messages. Care must be taken to avoid circular references since the LOS_ReferenceFrame enumerations are options in many of these messages.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ARRAY_FRAME">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The array reference frame is defined with respect to the boresight of the sensor.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
