			<xs:enumeration value="EXECUTING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The System begins execution of a Requirement,  For example, the System slews sensor and begins collection, begins to prepare weapons for release, starts jamming, etc.).  Once a Requirement transitions to this state, it can no longer be reallocated to another System, unless it transitions to the FAILED state.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="COMPLETED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Collection Requirements are considered complete when the resulting product is processed and disseminated.  All other Requirements are complete once the System transitions from the executing state after completing the Requirement as commanded/planned (vehicle releases weapon, stops jamming, etc.).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DROPPED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A Requirement has been dropped by a System and is subject for reallocation within the same System node before escalation to a higher node for reallocation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="FAILED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>The System node has determined that it cannot achieve the task.  The FAILED state escalates the issue for reallocation across Systems.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CANCELED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement has been canceled and will not be executed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RequirementObjectEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a Requirement-related object/actor.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="ALLOCATED_SYSTEM">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the object/System which the Requirement is allocated to.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TARGET_OBJECT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the object that is the target of the Requirement.  The term "target" is used in a general sense.  For some Requirement types, a more suitable term might be "subject", "point", "location", etc.  For example, the "target" of a SUPPORT Effect could be a blue/friendly System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SECONDARY_OBJECT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the secondary object for the Requirement.  Some Requirements are constrained by target object behavior on or relative to another battlespace object, the secondary object.  For example, an Electronic Attack Task can be a "three object/body problem"; the blue jammer is the ALLOCATED_SYSTEM, the red radar site is the TARGET_OBJECT and the blue protected asset is the SECONDARY_OBJECT.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ALL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates all objects related to a Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RequirementPlanningStateEnum" uci:version="000.001.000.001">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the planning state of a Requirement (Effect, Action, Task, etc.).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="UNALLOCATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A Requirement that is not allocated to any System.  The Requirement is either newly created, was not able to be allocated, or was previously allocated then DROPPED or FAILED during execution.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="PROPOSED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A Requirement that has been proposed to an allocation service and is not currently allocated to any other System.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ALLOCATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A Requirement that has been allocated to a System (or multiple Systems) in a *Plan but not yet planned in any *ActionPlan.  In this state, additional details of allocation results are expected in other planning status elements and associated *Plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="PLANNED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A Requirement that has been planned in an *ActionPlan (or multiple *ActionPlans).    In this state, additional details are expected in other planning status elements.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CANCELED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement has been canceled, and will not be allocated, planned, or executed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RequirementTimingEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates types of temporal/timing constraints for Requirements (Effect, Action, Task, etc.).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="PLANNING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a temporal constraint on completion of planning for the Requirement.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="EXECUTION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a temporal constraint on execution of the Requirement.  For Requirements whose execution and result are distinct, this enumerate indicates only a constraint on execution.  For example, a temporal constraint on execution of a Strike Task is a constraint on the release, launch, etc. of the weapon.  See sibling enumerate RESULT for additional details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="RESULT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a temporal constraint on the result of the Requirement.  For Requirements whose execution and result are distinct, this enumerate indicates only a constraint on the result.  For example, a temporal constraint on the result of a Strike Task is a constraint on the time of impact of the weapon.  See sibling enumerate EXECUTION for additional details.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DISSEMINATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a temporal constraint on the dissemination of data or Products of a Requirement.  For example, for a Requirement that produces an imagery Product, this enumerate indicates a constraint on the time when dissemination of the Product to consumers must be complete.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RequirementUsageEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates intended/allowed usage of a Requirement.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="PLANNED_C2">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement can be used as input for creating *Plans for Planned C2.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DIRECT_C2">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement can be used for unplanned, Direct C2.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="RESPONSE_C2">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Requirement can be used as a Response option for Response C2.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ResolutionMetersPerPixelType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates resolution in meters per pixel.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="uci:DoubleNonNegativeType" />
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ResourceAllocationStateEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the state of the resource allocation.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="NEW">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This enumeration is used when this is a new allocation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="UPDATE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This enumeration is used when this is an update to an existing allocation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DEALLOCATE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This enumeration is used when this is a deallocation to an existing allocation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="INFORMATIONAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This enumeration is used when the update is to the AccessPeriodExpectedPercentAccess element only.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ResourceAllocationStatusEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the state of the resource allocation.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="PENDING_ALLOCATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>When the RF_ResourceAllocationRequestStatus is initially sent with RequestStatus of ACCEPTED, the ResourceAllocationState may be set to "PENDING_ALLOCATION," as the allocation processing will not have completed, yet.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ALLOCATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Resources have been allocated but are not ready for use.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DEALLOCATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Resources are no longer allocated or available.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="AVAILABLE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Resources are available for use.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="REJECTED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates request has been rejected.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ResourceAllocatorCommandControlEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the combination of Mission Processing and Resource Allocator responsibility for resource selection.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="DIRECTED_MP_CONTROL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Mission Processing includes optional values, such as resource selections and jam technique.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="HYBRID_RA_CONTROL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Mission Processing may enter some optional values, such as resource selections and jam technique, and the Resource Allocator determines any missing values.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="HYBRID_OVERRIDE_ALLOWED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Resource Allocator may override optional fields entered by Mission Processing, such as resource selection and jam technique.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="NON_DIRECTED_CAPABILITY_CONTROL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Mission Processing does not enter resource selection, and Resource Allocator must make the selection.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ResponseTypeEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a type of Response for Response C2.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="REQUIREMENTS_TEMPLATE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Response that results in update or creation of Requirements based on a prescribed template.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="PLAN_ACTIVATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Response that results in activation of previously created *Plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ALERT_GENERATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Response that results in an alert.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DO_NOTHING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a Response that results in no actions being taken by the relevant System or Systems.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RF_ReportStateEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates whether an RF_Report is new, an update to an existing report or the report has been removed.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="NEW">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that the report is new.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="UPDATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that this report has been updated.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="REMOVED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that the report has been removed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RF_SignalContentEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Fundamental content of RF communications signal.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="VOICE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>VOICE - RF signal contains voice communications.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DATA">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>DATA - RF signal contains data content.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RF_TypeEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates Transmit (TX), Receive (RX), or Both (TX_RX).</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="RX">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Receive (RX) RF Type.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TX">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Transmit (TX) RF Type.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TX_RX">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates Both Transmit and Receive (TX_RX) RF Type.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RiskPrioritizationEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>An enumerated list of task prioritization ranking types.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="HIGH">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a task for which high amounts of risk are acceptable.  Typically associated with tasks that are marked with 'Precedence' in the ATO.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="MEDIUM">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a task for which medium amounts of risk are acceptable. Typically associated with tasks that are not marked with 'Precedence' in the ATO, but are at the next level of priority.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="LOW">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a task for which low amounts of risk are acceptable.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="NONE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a task for which no additional risk is acceptable, but which will be executed on a best-effort basis.  Typically, route changes are not made to support these tasks.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="ROME_IdentityType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the identity of the ROME ID, which is defined by ACTDF, where ROME is the acronym for Reconnaissance Operations Management Enterprise.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:maxLength value="10" />
			<xs:pattern value="[a-zA-Z0-9]+" />
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RotationEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates how the X and Y Offsets are to be interpreted. See annotations of individual enumerations for additional details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="UNROTATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>X (North) and Y (East) offsets wrt to a Mercator projection from the reference frame origin defined in the ReferenceFrame message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ROTATION_2D">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A 2D-rotated X-Y frame based upon the rotation about the Z-body axis (Yaw) defined in the corresponding Object message.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ROTATION_3D">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>X,Y,Z correspond to a rotated local NED coordinate system provided by the Orientation element.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="HEADING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A rotated X-Y frame based upon the rotation about the Z-body axis (Yaw) such that the x-axis is aligned to the North East component of the velocity vector.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="RouteEventEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
