			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="NOT_CALIBRATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the specific calibration completed successfully.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SubsystemEraseCommandEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the overall erase command status. See enumeration annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="ZEROIZE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a command to zeroize cryptographic keys and associated classified data. Requires organizational maintenance to reload keys. Depending on the chosen interpretation of the term "and associated classified data," ZEROIZE by itself (i.e., without a CDE) may either be interpreted to remove all classified data and return the Subsystem to its default classification level or remove only the portion of the classified data "associated" with the crypto data (and may not, in that case, return the Subsystem to its base classification level).  In this second interpretation, CDE should be combined with ZEROIZE. In either case, an organizational maintenance action will be required to enable the subsystem to return to normal operation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CLASSIFIED_DATA_ERASE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a command to remove all classified data created during Subsystem operation, returning the Subsystem to its default/native/base classification level. Depending on a subsystem's design, Classified Data Erase (CDE) can be commanded as a part of the Subsystem shutdown command (probably the normal use), or could be invoked during normal Subsystem operation. A Subsystem should reject a CDE command for situations it does not support. Restoring power to a Subsystem shutdown with CDE, restarts the Subsystem to normal operation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DISABLE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a command to invoke Subsystem-specific action requiring OEM or depot level maintenance to return the subsystem to normal operation.  DISABLE optionally may be interpreted to include the actions of ZEROIZE and CDE.  If it does not, it could be required to invoke CLASSIFIED_DATA_ERASE and/or ZEROIZE in addition to DISABLE in order to address all classified data.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CDE_AND_ZEROIZE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a command to perform both CDE and Zeroize processing.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SubsystemMaintenanceCommandEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates a subsystem maintenance command.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="SELECT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is used when the maintainer selects a test unit. This tells which unit a test is going to be tested.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DESELECT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is used when the maintainer deselects a test unit.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="EXECUTE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is used when the maintainer executes a test.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CONTINUE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Some tests will require visual inspection by the maintainer throughout the test. The maintainer will continue the test once visual inspection is complete.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="STOP">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>This is used when the maintainer is finished with a test and triggers SMNT to publish results. Note: This may only be needed for discrete tests if at all.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ABORT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Emergency only. test will need to stop testing and perform some cleanup.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SubsystemMaintenanceControlInterfacesEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>This enumerated type indicates the C2 messages and control flow patterns for Subsystem Maintenance Test.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="SUBSYSTEM_MAINTENANCE_COMMAND">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Subsystem supports initiated/manual maintenance test via the SubsystemMaintenaceCommand message.  Support of initiated maintenance test via this message implies the Subsystem can perform the maintenance test without changing the Subsystem state.  For example, if the Subsystem was in an OPERATE state, it can perform initiated/manual maintenance test interleaved with operational functions and capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SUBSYSTEM_STATE_COMMAND">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Subsystem supports initiated/manual BIT via the SubsystemStateCommand message.  Support of initiated BIT via this message implies the Subsystem will change the overall Subsystem state to MAINTENANCE while maintenance test is in progress.  For example, if the Subsystem was in an OPERATE state then commanded to perform maintenance test via the SubsystemStateCommand message, it will interrupt operational functions and capability while BIT is in progress.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SUBSYSTEM_INITIATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Subsystem supports self-initiated maintenance test.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SubsystemMaintenanceResultStatusEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the status of the subsystem maintenance tests.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="PASSED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Test passed, if every test event passed (i.e. no Abort or Failure occurred).</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="FAILED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Test failed if any event Failed but no Abort occurred.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ABORTED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Test aborted if any event Abort occurred.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="IN_PROGRESS">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Test in progress, there could be completed, in progress, or to be tested events.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="NOT_TESTED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>TEST not attempted, no test events have began testing.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SubsystemSettingEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates Capability and Service independent settings for Subsystems.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="LIVE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates using live inputs and outputs and with functional behaviors as expected in real missions.  This setting is generally expected to be mutually exclusive with the EXERCISE and SIMULATION settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="EXERCISE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates with exercise inputs and outputs as expected in exercise missions.  This setting is generally expected to be mutually exclusive with the LIVE and SIMULATION settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SIMULATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates with simulated inputs and outputs as expected in simulated missions.  This setting is generally expected to be mutually exclusive with the LIVE and EXERCISE settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TEST">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates with test inputs, outputs and functional behaviors.  This setting is generally expected to be additive to the LIVE, EXERCISE and/or SIMULATION settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TEST_INSTRUMENTATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates with test instrumentation outputs and functional behaviors.  This setting is generally expected to be additive to the TEST setting.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DATA_RECORDING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates with data recording outputs and functional behaviors.  This setting is independent of other Subsystem settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DEBUG_REPORTING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates with debug reporting outputs and functional behaviors.  This setting is independent of other Subsystem settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TRANSMIT_LEVEL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a setting in which the Subsystem operates at a specified power level.  This setting is independent of other Subsystem settings.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SubsystemStateEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the overall status/state of the Subsystem.  See enumeration annotations for further details.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="UNKNOWN">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is in an unknown state.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="NOT_INSTALLED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is not installed.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="OFF">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is in an OFF state as it was not powered up.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="PRE_INITIALIZATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is performing their bootstrap and initial OFP load.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="INITIALIZATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is performing its initialization and mission data load.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="STANDBY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem has completed its initialization and is ready to transition to the operation state.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="OPERATE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is allowed to perform its enabled capabilities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="OPERATE_RX_ONLY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is allowed to perform its enabled receive only capabilities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="OPERATE_TX_ONLY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is allowed to perform its enabled transmit only capabilities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="MAINTENANCE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is performing or has performed its maintenance procedure.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="CALIBRATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Subsystem state of calibration.  This form of calibration is initiated via SubsystemStateCommand and interrupts other functions.  Some Subsystems support a form of calibration that is initiated via SubsystemBIT_Command, doesn't require a Subsystem state change and doesn't interrupt other Subsystem functions.  See SubsystemCalibrationConfiguration for further details regarding the difference between types of commanded/initiated calibration.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="INITIATED_BIT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the Subsystem state of Initiated Built In Test (BIT).  This form of initiated BIT is initiated via SubsystemStateCommand and interrupts other functions.  Some Subsystems support a form of initiated BIT that is initiated via SubsystemBIT_Command, doesn't require a Subsystem state change and doesn't interrupt other Subsystem functions.  See SubsystemBIT_Configuration for further details regarding the difference between types of commanded/initiated BIT.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SHUTDOWN">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the subsystem is performing or has performed its shutdown procedure. Once complete, no messages will be sent by the subsystem.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DEGRADED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>A subsystem is considered degraded when a non-critical anomaly is encountered restricting the subsystem from performing the mission in its entirety but is still able to perform at least one use case within the mission.  The following is a non-exhaustive list of examples that could cause a subsystem to enter a DEGRADED state;
   - Redundant component failed but subsystem is still operational.
   - Decline in quality of service and/or functionality.
   - Mismatch of discretes.
   - Subset of components has failed however, functionality is still available.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SupportCapabilityTypeEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the type of support capability.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="DOOR">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the support capability is a door that must be manipulated in support of another capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SELF_DEFENSE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the support capability is a self-defense capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="STORE_MANAGEMENT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the support capability is a stores management capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TURRET">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the support capability is a turret that must be manipulated in support of another capability.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SupportedFunctionEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates whether a process can perform planning and/or validation functions.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="PLANNING">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that this process is able to perform planning. This could be exclusive of OR in addition to validation.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="VALIDATION">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates *PlanValidation messages as an interface.  A System that supports *PlanValidation messages can be invoked to validate an existing *Plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SurfaceTypeEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>An enumeration indicating the different types of surfaces or terrain.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="URBAN">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a built-up area with a moderate population density and infrastructure of built environment.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="RURAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a countryside or a geographic area that is located outside towns and cities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DESERT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a barren area of landscape where little precipitation occurs and, consequently, living conditions are hostile for plant and animal life.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="MOUNTAIN">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a mountainous region where the elevation changes are roughly 2000 feet or more.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="LITTORAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a coastal area where sea and land meet. May also be used for swamp like conditions.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="MARINE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a open water area such as sea or ocean. Can be used in conjunction with SeaState to further define active surface conditions.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SurveillanceIdentifierType" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Surveillance Identifier (modern address type): lockout interactions</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:int">
			<xs:minInclusive value="1" />
			<xs:maxInclusive value="63" />
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SwapEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the type of characteristic swap to be performed between two entities.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="SWAP_ALL_CHARACTERISTICS">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates all the entity information is to be swapped between two entities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SWAP_KINEMATICS_ONLY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that only kinematic information is to be swapped between the entities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="SWAP_NON_KINEMATICS_ONLY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that only non kinematic information (e.g. Identity) is to be swapped between the entities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SystemContingencyLevelEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Designates the contingency state of the System.  Contingencies can be an indication that the System will/has automatically modified its means of navigation.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="NORMAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates that the vehicle is operating normally.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="MISSION_CRITICAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a mission critical level of contingency. Possible causes include failure of one or more subsystems, mission avionics failure, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="FLIGHT_CRITICAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a flight critical level of contingency. Possible causes include critically low fuel state, excessive icing, problems with control surface actuation, physical damage, failure of flight critical avionics, etc.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="LOST_COMMS">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the vehicle has lost communication with its controlling system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SystemDeploymentEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the specific deployment capability of the System that can deploy another System.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="CARRY">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a capability to carry another system.  This differs from cargo in that the system once deployed has its own capabilities.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="RELEASE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a capability to release another system. A system in this sense has capabilities of its own once it is released.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="TOW">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a capability for one system to tow another system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="LAUNCH">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a capability to launch another system. This differs from RELEASE in that a type of "force" is applied.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="RETRACT">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a capability to retract an independent system under tow.  For example, a tethered space object may be pulled back in when its mission or objective is complete.  System deployment means the object being retracted was an independent system and not considered a part of the parent system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="RECOVER">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates a capability to recover another system.  This differs from retract in that the object that was once independent is reclaimed or retrieved.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SystemSourceEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Defines whether the associated data was generated from actual system data or an estimation service.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="ACTUAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the data is being reported by a system or its surrogate.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="ESTIMATED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the data is an estimate.  For example, if the system's actual data isn't available due to a comms outage then the data could be estimated based on the system's last known plan.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="OVERRIDE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>Indicates the data is a partial or complete override of values reported by the system.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="SystemStateEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Represents the operational state of the system with respect to active mission activities.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="OPERATIONAL">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>OPERATIONAL indicates the system is currently active, communicating, and functioning properly.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="DEGRADED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>DEGRADED indicates that there is some segment of system capability that is no longer functional but some ability to process mission tasks remains.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="FAILED">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>FAILED indicates that the system can no longer process any mission functions.  The system is either returning to base or has been lost.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
			<xs:enumeration value="INACTIVE">
				<xs:annotation>
					<xs:documentation>INACTIVE is used when a system is coming online but has not yet begun to communicate, when it has ended its mission and is no longer available to be tasked, or when this message is being used for maintenance and platform-logistics purposes outside of operations.</xs:documentation>
				</xs:annotation>
			</xs:enumeration>
		</xs:restriction>
	</xs:simpleType>
	<xs:simpleType name="TacticalOrderCapabilityEnum" uci:version="000.000.000.000">
		<xs:annotation>
			<xs:documentation>Indicates the types of tactical orders.</xs:documentation>
		</xs:annotation>
		<xs:restriction base="xs:string">
			<xs:enumeration value="AIR_TO_AIR_ROLE">
